      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>McDOUGALL'S Pateo SELF-RAISING _FLOUR liaE k . The Examiner, Thursday, April 12, 1906.</head>
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Pateo SELF-RAISING _FLOUR liaE k . The Examiner, Thursday, April 12, 1906.</p>
        <p>then he told her his fears. He had discovered that, though the provisions were almost exhausted, there was a keg of rum on board, and he believed that when their condition grew utterly hopeless the sailors maddened by despair would drink the fiery spirit until they were intoxicated, and in that case her position in the boat would be fraught with peril lyond all words. "I am unarmed," he told' her. "Or God knows, I would 'deal death to anyone who troubled you, but, as it is, I am powerless. Helen, my own, my darling, if worst comes to worst, will you leave the boat with me and trust to the mercy of tho • ~•aves rather than to tho.ie demons ?</p>
        <p>ii..-Aen," he went on tenaerty, - can pia understand our plight ? Is your head clear enough to take in what I tell you ? "</p>
        <p>" I think so-I feel desperately weak and my head aches badly, but I am quite in my right senses. I know that you and I were picked up by an Italian ship and that there are no English on board." " And no woman, save yourself. Helen, Captain Guida and everyone here believe 'that we are husband and wife."</p>
        <p>A faint pink colour dy,ad her cheek, but she did l not seem angry or annoyed, and Ronald went on :</p>
        <p>" You cannot have mistaken my feelings, Nell. From our first meeting on board the ' Cygnet' my whole heart went out to you ; long before the fire I kn w that the happiness of my life depended upon my winning you, and again and again I longed to tell you of my love and ask if you could give me any hope. Only one thing kept me silent: until I left the ship at Marseilles we must perforce continue fellow-passengers, meeting each other constantly, and if you had refused me the posi- tion would have been agony to me and in- tolerable for you. It seemed to me the only honourable course was to keep silent until we met in England, and then when you were safe under the protection of the Jardines I could ask my question fearlessly. But, clearest; things are changed now; the strangers around us believe that we are actually married. God knows that we should be so is the dearest wish of my heart, and there must be an English church with an English clergyman ' at Palermo who could perform the ceremony. Tell me, Nell, may I let the mistake go uncorrected and allow the captain to go on believing you are mine, and then, just as soon as the legal forms will permit, will you become indeed and in truth my wife? " She loved him passionately; the heart which had been deaf to poor Charley Gresham's impassioned pleading was absolutely and entirely Ronald's. Only one thing made her hesitate, would he forgive her her deception ? He still believed that she was Miss Jardine's paid companion and not the heiress herself, and sooner . or later he would have to learn the truth. Would his love survive the strain? She could not give kim up, she loved him so; after all, why should she dash the cup of happiness from her lips instead of drinking it ? If she married Ronald as Helen Jasper the marriage would be legal because he believed the name to be really her own : she</p>
        <p>Aid Helen Jardine whispered, : " I will go with you.l" For the girl whose heart Charles Gresham ha I never been abl-2. to touch loved Ronald Stuart with every fibre of her nature, loved him with an intensity which would have made her prefer certain death with ',him to the chance of life without him.</p>
        <p>No out guessed their purpose, for Ronald Stuart Went cm cheering the women and I-Men sang little nursery ballads to the child- ren just as though they had not decided to their companions in misfortune if the wiirA came.</p>
        <p>But things did not happen quite as Ronald. .had feared. It was true the ,sailors broached the keg of rum and partook so freely of its contents as to lose all power of pulling at the oars, but at the very moment when Mr. Stuart was about to signal to Helen that the time had come to forsake the boat, owing no doubt to the clumsy movements of the sailors it capsized, and all those on board were swept struggling into the waves. • 1:Ju..:1.1 Stuart was an expert swimmer. He caugla Helen in his arms and struck out bravely to escape from the scene of disaster, lest the crks of t!kir drowning comrades shouLl br:ak her heart. .</p>
        <p>wfiuld gladly have saved all tho3e help- ls and ch.:Urea bat he had not the po%ver, there was but a faint chance of life be him. encumbered by the weight of one burden. and to attempt more would have been cemia.death.</p>
        <p>" At leaAt we shall die together," he told but the thought was not quite enough to comfort him, for he could not help remem- lwring• haw happy they might have been : Xell wi. imi. lwart'i desire. with her for his hi; life would havboen perfect:</p>
        <p>Menaly with the English Consul at Palermo. I can introduce you to him, and of course he knows the ropes. "You will be a port of Providence to us both," said Ronald: "But, Edgar, there's another difficulty, my poor girl is desperately ill."</p>
        <p>) Captain Guida thougnt tile Lnglistiman the ' best of husbands, and being a kind-hearted man, bestirred himself to think of some rag ' of comfort for poor Stuart ; in his laboured French he told Mr. Stuart of an English -doctor settled only an easy drive from Palermo who was noted for his skill. "Mr. Halliburton is very rich," the sailor "That's not to be wondered at after all she said with a little air of envy he could not has gone through." suppress. "He does not need to work, but. But when Dr. Halliburton had seen Helen .ark you, sir, he came out to Sicily for the -he changed his mind: Instead of going back health of his wife, and for her sake as a thank- to consult his wife he had the patient wrapped offering for her restoration he never refuses in a _blanket and carried straight to his car- his i services to any fellow-countryman in riage. trouble." Ronald Stuart paid all claims the hotel Ronald Stuart, an intensely proud man, could make and then took his place opposite hated the very thought of accepting gratis Helen. It waa a very short drive and Janey medical . attendance for Helen, but he was Halliburton fully justified her husband's desperately eager to secure the best advice faith in her hospitality, for she received the for her, and the delicacy of the position in poor unconscious girl as though she had been which fate had placed them both made him a. sister or at least a dear friend of her own, eatch at the chance of getting an Englishman The best spare room was prepared, and in less to prescribe for her. than an hour Helen was in bed, watched over It Would have been torture to him to have by a white-capped Sister of Charity who had to explain matters to a foreigner, who, from been installed as her nurse. his imperfect French (of Italian he knew "Brain fever," said Dr. Halliburton, " and nothing) might take a very mistaken idea lam afraid it will go hard with her. My dear resp3cting Helen's relations with her corn- 1 fellow, don't look so heartbroken, the case is panion. I not hopeless, but I dare not deceive you, the Mr. Stuart was liberality itself when the poor girl is in danger." time came for leaving the " Yolande." He And then began a time Ronald Stuart never gave Captain Guida three bank notes for looked back upon without pain. For days distribution among the crew and pressed on and weeks his hopes, his thoughts, aye, even the officer's own acceptance a signet ring from th 3 well-spring of his life, seemed bound up his own finger •in which flashed a solitaire in that sick room. Helen was desperately ill, diamond of great value, her wanderings were piteous to hear, she "1 wonder I did not lose it in the sea," he rayed of the burning ship, of the cold cruel remarked to Helen when he told her what he water, then in what seemed , a more lucid had done. "I am thankful I did not for I interval she would beg forgiveness of some- could not have offered the captain money fot one she called Hildred, and entreat her not to saving our lives." betray her secret. , T.Ve i looked at him with a strange yearn- ' "-Is this Hildred her sister? " asked Dr, in dr.-ad in her beautiful eyes. Halliburton of poor Ronald. " Would it be 4 Will you always be glad he saved mine ? " possible to send -for her ? They are evidently she asked sadly. "Oh ! Ronald, will / your • deeply attached and the presence of one love last always ? " 1 known and loved might be for good. " Always through time and eternity," he 1 " Hildred Jardine was a passenger on board told her. "And now, my darling, try and the ' Cygnet. She was a great heiress, and my be hopeful. I am going to take yOu to an poor little-. girl only her humble corn- English doctor who will find some way of panion. but I never saw two women more making you better." . attached." . She shivered as one smitten with a sudden 1 "Then why not send for her ? " dread. " You . don't understand.; we left her " I feel icy cold. Ronald, and yet my brain on the • burning ship—left her to