      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Cruel Temptation.</head>
        <p>Cruel Temptation.</p>
        <p>FLORENCE HODSKINSON,</p>
        <p>AUTHOR OF 64 Tinsel and Gold," Sx., Ecc</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVIII: THE true Helen Jardine lived. and the story fo her perils and wonderful escape was romantic enough to have drawn tears, from any kind, womanly heart. When she found herself in the second boat which left the ill-fated " Cygnet " Helen was well-nigh distracted by terror. A gentle, ' winsome creature, she had never before been face to face with great danger; though she had made a brave fight with poverty, though since her father's death she had been prac- tically alone in the world and had had to earn her own living, yet she had never had any great perils to face, and so, in presence of this. sudden and unlooked-for disaster, she was almost stunned. Ronald Stuart loved her all the batter for her helplessness; there was something in her gentle, clinging nature which appealed in- stinctively to the strong manliness of his own. He was sitting next to her, and crowded as was the boat, .he managed to whisper a few words of comfort and consolation to her. • The boat was mainly filled by women and . children; there was bnly . one other male passenger besides Mr.. Stuart, and he was so far advanced in conumpti.,:m as to make it probable that in any case his days would be few ; it was the sight of his wife's agony at tlie I thought of being parted from him Avhich made Hildred yield her own place in the boat . to this poor invalid, Mr. Dawson. , From the moment they left the ship Ronal;.l Stuart recognised to the full the difficulties 01. his task ; he could not count on Mr. '.Dawson for help because the poor man was too ill to struggle, with rebellious •sailors ; of the latter four were foreigners, 'and, to make niatti.rs worse, the boat, though Irerricilly victualled</p>
        <p>before leaving the " C:ygnet," had not been able to spare room for many stores, since each few feet of space on her so occupied meant refusing a chance of life to one of the poor doomed passengers. As they pulled away from the " Cygnet " Ronald Stuart metally reviewed the silaa- tion. Four of the 'sailors who manned it were foreigners, Swedes and Danes ; they were tall, povierfully built' men, and if it came to a struggle he could never stand alone against them ; there was in the ship sufficient food to provide full rations for two days; if the stores were doltsl out frugally they might last three, but it would mean semi-hunger from the first, and since the men needed all their strength for, rowing it was hardly fair to deny them the food which was theirs. Mr. Stuart considered for a moment and then made up his mind, While the food lasted they would have three meals a, day, share and share alike ; he did not want an altercation with the sailors, yet he felt pretty sure one would come later on, but he prayed with all his heart they might sight a snip before things got to open warfare. , What his brave spirit was to those on board he himself in his simple, unaffected modesty • CHAPTER XXIX. never imagined. It was his cheerfulness CLEAR over the water floated that friendly which kept the poor women whose husbands hail, aml though the words were not English had been left on the " Cygnet " from blank nor (as far as he could make out) any language despair; he found time to tell fairy stories to Ronald Swart had ever heard, he was only the kiddies, he. tried his utmost to keep every- too thankful for, the sound of a human voice. one from dwelling too much on their danger And then he saw a small foreign-looking and affected to treat their present life as a craft at a little distance and knew that the sort of picnic where they must be prepared to tall figure on the bridge'must be her coin- rough it" with 'good humour, since the mander. inconveniences could not last long. A wild /cry for help went up from porn And through it all Helen Jardine—only he Ronald ; it' was not disregarded, the strange- did not know her by that name—was his first looking vessel *came a little nearer and then thought and his last. Through it all, even lowered a rope over the side ;.with hands that when he spoke most hopefully to the Others, treinb!ei Ronald fastened the rope round . he had made his. plan what to do the Ili:lea's waist and waited in a heartsick ' worst .came, agony of suspens4 till he saw .her drawn Before he left the " Cygnet " he had secured safely on board the " Yolanda." a couple of lifebelts for himself and the girl he His own turn soon followed. Poor fellow, loved; he was an expert swimmer, aud it worst when he felt his feet touch on the deck he came to Worst he would make a brave dash broi.e down and well-nigh wept, for life was for life. strangely sweet to him and death had seemed The first day passed off without difficulty, all too near. • only that the hearts of those on board waxed Nothint/ could hav‘ exceeded the kindness fainter within them as they saw no sign of a of their 'hosts ; the vessel was Italian, her passing ship. Ronald Stuart told the crew captain and crew the same, but the former he believed they had lost their bearings and could speak French after a fashiOn, and by were out of the track of the ocean lineis, but dint of rubbing up the half-forgotten lore of they grew insolent and declared they Were his school-days Ronald made him understand doing their best and could do no more ; if it that he and Nell had been shipwrecked • and came to that, they said, he had no authority that they would be thankful to be landed at over them, they could rid the boat of its load any port whence they could corinnunicate if they chose, and, freed from such a heavy with home. burden it would take far less exertion to row He had some English banknotes in a water- it; the food, too, would last linger if there proof bag of oilsilk,