        <p>might wait months or years isnore sne told him the truth ; it would not invalidate the</p>
        <p>Iv excitable state, made Air. Stuart change his plans ; instead 2f driving to Dr. Halli- button's he took rooms at the best hotel he could find in the vicinity of the English physician's house and wrote an urgent note to his fellow-countryman entreating him to come to see Helen.</p>
        <p>ceremony.</p>
        <p>Since from Ronald's story it seemed certain the true Hildred Jasper was dead, there was absolutely no fear of Helen's- secret being revealed. The unknown relations in England would suppose that she and her. Companion had perished on the burning ship. The same news would travel to Australia and reach Charles Gresham, and he would realise that his love v.-az; lost to him.</p>
        <p>. _ She wrs lying half-unconscious on a bed while Mr. Stuart in the adjoining sitting-room waited eagerly for Dr. Halliburton. - Those moments were among the mos; embarrassing of his life, for what if the doctor proved to be an old man with the rigid notions of conventitmality who would not understand the position ? . What if he cast any harsh doubts on poor Helen; how in the world could she be saved ?</p>
        <p>Yes. it was tile hest way ,out of the tangled web into which her deception had led her. She would marry Ronald and be happy. with hi.; beautiful home .4 Wilkm-tit-me and live thousand a year ; :hat would they want with Alanilale and its revenues ? Let her cousins keep it Rodaid," said the girl in her sweet mlNkical voice. "i love you, too--if you are sure, quite sure that you wilt never tire of me, let us be married—captain . Guida and the sailors can be left in their mistake and when We got to Palermo you and I will join our lives for ever."</p>
        <p>Ent lat.', which in many ways had been cruelly hard to Mr. Stuart, in this one thing favoured him. Dr. Hallibufton proved to be a man of his own age, and his first words removed all Ronald's fears :</p>
        <p>" Why, it's Ronnie Stuart ; do you mean you have forgotten me , and the old Charter- . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>hoase days ? It, must be a dozen years or siace we met, but I should have known you anywhere. You don't look as if you understood who I am ; I suppose the name of Halliburton 'puzzles you." " You can't be Edgar Bruce? "</p>
        <p>"God bless von, my darling." The girt •shivered slightly at his words. She was deceiving him cnielly ; her very marriage xvould be a fraud. Could God's blessing rest on an union that was founded on a lie ? Only she loved him so she could not bear to tell him tlie truth, and '4onald, trusting her entirely, suspected nothing. He kksed her once passionately on her. lips and then went on deck to answer the enquiries of the captain about 'his wile's" health. •</p>
        <p>„ ” But Liam. I came in for a fortune on condition I took the name of Halliburton, and as I really didn't mind the exchange and thought two thousand a year cheaply pur- chased at the price, I fulfilled the condition. My wife is the sweetest girl in the world, but she comes of a consumptive family and can't live hi England, So we, have pitched our tent !WV,. Ocoursc, you and Mrs. Stuart must have tlw luttel and be our guests, for the rest of yonr stay."</p>
        <p>Surely Helen's fraud was as great a sin as Hildred's ; greater perhaps, since she had not the same cruel temptation.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXX: • THINGS did not happen quite as might have been expected for after the first return to eim,eiolisness. llelen had one or two faintimf tits which fairly terriiied Mr. Stuart by thei.r length and the intense difficulty he had in restoring her to hie. lie kricw absolutely nothing of illness ; for years. he had 14veil apart front feminine companionship : his mother had been dead. for years, he had no sisters, his closest- friends were bad elors, and so, poor . fellow, he knew nothing )1. women's ailments. and he was so anxious about the girl he loved that for some time he , believed she had only escaped the fury of the sea to die of ex haustion. t</p>
        <p>And 'the? ltOnall told him,everything from IP.OTTICri t of his Mtroduction to Miss Jasper to their rescue by the captain of the ." Hlmrton, I'uh in an awful fix. God knows flekn awl I have transgressed no law, inorar'ott divine, and oor one desire is to be marritAl at once. but .she is desperately ill, anti I,now noLhing of the ri4les and forms which lave bc• gone through to make a marriage</p>
        <p>" And I • don't efflux." confessed Halli- burtim, "'but one thing is certain, Miss Jasper must not stay here.. I'll go home and tell ii oe, and then send my carriage for the yonng ha iv ; she may stay with us until we can arrange for the marriage. I am very</p>
