        <p>remedy ever known. , ..... , Beware of Substitutes; .47: for "OWBRIDGE." when buying Lung Tonic and TAKE NO OTHER.</p>
        <p>course ox Helen's illness, so the two men knew nothing of the rescue of "Miss Jardine" from the wreck of the burning "Cygnet." while as for Helen herself she was far too ill for weeks es en to wonder what had become of her friend:</p>
        <p>Mrs. Halfiburton had, of course, to be taken into the / secret that the invalid was Mr. Stuart's fiancée, not his wife, and her woman's wit devised a plan which would be far less embarrassing to Helen than the doctors idea of corifiding the whole story to the British Consul at Palermo.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Halliburton proposed that, as soon as Helen could travel, the invalid should go with herself and her husband by boat to Naples and thence by train to Rome, where she could stay with them in her maiden name. Dr. Halliburton knew an English clergyman then residing in Rome ; it Would be perfectly easy to tell him' that Miss Jasper was just recovering from a terrible illness and it was utterly impossible for her to return to England to be married, so • her fiancee was to join them in Rome and wished the ceremony to take place there as soon as possible after his arrival. Mr. Stuart had written to his bankers and received .an ample remittance ; to pretty little Mrs. Halliburton he entrusted the task of choosing a trousseau for Helen and he delignted the doctor and his wife by taking a furnished villa near their house for a year. " -%\"e can leave when it gets too hot," he said cheerully, "and I should like my darling's first married home' to be in the place where tzhe was given back to me, as it were, from the .grave, given back to life and love."</p>
        <p>They Were married. Though still very weak and fragile Helen _WaS perfectly restored as far as memory and are power of mental thought went, and she knew perfectly well that she was acting a lciceptive part by marrying Ronald as the poor companion, Helen Jasper, when she was really Miss Jardine, heiress of Alandale. . But she could not bring herself to tell her future husband the truth. Surely Ronald had given her sufficient proofs of love for her to have trusted to his devotion ; but no, she rethembered his rigid truth, his hatred ef all shams, and she would not run the risk of letting him know that she had deceived him. Besides, though naturally sweet-tempered, Helen's feelings towards her English relations had never been cordial, for she was devoted to her father and could not forget Ithat his letters to his people of piteous appeal for help had been left unanswered. To Helen it seemed only a fitting reward</p>
        <p>that her cousin, Lord Arandale, should be allowed to believe himself master of the property. only later on to have the inheritance swept away by the reappearance ot its rightful owner, Helen had no intention 01 foregoing her claim for ever; oh! no, but she meant to wait till she was Ronald's wife, till his love was secured dto her by the marriage tie before she risked her confession.</p>
        <p>S6l as Helen Jasper, she stood beside him at the altar, as Helen Jasper she signed het name after the ceremony, and not one of the few -spectators present dreamyl that the bride who looked so fascinatingly lovely her fragile beauty was really one of the riclitst heiresses in England. "My darling wife," cried Ronald, wlrn the 'moment they were alone together he took her into his arms and kissed her, " my little Nell." They spent their honeymoon in Floreneo, then they went home to the pretty little villa near Dr. Halliburton's and were as happy as two people could be. , _ _ _</p>
        <p>If at tunes Ronald still felt anxious at hi, wife's excessive delicacy, if now and again Helen remembered the secret she wae keepin from him, these were only just enough trouble to accentuate their happiness,. and the first real shadow across their lot was when at the end of .July Mr. .Stuart proposed that the' should go to England. " Halliburtoii thinks, Nell, you ought to winter in the south, and that, if we Visit our native land at all, it must be between now and the end of September. When shell we start?" " Not at all," said the girl-wife quickly, "Ronald, I hate the bare idea of going to England." " But, darling, I have duties and respon- sibilities there. I have an excellent agent, but in justice to my tenants I ought sometimes to visit Willowdene, .and besides, Lhave close friends 'and a few relations I do not Want to lose touch with."</p>
        <p>• "Then you can go to England for a month and, do your business and see your friends, I will actually ' spare 'you- a whole month, Ronald, but—l won't gci to Enghm.l." " Why not ? "he asked gently. "It is your native land, your own mother country."</p>
        <p>. .. " If so, it lias been a veritable step-mother. --England has done nothing for me nod I ncver *rant to see it" , ," You did not always think so."</p>
        <p>, "What do you mean ? - "On board the Cygnet ' you iintl poor Miss Jardine used to , talk 'cheerfully enough of all you meant to do in Enr,lan:l.; you (.id not hate the thought of going there then,"</p>
        <p>" I had not nearly hist my lietwlec on my way there," she 1-atm-lied. "..Poici.l, I tal.e the fire on the 'Cygnet ' the awful tinw that followed as a distinct warning nit to go to England.", Ronald let the subject •piss then, knew she_had vexed him ; it was the I;r,t. jar in their happiness. -</p>
        <p>And the reason she hated the thought a:- England was ! simply "that • that ea:mtr:,- *itained Alandale. Since her 1-11...-ci g• , Helen's views respecting • A land ale had changed for, from the ,lettcrs Ronald's rela- tions sent him in reply to. his annisuarxutent of their marriage. Helen •gathered that it would have been counted to her as a Vrem: honour if she had belonged to such a family as the Jardines of Alandale. Mr. Stuart's friends evidently thought he had made a _mesalliance ; .he !did nJt care a jot. but his wife did. Gradually the thought came!. to mind that 'sooner or later she must' tell her' husband the truth. Ronald's standard 'of duty tras very high ; she knew it would not allow him to remain very much longer away from England :without at least! a flying visi'c to WilloWdene, and if she let him go alone, would net 'his friends think he had chosen wife utterly beneath him, would they not think-lightly of his choice ? . " Hildred was right," the poor girl thought sadly, " she always warned me harm would come of my deception, but to me it seemed kuite innocent." She made- up her mind that very soon she would tell Ronald the truth, Wheii one day he suddenly flashed a piece of news upon her which was 'a veritable thunderbolt. " Helen,. I have something to ,tell you which .will make you intensely happy—Miss Jardine is-alive." "It's quite impossible," said Helen some- what 'resentfully; "• you always told -me she went clown with the Cygnet." "She is alive—an English newspaper was sent me yesterday, probably because it con- tained the notice of a friend's-marriage. I was turning it over just now .aimlessly enough, when I came upon a long paragraph headed, The Alandale Murder,'; and the name attrac- ted me because I remembered it was the name of Miss jardine's home." " But that doesn't prove she is alive." "Yes, it .does ; the man accused of the. murder is the Earl of Alandale, and the account specially mentions that Aliss Helen Jardine of Alandale was called as a witness. In another part of the same paper is an account of Miss Jardine's perils earlier in the year when she was rescued from the burning wreck of the Cygnet ' and taken on board a steam yacht. rhe paper hints that the murdered man was in Australian admirer of hers and that Lord Alandale shot him out of jealousy." . .</p>
        <p>, , But Mr. Stuart was speaking to ears that did not hear—Helen had fainted.</p>
        <p>' 'To be concluded.)</p>
