      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>UNPRECEDENTED SUCCESS</head>
        <p>UNPRECEDENTED SUCCESS</p>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY RATES REDUCED.—The annual meet- ing of the Great Sankey Parish Council was held in the Sankey Schools on Monday evening when there were present Messrs. G. Thompson, W. Furness, A. Birch, 11. Fairclough and J. Smith (clerk).—Mr. Furness moved the re-election of Mr. Thompson as chairman of the Council. Mr. Thompson took an intense interest in the welfare of the Parish and he thought it would be a mistake for them to make a change.—Mr. Fairclough seconded and the motion was approved.—Mr. W. Taylor was re-elected vice- chairman. The overseers, Messrs. Thompson and Fairclough, were re-appointed. The Vicar (Rev. .1. 11. Jones), was re-appointed representative on the District Local Education Sub-Committee and Messrs. Birch and Thompson were re-elected represen- tative managers of the Sankey Schools.—Mr. Thomp- son said he had some pleasant news for the ratepayers, as the overseers had been able to reduce the rates by 2d. in the .£ for the next half-year. Had it not been for a probable advance of lid. in the Education Rate the reduction would have been one of 4d. The overseers, however, had been warned of the probable advance, and therefore thought it better to go surely and • not to make such a reduction that it would be necessary to make an advance the next half-year. The rates for the coming half-year would be 2s. in the £. (Hear, hear.) —Mr. Birch expressed his pleasure at the reduction, especially as the tendency for the past few years had been to increase the rates until they had been intoler- able.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DOCTOR'S SATISFACTION.</head>
        <p>DOCTOR'S SATISFACTION.</p>
        <p>! Zam-Buk Cures Severe Case of</p>
        <p>Weeping Scalp-Eczema,</p>
        <p>- MI s. Ann Inston, of 7, Field Street, of! Langworthy Road, Seedley, Manchester,said to a "Weekly chronicle" reporter : " I fancy that the mischief was caused by her putting some other girl's hat on at school. The first sore patch was like a ringworm OD the child's temple, and T found another on the crown of her head a day or two later. A chemist sold me some ointment which seemed to make the sores spread. Several others appeared, and they : became full of matter. There was considerable discharge and the scalp was soon one mass of wet sores. The irritation and headaches prevented her going to school and her hair had to ho out off. " At last we took her to a doctor, and he told us the disease was weeping eczema. Poulticing did it no good, to be treated it with powders. It was of no use. She suffered just over six months, and I then told the doctor that we were going to try Zam-Buk. He expressed interest in the proposal, and said he would watch the result. "After -ising one box of i4am-13 tilt, the sores began to crumble off, and her head bees me cooler and easier. Under the other treatrnernts the scalp had appeared wet and sore, but under the Zam-Buk treatment the scabs crumbled off,„,:ind the sores healed, to reveal a new he: My skin 4mderneath. I kept rubbing Zam-Buk in until the sores altogether disappeared, and I can con- scientiously say that Zam-Buk alone has cured her. The doctor was quite satisfied, and said he should not hesi- tate to prescribe Zam-Buk in similar cases." Zam-Buk is daily earning the commendation of doctors and nutses, and it is more and more frequently becoming the official prescription for skin and scalp diseases, piles, pains, swellings, wounds, injuries, 4tc. Keep a box on a handy shelf.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SIX YEARS' INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>SIX YEARS' INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>A Collier Cured by Ele Beans. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Hobert Clententson, of 25, North roalpit Lane, Astley, near Manchester, has been cured of acute indi- gestion by Bile Be2ns. To a Manchester pressman, he said :</p>
        <p>"ram a collier, employed at one of the Astley pits, and till six years ago I was as strong and healthy as most men. Then I began to suffer from acute indiges- tion. The pains after eating were terrible. My stomach was distended with wind, and I had a choking sensation in the throat, which made me afraid to eat. I was off work for weeks through the complaint, and I was reduced to a terrible state of weakness. "Reading in a newspaper of the good results Bile Beans are achieving, I decided to try them. After the first box I felt relief, end I was able to eat _without having pain afterwards. Gradually I recovered my former health and vigour, till I was eventually quite cured. That was two years ago, and I have never had the slightest touch of indigestion since." Bile Beans are sold only in sealed boxes of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Manufactur- ing Co., 4, Red Cross-street, London, E.C., at Is. Id. or 2s. 9d, large family size (containing three times as- many beans as the Is. lid size).</p>
      </div>
