        <p>ing a crowded room, but now that landmark is removed la few anxious momenta is often in store for them at afternoon receptions, or " At Homes." Travelling Attire.—With Easter at band, travelling costumes have been securing much thought and atten- tion, alpaca being the fabric chosen at the moment, though later, linen will come to the front for this particular purpose. The coat and skirt style is the one preferred and with this is worn the white lingerie blouse, brown boots, and a small, very neat straw hat, simply trimmed with ribbon, and perhaps a quill. In no way has Fashion made greater, and more delightful strides than in travelling attire, and now-a-days we can hold our own very well, even against our long-admired, and frequently much envied Parisienne sisters. A New " Little Guide."—The next volume kla the series of "Little Guides," published by Messrs. Methuen and Co., will be the "East Riding of Yorkshire," by Mr. Joseph Ernest Morris, and it will no doubt be very welcome to all those who possess Mr. Morris's previous guide to the North Riding. There are many illustra- tions and a map. MAD GE.</p>
