      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SMART LONG COAT.</head>
        <p>SMART LONG COAT.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is a simple but particularly smart long coat of fawn cloth. It is Light in weight, though possessing a consider- able amount of warmth, and would make an ad- mirable Isummer travelling or driving coat, or a. serviceable wran to slip over a light frock on chilly days and eVenings. The coat fits closely hi the back and over the shoulders, but is semi- fitting in front, being cut with ample fulness in the lower part to avoid all danger of crushing a</p>
        <p>delicate skirt. Down the front, on either side of tho opening, the cloth is turned back to form a sort of facing, which facing develops on the shoulders into a shaped yoke. • Groups of hand- some buttons are arranged at intervals on this cloth facing, giving the effect of holding it down to the coat beneath. A touch of smartness is given to the coat by the small revers and collar of velvet in a darker tone of fawn. The sleeves are somewhat of the gigot type, and are finished at the wrists with buttoned cloth cuffs matching the turn-back fronts.</p>
        <p>2 -r----- ,Vt ~ \ i ;4 •et ..." 1,1 ~ \ C'4l,:: 'th ' \\ ' 1 t' " • ‘. t, /I II i . 4 ,\\ i ,i , 11.0 Pli?: ' i it. ! : ' I , 1 .3 q . t , , ~.. . ~</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SIIOES AND STOCKINGS.</head>
        <p>SIIOES AND STOCKINGS.</p>
        <p>The lace-fronted stockings which have so long been popular are being displaced by plain silk hose with self-embroidered clocks, says the Tatler. Oceasionally the latter are flanked by supplementary lines of open-work stitching on either side, and in some examples the clocks are indicated by minute punctures. The general effect sought is of punctilious neatness. Coloured kot-wear is more fashionable than it has been for many seasons, and a strong effort is being made by smart chausseurs to encourage public, taste in this direction. Prune, purple, green, and sealing-wax red shoes are some of the temptations offered. They trust be exactly matched by the stockings, and when graced by a pretty foot are decidedly good style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SIMPLE COAT AND SKIRT.</head>
        <p>SIMPLE COAT AND SKIRT.</p>
        <p>The coat and skirt sliewn in our illustration is an simple that it can be a.t-ter,ted by any care- ful home worker. The only difficulty will be the set of the corselet skirt, and this can be got over by permanently mounting the skirt on a pair of Itell-shaped but not necessarily expensive col, 1 sets, the fastenings being effected behind where the corset laces. When this is • done, and the</p>
        <p>skirt has been well shrunk atct pressed, a good fit will be ensured. White serge is the material chosen for this dainty spring costume, and the akirt is made with a deep flo,unce tucked on to the upper skirt, and headed 'and . finished with narrow knife-pleated silk frill. The little coat is quite plain and easy to make, the lace collar and cuffs being bought ready-made, and just stitched into place and edged with tiny frills to match those on skirt. AMERICAN WOMEN.</p>
        <p>The most remarkable social phenomenon of the New World, writes Mrs. Craigic in an American journal, is the position of women. While few take an interest in political affairs, while they seldom, if ever, show the least interest in the business or professional interests of their male relatives, they. show an extraordinary activity in their club life, in organisations of every sort and description, philanthropic, social, and educa- tional, which affect, of course, tho entire Ameri- can race.</p>
