      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>To-DAY'9 RECIPE.</head>
        <p>To-DAY'9 RECIPE.</p>
        <p>MACARONI A LA NAPOLITAINEo—TO/0 a piece of gravy-beef, cut in small pieces, put it into a stewpan with • a sliec.l cnion and a piece of butter; toss it on the fire till the onion and the pieces of meat are browned, then add a glass of white wine, a btmch of sweet herbs, a sliced carrot, pepper and salt to taste, a few tomatoes and mushrooms cut up, and a good allowance either of stock or tomato sauce. Let the whole simmer for a couple /of hours, and then skim off the fat and strain. Put the boiled macaroni into a nancenan with a niece of butter. nlenti of grated Par nesan or (!heddar cheese, and as much of the gravy as it will absorb; toss it on the fire a little, and then serve. Spag,hetti meg be used instead of macaroni. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC "BREATH IMAGES."</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC "BREATH IMAGES."</p>
        <p>When the glass plate of a photographic negro, five is spraiged free from film and cleaned nhemically:- nothing remains visible, but on breathing on the glass the photographic image may be brought out again as a. faint grey and white positive. It is supposed ' that the latent image—the undeveloped photograph—is an electro-chemical formation of molecular nuclxi, around which the visible ' image is subsequently built up by development.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>NOVEL USE OF COMPItESSED AIR.</head>
        <p>NOVEL USE OF COMPItESSED AIR.</p>
        <p>A novel use of compressed air is made by rail- way companies in the ..'•.'critherik States of America. When the loads of cotton are being taken to the coast there is always danger of their becoming damaged through. sparks from the loco- motives. To prevent this, the locomotive boilers art' filled with compressed air. A trainload of severmi thousand bales of cotton can be hauled by these locomotives at a rate of twelve miles an hour, although no fire is used in working them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EARTH WARMER.</head>
        <p>THE EARTH WARMER.</p>
        <p>That the earth is growing temporarily 'warmer is shewn by the mountain glaciers. These are made by varying temperature and moisture to increase and diminish in size during periods of years that may be found to be more or less regular cycles, and a period of quite general de- crease began about forty-five years ago. This has continued, with !piny local interruptions, as in the case cf Glacier _Blanc, which advanced from 1869 to 1896. Observations of ninety glaciers in the Swiss Alps, and many others in the French and Italian Alps, in Norway, Greenland, the Caucasus. the Pamir, the North-West United States, Western Canada and Africa, show that practically all are growing smaller. In the Savoy Alps and the Pyrenees small glaciers have quite disappeared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BRAIN AND THE INTELLECT.</head>
        <p>THE BRAIN AND THE INTELLECT.</p>
        <p>Investigations go to strengthen the belief that we do our best thinking with the back of the brain, and that that portion of the organ is en- , Ctled to be regarded as the seat of intellect. The left half of the brain is also looked upon as pre-eminently the intellectual half by some authorities. Dr. Phelps, a student of the sub- ject. notes that the right half is capable of ens- taming severe injury without shewing marked ill-effects. Cases of this kind have often puzzled physiologists, but the mystery disappears if we admit that the left half of the organ does most of our thinking- Some authorities are inclined to think that the halves of the brain are in a measure separate, so that every man may be said to have two brains. But none of these con- clusions is yet definitely accepted by all scientific) men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HUMAN MOTIVE FORCE.</head>
        <p>HUMAN MOTIVE FORCE.</p>
        <p>According to the researches of Fischer, the latent calorific energy., stored in the feed ob. sorbed by an adult man a day is 3,000 to 3,500 calories Of heat. A notable part of this energy is used within the body for determining animal activity, respiration, digestion, elimination, &amp;c. The excess may be expended in mechanical work. A day of eight hours and average and continuous work is equivalent to a work of 127,000 kilogramme-meters, or 300 calories, or a little less than one-half horse-power. Under these conditions the cost price of 100 horse-power may be thus calculated: Man, 250 workmen at 3 francs per day, 750 francs; horse-power, 10 horse power, all expenses included, 60 francs engine, steam, 6 francs; engine, gas, 3.50 francs. Human motive force is, therefore, 100 times dearer than mechanical motive force.</p>
        <p>TO DETECT ANILINE OR SALICYLIC ACID. A method for detecting the -presence of aniline or salicylic acid in foods has been developed by C. Lawal, says the Stientific American. Pieces of wool are first prepared,, from which the oily mat- ter has been well removed by boiling in a soda solution and washing until all the alkali has dis- appeared. The substance to be analysed is di- luted with water and filtered. We take 100 c. c. of the filtered liquid, adding 4 c. c. of hydro- chloric acid and put in a wool strip. The wool is then washed in cold water, then boiled in slightly acidulated water. In the presence of aniline colours, the wool becomes coloured, and the colour is soluble in ammonia. It reappears upon acidulation, while the vegetable colours turn to red, green, or yellow in contact with ammonia. To detect salicylic acid, we treat the substance with water and sulphuric acid, taking up the liquid with ether. The latter is then evaporated on a watch glass, and the residue is treated with ferric :chloride. A violet coloration indicates salicylic acid. A flesh-coloured precipi- tate shows benzoic acid. Should tannin be also present, it must be first precipitated by means of sub-acetate of lead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NEW BALLOON "POWER."</head>
        <p>NEW BALLOON "POWER."</p>
        <p>France. has the honour of recording an impor- tant invention in connection with aeronautics. Hitherto practiaal difficulties in connection with the charging of balloons has militated greatly againSt their use by ern jos in the field. The French Army poisesses 300 specially constructed vehicles for transporting hydrogen for airship.s. Each of these vehicles has to be drawn by six horses, and to be, returned to the depot when once unloaded. M. G. F. Jaubert claims to have discovered a power of evolving hydrogen from water which does away with all this. This is done by the action Oil'water of hydride of cal- emm, or, as he terms it. "hydrolith," of which one kilogramm.?, yields 1,250 litres of pure hydro. gen. It thus appears that with 500 kilogrammes (Newt.) of hydride of calcium, Movable Luny- . where and Altable anywhere with water, the French Army 1 aeronauts could do_ all that is (lone with their 300 hydrogen carts, weighing three and a•half tens eech when loaded. The French Army has at present three types rf balloons, the colonial ‘balloon, of 350 cubic metres cal.acity, the campaign balloon of ' 500 cubic metres capacity, and the siege balloon of 000 cubic metres capacity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SUN-SPOTS AND VEGETATION.</head>
        <p>SUN-SPOTS AND VEGETATION.</p>
        <p>- - Tho Frbnch Astronomer and naturalist, M. Flammarion, has succeeded, so he says, in estab- lishing a relation between horse-chestnuts and sun-Spots. He finds that the leaf-buds burst and the flowers appear on the horse-chestnut trees partici when the spots on the sun are largest. Hence, he is trying incidentally to find out which of the sun's rays really rio the work of growing fruits and vegetables. To this end, says an American paper, he built a number of small greenhouses—one of tha ordinary kind, Another covered with red glass, a . further one of blue glass, one of green glass, and so on, represent- ing the various hues of the solar spectrum. In each of these greenhouses he sowed fifty lettuce. plants, and as a result he found that those which grew in the red light spindled up into the air, whiic.thosc in-the blue light and green light de- veloped hardly at all. It was much the same wa3 with other vegetables, the potatoes planted in the red house running to stalks and leaves, while the tubers obtained were hardly bigger than peas. It was ascertained, however, that ripe strawberries and other fruits could be kept in good condition for -many. days, and held back from over-ripening, by placing them under blue INDIAN OCEAN EXPLORATION.</p>
        <p>Some highly - scientific results have been achieved by the Sladen Trust Expedition to the Indian Ocean for the exploration of those waters. One important point which was ascertained, ac- cording to Mr. Stanley Gardiner, M.A., Lec- turer in Zoology and Fellow of Caius College of Cambridge University, who was in charge of the party, was the extension outward of all the reefs, on their own remains or debris, in much the same way as a moraine is formed at the base of a: glacier. These masses of rock were found to be thickly covered by various growths and marine animals. Huge stems of black coral (the rarest variety) extending to seven feet in length were secured, though white coral was found to be the principal constituent of the reefs. Numerous quantities of deep-sea fish were secured. The greater proportion of these are believed to be absolutely new and HITHERTO nnolowm SPECIMENS. Some were possessed of enormous eyes, others had only rudimentary ones scarcely larger than a pinhead, while many were quite,blind. It was also ascertained that there is an abundance of life existing at a depth of 1,200 'fathoms in waters 2,500 fathoms deep. This floating life comprises the food of whales and other deep-sea fish, and this discovery is of great scientific interest, since it has hitherto been believed to thrive only near the surface. The expedition secured a large num- ber of huge squids of great variety,' jolly-fish, and prawns, some of which were six inches in length. Curiously enough, while some of the latter were quite blind, others had eyes of large size. This deficiency in the former was compen- sated for by lone delicate antennw. which in some CMS extended to twice the length of the body. Nearly all, however, possessed phosphor- escent organs, due to the great depth. at wkale lins live.</p>
        <p>..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. R. Dobson. The will of the late Mr. itolyirt Dobson, of Si- anley- street has been proved at ;i2.36,42.6.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crostield, M.P.</p>
        <p>At the Spring meeting,of the Golf Club, at Cannes, last week, a putting match, after a spirited contest, was won by Mrs. Fredelick Walker, of Villa Mezzo Monte, with Cluntess Torby second. The prizes were presented by the Grand Duke Vladimir and Mr. Anthony Drezcl. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, and Mr. F. E. Fenner were the winners of the men's prizes, given by the same members. Death of Mr. A. W. Winstantley. . .</p>
        <p>The death took place at his reside;ce, IG. Arthur- sheet, on Tuesday morning, at eight o'clock, of Mr. Ambrose Walter Winstanley, eldest son of Mr. George Winstanley, of St. Helens. The deceased, who was 139 years of age, had been a suffarcr from asthma. 'lle was a traveller for the Vacuum Oil Company, London. He was a Conservative in politics and worshipped at the Wesleyan Church in Bewsey-road. He leaves a widow and five childien. PREMIER Cvcr.Es are bard to beat, 7,8, 10, 12 and 14 guineas. Cash or twelve monthly payments. Sole Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge St., War- rington.—Advt. Gifts to the District Nursing Association. The Superintendent of the District Nursing Associa- tion acknowledges, with many thanks, the following gifts : —Garments, Miss Dreber, Mrs. Crosfield, Mrs. Broadbent and Mrs. Fairclough; magazines, Mrs. Porritt, Miss Geddes, Mrs. Starkey and Miss Murray ; two glass dishes, Mrs. Fogg; crutches, Mrs. Williams; toys, Master Ivor Bernhardt; concert tickets' Mrs. Crosfield, Mik. Pearson, Warrington Male Voice Choir, and St. Mary's Dramatic Society; dripping, Miss Kiernan; (thicken broth, Miss Atkinson : flowers, ' Mrs. Crosfield, Mrs. Carleton, Mrs. Leigh, and the , Wojkingmen'sllission ; fruit, Mr. Simenck ; circular air cushion, Mrs Crowther. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCE•BRIDGIO-STREET.—Advt. Fatal Burns at Warrington. _</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse held awn inquest on Saturiav the Warrington Police Court on the body of Catherine Doherty, aged forty-nine, of 6, Dial-street, who died in the Infirmary on the previous Tuesday from severe burns. On Monday night the other members of the I household retired to bed leaving deceased, who was under the influence of drink. sittinA on a bucket in front of the fire. Shortly afterwards her nephew, named Tandy, who was in bed, heard screams, and on going downstairs found the deceased with her clothing ablaze. A man named Ellison extinguished the flames with an overcoat. A verdict of " dental death " was returned. Asx for CUNNINMIAM'S HIGFI-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—AcIvt. Oddfellowshlp. A paper on " National Service" was given by Bro. G. IL Crosfield at the meeting of the Prince of Wales Lndge of Oddfellows, held on Saturday, at which there were present: - fl. Maddock, N.G. ; T. P. Bradshaw, Prov. G.M. ; Frank Webster, C.S. ; F. G. Taylor, P.G. ; T. Wadsworth, P.G. ; J. Massey, P.P.G.M., and others.—Bro. Crosfield dealt with the impossibility of disarment and settlement by arbitration, and RS to the need for being prepared for war by the establishment of a strong navy, a small highly trained professional army for Imperial uses, and a National Militia raised primarily for home defence, but available to be called upon to voluntary swell the regular army if engaged in a serious war over the seas A discussion took lace in which the Bros. H. Maddock, J. Hall, ,T. Taylor, J. Whitham, F. G Taylor, and G. F. Freeman took part. Bro. Crosfield replied very fully to his critics.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Death from Laudanum Poisoning. At the Police Station, on Saturday, Mr. S. Brig- house, County Coroner, held an inquiry into the cir- cumstances attending the death of John Lloyd, forty- nine, mechanical engineer, 43, Legla-street, who died at the Warrington Infirmary on Friday from opium poisoning.—The evidence showed that deceased on Wednesday retired to bed in his usual health At seven o'clock neat morning his wife got up to attend Co her household duties, leaving him in bed in a deep sleep. Half an hour later she tried to rouse him, and toeing unable to do so sent for medical assistance, and deceased was removed to the Infirmary. In the pocket of his jacket, which was hanging on the bed rail, was found a small bottle containing laudanum Deceased, Mrs. Lloyd stated, was a sufferer from cramp and sleeplessness, and had been in the habit of taking the drug for the past fifteen years. He was rather low-spirited on account of being out of work, but had never threatened to take away his life.— In answer to the Coroner Mrs. Lloyd said her husband could take thirty drops of laudanum in one dose. —The Rev. W. Bracecamp, vicar of St. Paul's, said deceased had been out of work, and for some time had been practically starving. The laudanum would, there- fore, take a greater effect upon him.—The Coroner pointed out that there was no evidence to show that [deceased intended to commit suicide. The habit of 'taking laudanum was a very seductive one, and some who formed the habit could with impunity take as much laudanum as would kill an ordinary individual. If the deceased had not had much food his system r would not be able to stand the drug as well as it might otherwise do. A verdict of" Death from mis- adventure " was returned.—At the close of the inquest the Coroner handed Mrs. Lloyd 355. from the poor- box, and the jury also made a collection on her behalf. BABY CARRIAGES. New and Artistic designs.— J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, &amp; 55, Bridge st., Warriagton.—Advt. Ancient Order of Foresters, Court 430.</p>
        <p>_ The members of the above met at the Blue Lion I Hotel on Saturday, for their annual tea and social, when a large gathering did full justice to the excel- lent fare provided by the hosts Mr. Lake. The Chair- man, District Chief Ranger Peter. Peacock, in the course of his remarks stated in his opinion such gatherings as these were the best means of furthering the interests of the Order. Ile was proud to belong to such an honourable Order which boasted of nine hundred and twenty-four thousand members,and eight millions in funds —havire, had an increase of two hundred and sixty-four thousand pounds last year. P.D.C.R. Brother James in submitting the toast of the Warrington District pointed out the success attending the efforts of the members, and S.C.R. Bro. E. Clayton as the last District Delegate replied praising the members from Court 430 for the efficisnt manner they carried on the business. P.D.C.R. Bro. Hipkiss, from Court No. 138, gave the toast of Court 430, congratu- lating them on their excellent membership and financial standing, which was responded to by P.D.C.R. 4 Bro. Chrimes, Secretary, who Lave a brief history of this Couit's progress, from the year 1805 with a membership of 70, and funds £B6, until to-day. This Court had 362 members, funds £l4BO. During his secretaryship of ten years, the funds had increased by £4OO, and membership by 125 despite deaths and secessions of 123 members. During the past 41 years £6,447 has been paid by the] Coutt in sickness and deaths, also £lOO to distressed members. He thanked the members for their enthusiastic co-operation. An interesting diversion in the shape of presentations to Bros. P.C.R. Thomas Roden and P.C.R. William Payton whose services were eulogised by Trustees P.C.II.'s Bros. J. Gregory and W. R. Smith. The Immediate P.D.C.R. Bro. Hipkiss also gave second cash prize to Bro. Roden for bringing most new members, which was presented by P.D.C.R. Bro. Hankinson. The humorous part of the programme was ably given by Mr. Percy Hignett, and the other artistes, Bros. Poster, Tatlock, Jenna and Chattel, rendered songs to the appreciation of the members. Mr. Percy Hignett was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>- - J, R. Wow), Piano Dealer and Tuner, 47,, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>AMBULINGE WORK.</head>
        <p>AMBULINGE WORK.</p>
        <p>Prize Distribution at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Ambulance work has made gratifying progress in Warrington of late years and the efficiency of the local Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade is a tribute to the great interest which officers and men alike take in the movement. On Saturday evening there was an interesting gathering at the headquarters of the Corps —the Parade room at the Police Station—when the dis- tribution of prizes gaiusd during the year took place. Superintendent Baldry presided and the attendance included Miss Crosilell (who distributed the prizes), Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, Dr. Adams, First officer , Williams, Second Officer Hulse, and Third officer ' Taylor. Superintendent BALDRY announced that the Chief Superintendent (Captain Waring) was unable to be present, owing to another bereavement, that of his wife's mother. Ha was sure they would express to Captain , Waring their deepest sympathy. I Miss CROSFIELD said it gave her much pleasure to be present that evening, because in common with many others she admired the useful and admirable work which the ambulance risen did. It must be a great satisfaction to them to know that the work was useful, and of that there could be no question. She bad never been through a course of ambulance training, but when she was a girl a lady friend told her that she would not like to resuscitate an apparently drowned person. She was sure they would all feel for Captain Waring, and accord to him a hearty vote of sympathy in the second bereave- ment he had just sustained. (Hear, hear.) Superintendent BALDRY' said it was a singular coinci- dence that Miss Crosfield, who had spoke of resuscitat- ing an apparently drowned person, had to present the Royal Humane Society's certificate for the very identi- cal thing of which she had been speaking. Miss CROSPIELD then distributed the prises as follows: Medallions.—Privates W. H. JarTIPS, J. R. Woodcock, F. Betties, A. E. Spann, A. C. Smithson, and T. S. Gar- nett.</p>
        <p>Second Year Certificates.—Privates G. Brown, A, E. Price, J. Marrar, S. Le Four, and W. Roberts. Royal Humans Society's Cartiticate.—Private Oswald Buckley. Greatest possible number of attendances at drill.— Sergeants W. A. Banning. W. Newns, and W. D. Fell ; and Privates NV. Dimelow, J. H. Sayer, B. Green, A. Sinclair, W. H. James, A. E. Spann, H. Gilbert, J Smith, W. Clements, and S. Le Four.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Baldry then received from the hands of Miss Crosfield, a sugar basin, presented by Captain Waring,as a small token of the services he had rendered ' to Captain Waring and the corps. Dr. Adams was presented with a handsome silver cheese dish,which had been subscribed for by the mem- bers of the corps, for the excellent services he had rendered to the corps as chief surgeon, the presentation being made by Superintendent Baldry. Dr. ADANIS said the presentation came as an absolute surnrise. He supposed he was one of the good attend- ance men, because he did not know what other merits he had to deserve such a handsome recognition for anything he had done for them. He was very proud to see so many attendance prizes, and certificates. If the brigade went on increasing he did not know whether they would be able to present prizes. It took him back to the time when they had few members, and it was a record to see so many present, and anyone might well be proud. (Applause.) He did not think he would have been able to do as much as he had done but for the help of his brother surgeons. (Hear, hear.) Alluding to some of the men who were withnut uniforms, he honed when fends f ivoured them to have them in uniforms. Meer, heir.) The work they accomplished was well-known to others, and they took a justifiable pride in the fact that one of their members had received the Royal Humane Society's certificate. (Applause.) A display was then given by several men as to the methods to be used in resuscitating an apparently drowned person, and considerable interest was taken in the proceedings. Second-Officer Hume in proposing a vote of thanks to Miss Crnsfield, said they all regretted the absence of the Chief Superintendent. It was a great pleasure to have a gathering like that, and it was also encouraging to see so many ladies present. Alluding to the nursing division formed by the ladies in the brigade he hoped they would continue to have the same regard for the work in the future as they had alre tdy displayed, and then Warrington would hold its own not only for the men's brigade but also for the ladies brigade. (Applause). 1 Dr. An kNIS in seconding, said in the early days of the struggle Captain George Crosfitsld was good enough for , some years to undertake the secretarial work of the brigade when it was trying to grow,and when they could find no one to take lip the duties of an apparently moribundand dying institution. They owed a debt of I gratitude to the Crosfield family, and he had very much pleasure in seconding the vote of thanks. Miss CROSFERLD briefly replied. Superintendent BALDRY tendered his than-s to the men who had assembled to witness the distribution of the prizes, and he hoped in time to come the siublic would ake a little more interest in their work. (Hear, I hear.) Only those who had been attended by ambu- lance men could speak of the services rendered. They treated scores of cases every week which did not come before the public notice. (Applause.) A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the pro- ceedings. SUPPER AT TETE LION HOTEL.</p>
        <p>After the ceremony at the Police Station the mem- bers of the Corps adjourned to the Lion Hotel where supper was served. Second Officer T. J. Hulse presided over a large gathering. The loyal toasts csere heartily honoured on the pro- position of the Chairman. The CLIAIRM 11,1 proposed "The Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade" and expressed the hope that it would continue to progress like it had done during the past year. At this stae Superintendent Baldry said that during the present football season a Warrington player who was injured on the Wilderspool ground !was attended to by Private Price, a member of the Corps, and in order to show their appreciation of his services the Football Club had sent him a medal. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. W. B. ROBERTS said he had pleasure in handing the medal to Private Price on behalf of the Football Club. The tiction of Trivate Price was evidence of his enthusiasm in ambulance work, and should be an example to other men. (Applause.) First (Meer WILLIAMS proposed the health of" The chief surgeon (Dr. Adams) and the hon. surgeons." Without the assistance of the medical profession the Corps would be a nonentity. It was tNthe doctors that .17 owed a debt of gratitude for teaching them what in tie of accident turned out to be a blessing, and in many cases saved life. Dr. ADAMS responded and gave some interesting reminiscences of the beginning and progress of the ambulance movement in the locality. He remarked that he came to Warrinetou in 1883, but prior to that date he had an interesting experience in ambulance work at Maryport. Cumberland, where he started a suc- cessfill'class which was flourishing at the present day (Applause.) With regard to the work at Warrington, it was on the suggestion of the late Mr. John Pollitt that he organized a class which was held in the wheelwrights' shop at the works of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. In 1892 another class was formed at Latchford and two courses of instruction were given. In the meantime the Cheshire Lines Coin- rnittee had carried on is very active ambulance work and in 1394 the Latchford and Cheshire Lines classes were amalgamated and the meetings were held at the Coffee House. Then they became more ambitious and secured a room at the Friends' meeting house. At all the classes to which he had referred he believed that the percentage of passes obtained at the examinations was never under ninety and that, he considered, was very satisfactory and creditable. ',Applause.) He was glad that some who were members at the beginning of the move ment were still active ambulance workers. (Hear, hear) The corps afterwards met at Messrs. Crosfields' works land in 1890 removed to the Gymnasium where for the 11 first time they had a fair open spice to manceuvre upon, I He looked back with very greit pleasure to the work at, tb Gymnasium as it was there that the Corps really attair Represent efficiency. A class had recently been fermi for ladies and there were now many efficient pupil The introduction of the class led to a demand for rum accommodation and the Corps were now very comfort- ably installed in the parade room at the Police Station. They owed to the Chief Constable and the Corporation a deep debt of gratitude for the handsome accommoda- tion afforded them. He looked forward to the time when the Corps would achieve inflnitely greater records than anything they had accornplised in the past. (Applause).</p>
        <p>the i ned led Is. 11'13</p>
      </div>
