      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>OUR NEW SERIAL.</head>
        <p>OUR NEW SERIAL.</p>
        <p>'A SON ON E NIPIR .1</p>
        <p>This is the title of the next story we shall publish in our columns. It is from the pen of the well-known novelist and journalist, MORLEY. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>'4 :A, SON OF EMPIRE,'</p>
        <p>MORLEY ROBERTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>AUTHOR OF</head>
        <p>AUTHOR OF</p>
        <p>"The Master of the Silver Sea," "The Adventures of a Ship's Doctor," "Strong Men and True,"" The Keeper of the Waters," "The Colossus," etc.</p>
        <p>The Author of our story has a world-wide / reputation. He went to Australia before he I was twenty years of age, and worked on the Victorian railroads and in the bush of New South Wales, chiefly with sheep and cattle,</p>
        <p>Then he served as a sailor, and ultimately found his way into Government employment</p>
        <p>but the workOf a civil servant wasloo- slow</p>
        <p>for him, and he started on a long series of travels. Few men have had such a varied experience as MORLEY ROBERTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SON OF EMPIRE.'</head>
        <p>SON OF EMPIRE.'</p>
        <p>`A SON OF EMPIRIC</p>
        <p>SOMETHING ABOUT THE STORY:</p>
        <p>Madge Gretton, while touring abroad with her mother, becomes acquainted,in an uncon- ventional way,with a certain Captain Richard Blundell, a military man of great reputation as a traveller and linguist,and whose advance- ment in the army is hindered by an old feud with one of his superiors. Madge is an ardent Imperialist. She long ago made up her mind to marry a man such as Blundell. Her mother strives to make her respect the conventions, but one morning the girl secretly goes mountaineering with Blundell, who is interested in her, and she falls in love with him in earnest. She goes to see him off when he departs for Dum-Dum, and he kisses her. It transpires that his greatest enemy in the service is Sir Daniel Fielding, the Adjutant- General, the father of Madge's closest friend, Annette Fielding. Sir Daniel Fielding's intense hatred of Blundell arose from a light affair of the heart, in which the younger man won and made his chief appear ridiculous. Sir Daniel has had a life-long revenge by preventing Blundell's advancement in the service. When Madge returns home she casts about for a plan whereby she can dis- cover the cause of Sir Daniel's hatred, hoping afterwards to obtain some influence which can be used in Blundell's favour. She finds that the hatred is implacable, and then determines at the opening of a frontier war to aid her hero by sending a cipher cable- gram in Sir Daniel's name requesting the Commander-in-Chief in India to provide Blundell with employment in a distinguished position. This is done. Blundell obtains a command and distinguishes himself greatly. In the very moment of success word comes that the cablegram has been repudiated as a forgery, and Blundell is placed in an ugly position, which will end in his being cashiered unless he can offer an explanation. He knows almost intuitively that it was Madge who concocted the cablegram, but bravely determines to shield her, let the cost be what it may. The scene shifts again to England, and Madge soon knows through the Fieldings that the forgery has been found out, and has the bitterness of knowing that she has probably ruined the man she hoped to assist. While he, unknown to her, is doing all he can to shield her, she is making every sacri- fice to rehabilitate him; and so love's conflict goes on, the man and woman both bent upon acts of self-sacrifice. An admirable and intensely vivid tale is brought to an end in</p>
        <p>a-- But our readers must learn what the ending is from the story itself. 4 A ci k-7 ON OF EM PI</p>
        <p>b- A SON OF EMPIRE,</p>
        <p>The opening chapters II ill appear in our issue of</p>
        <p>APRIL 28TH.</p>
        <p>RE.a. 17 16</p>
      </div>
