      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>APRIL 12th 1906.</head>
        <p>APRIL 12th 1906.</p>
        <p>The Bill introduced to the THE EDUCATION House of Commons by Mr. BILL. AUGUSTINE BIRRELL on Monday will no doubt be ' the subject of raging controversy, and will occupy much of the remainder of the Session. As to some of its provisions there will be wide and deep differ- ences of opinion. We see no reason to think, however, that the measure will be materially changed in its character before it passes into law. Churchmen will no doubt describe it as a Nonconformist measure, first of all for the reason that it meets Nonconformist griev- ances, and then because they have never brought themselves to understand that undenorni- national teaching cannot by any possible construc- tion be the teaching of the tenets of .Nonconformitr The Nonconformists have never asked for Os introduction of Nonconformist teaching into the schools, and their special forms of belief are as much excluded from the schools by this Bill o are those of the Church of England or the Church I of Rome. The Government measure sets out by. placing all schools supported by public money on fair and equal terms. We have bad two classes of schools under Mr. Balfour's Act, the " provided "class and the "non-provided "class. After January 1, 1908, only one class of school will be maintained by the local Education Authority. All the schools will be "provided." They will be of the same standing, that is to say, as the old School Board schools. If any denominational teaching is given, it will be by special arrange- ment, by desire of the parents, outside ' those school hours which count, and at the expense and through the teachers of the denomi- nation by which it is given. There will be no com- pulsory attendance at religious instruction, and the school teachers will not be permitted to give denominational teaching. Mr. BIELRELL, as we anticipated would be the cod, does not go the length of introducing into his Bill the principle that the State should identify itself only with secular instruction ; but he does, notwithstand- ing Mr. C. F. G. 312.arEameres contention to the contrary, make provision that the State shall subsidise no form oi religion. A strong effort will be made, especially by the Labour Party, to secure the exclusion of all religious teaching from the school. On this basis, perhaps the great question of the education of the young will ultimately be settled. Professor ANTlioNy is probably right when he says to us that if such a system were introduced,the Churches without distinction of name would rise to their opportunities, perceive and discharge their duties, and there would be no more irreligion in the land than there is at the present time. Mt the Government merely exercises ordinary dis- cretion in assuming that the time has not yet come when a measure on those lines could either be carried through the House of Commons or would be generally acceptable to the people. What it proposes to sweep away is not such religious teaching as can be approved equally by members of all denominations, but those facilities for proselytism which were contemplated and pro- vided for by Mr. BaLrouit's Act. There will be, after Mr. BleatEr.L's Bill has been carried into law, no schools realising Lord HUGH CECIL'S ideal,— and the ideal of all the Churchmen who talk about our schools" with a back-door letang from the schoolroom into the Established Church. Sir WlLLeseat ANsoer remarked on Monday that those who desire to bring up their children in communion with a particalar Church are not satis- fied with mere Bible teaching; they wish the Bible read in connection with the Catechism and the Prayer Book, and think that unless the two are worked together the child does not get that teach- ing which it ought to receive. He did not go the length of saying, as is being said by some other critics of the new measure, tbat the Church will endeavour to restore voluntaryism by setting up new schools of its own, or declining to hand over those which it has now in hand. He knows well enough, of course, that voluntaryism has been a hollow pretence and mockery for many years past, and that the raising of subscriptions to give new life to it is altogether beyond reasonable hope From the denominational point of view, the Government measure can be objectionable only to those who are in search of special privileges for their own denomination. On the ground of absolute faieness, it is open only to the criticism of those who object to all religious teaching, , whether undenominatio na 1 or not. For the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="6" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>moment, it will be judged with some heat, alike .by these and by Churchmen and Roman Catholics. it may be anticipated, however, that feelimg will cool down very considerably during the Easter • Recess, and that it will in a fortnight's time be seen with much more clearness than now that Mr. BranELL has come as near to the suggestion of a practical and lasting settlement—short of abso- lute secularization—as is at this time possible.</p>
        <p>The immediate effect of Mr. OPPOSITION TO AUGUSTINE BIRRELL'S Ed u- THE BILL. cation Bill has been to create widespread confusion among those who may be reckoned on as for one _reason or another the opponents of that measure.</p>
        <p>-The main difficulty is to find a point of attack in regard to which there can be anything even nearly approaching to agreement. Under all the criticism which has so far found expression there is clearly discernible the uncomfortable conviction that the minister of Education has produced a Bill so intractably just in its proposals that it must news-_ sarily command the approval of the bulk of the nation. It is seen to be impossible to claim more than the Bill offers without claiming more than is , actually due. Opposition, therefore, in most cases takes the form of incoherent anger. The Bill, says one leading Tory journal, proposes the establishment and endowment of Protectionist 'Nonconformity. The man who would say that would, as CHARLES LUIB remarks, say anything. And yet something like it is said over and over 'again. For instance, the " Pall Mall GazAte " ‘'which—much to our astonishment, we admit— declares that popular control of all schools main- tained by the community has become inevitable, and that it is exceedingly difficult to see how that control could be reconciled with leaving the appointment of the teachers to other hands than those of the popular authority) regards undenotni nationalism as the incubation of principles which , amount to a denial of the believer's standpoint whether he be Roman Catholic or Anglican. All that can be said concerning declarations of this kind is that, whatever amount of sincerity</p>
        <p>InaY be behind them, they are not true. The Bill</p>
        <p>Pr°Poses neither to teach the religion of lioncon- ;orinists nor anything that is hostile, or is an 'fliPeairnent to the definite teaching of any I.6ligiols body whatever. To affirm the contrary ef this ia merely to say what isnot capable of being suatained lsy argument and illustration. It is at • the same time a confession that the Bill is not °Pen to reasoned attack on principles. The • - limes" holds out to Churchmen the hope that, 'when Mr. BALFOUR Comes back to the House of ' Cornmons refreshed and reinvigorated by his rest- , enre, he will be able to say something to Mr. ' lllRltar.r. that nobody else can at present think of. is probable enough. In the meantime we have 80ane heated expressions from Dr. CA.sawrELTA,the Roman Catholic Bishop of Salford, and the Aiwa- ' lIISIIoP OF CANTERBURY. Dr. CABARTELLI affirms ; that the Education Bill is unsatisfactory from , beginning to end. "It is," he says, "an endow- ' Itent of a specific form of religion which I may A 1.1111 Birrelligion, an endowment at our expense 4 seems to be a practibal confiscation of our It liehools." The joke about Birrelligion is not , 13:iginal, by the way, and it would only have the ile."age value of a rather small joke if it were. (lc 14 no sort of manner describes the actual facts ""` the case. -ka to Dr. CASARTELLfB particular grievance, we Illay point out that at a meeting of the Genera Committee of the National Free Church Pannell 1 on Tuesday, a resolution was passed declaring that I. some of the proposals of the Bill are open to serious objection. Those proposals are, of course, easily ' identified. They are those which have been intro- duced for the purpose of placating Dr. CASARTELLI and his co-religionists. Mr. BIRRELL is in the position of a judge who satisfies neither party to</p>
        <p>the suit because he has held the balance • of justice so exactly. Even the ARCHBISHOP OP CANTERBURY'S condemnation of the measure requires some further elucidation. Dr. DAVID. seN Says that the Government Bill with draws from Churchmen the right to carry Principles which for more than half a century succ.ssive Givernments of all parties have encour- aged them to maintain. We may, perhaps, be , Permitted to ask what principles these may be, , and also to point out that if Churchmen had not jadliced Mr. BALFOUR to use his large and sub- , „ -arvient majority to break down the compromise c)f 1810, no new Education Bill would now have ,been necessary. His Grace of Canterbury, who tras played a far from wise or tolerant part in the education controversy, wonders whether all the rnembers of the present Cabinet can be consenting Parties to such a treatment of Trust property as Would violate the intentions of donors, some of Whom are even now alive. This presumes that the Government propose to alienate Trust property . from the Church. Does the Archbishop really believe that it has any such intention? Can he be ignorant of the fact that the Church is perfectly at liberty to keep its Trust properties in its own ‘, hands on the exact terms of the Trust? This protest does not strike us as at all a serious • Inlpeachtnent of the justice of the Bill or the inten- tions of the Government. It strikes us rather, iadeed, as something intended to incense those Ivr4ose imperfect understanding of the measure is tilil4ly to be a more powerful motive force than eir knowledge. So far, the criticism of Mr. laaELL's Bill seem to be altogether in its favour, ep „ee when it takes the form of denuaciation ; for r°ose who denounce it decline up to the present to .tk it in the face. As the " Times " remarks, ~rl4 a style of language which smacks somewhat of aria Reform, "it is difficult to go the whole hog, against the Bill." It is, indeed ;land so, perhaps the intemperate attacks that are made upon it in ac)rae quarters ought to be regarded with frienolly tolerance as the natural outcries of those whose Privileges are about to be shorn down somewhat 111 the interests of common justice.</p>
        <p>It was peculiarly aopro- A FITTING priate that a handsome memorial to the late Mr.</p>
        <p>John Crosfield should find ;Place in the Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. here has been no more generous benefactor to mission than the late Mr. Crosfield who as its cPeder took the keenest interest in the progress ttl'; the work. He was indeed delighted to find that t"a Place was so much appreciated and that it ,aPidly became a centre of great activity and a Power for good in the neighbourhood. The to which was unveiled on Sunday will serve :.43 perpetuate the memory of one whoseilvords and tatctions always exemplified the principles of toler- P°on in their broadest sense. The Rev. John to.nige, who in a brief address paid a fitting tribute ha the "my excellent qualities of Mr. Crosfield, iPIly alluded to this phase of his character "Ich"all who knew him admired. As he aptly tiltilzt it, Mr. Crosfield " was tolerant of everything ePt intolerance." It is a source of satisfaction</p>
        <p>to the mission workers that Mrs. Crosfield con- tinues to he so closely identified with their efforts and her gift uf the wiaduw is deeply appreciated by all.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spicer's Vagrants' Chil- VAGRANTs' dren Bill. which has been CIIILDHEN. introduced into the House of Commons, is the second attempt to deal with a matter which the opinion of those interested in philanthropic work, endorsed by the findings of the Vagrancy Com- mittee, has long regarded as demanding iminediata attention. In 1903 Sir John Gorst introduced similar bill, which however, got no chance of , making progress ; but it is hoped that in this case the attempt at legislation will meet with a better fate. The present Bill proposes to empower the ' arrest, without warrant, of persons reasonably sus- pectA of dragging their children about the country and evading the education laws. It would provid for the children to be taken out of the control of their parents, supposing the charge were proved, and placed in institutions, boarded out, or sent to one of the Colonies, as might be best. Sir John Gorst was profoundly interested in this matter in connection with his duties as Vice-President oi the Council during Lord S dishnry's last Govern- ment, and he urged very strongly the necessity for action. The statistics which have been pub lished show the very large numbers of children who are carried about the country from union to union by tramps. That the work of rescu;ng these little ones from the association with thieves and bad characters, from conditions of life repel- lent to civilisation, and from blank ignoranc,i, should be helped forward as speedily as possible many Members of Parliament feel deeply.</p>
        <p>Practical jokes have always I RIGGING. obtained amongst healthy young men, and the ragging ' cases which are so much talked about and con- demned in the army are nothing to what they were a century ago, when the newspapers failed to report them. The funny man in an officer:a mess is , generally popular.bocausehis wit and humour is a great relief after the humdrnm of daily military routine. If he suggests something occasionally which appears outrageous to the civilian mind, it , must be remembered that there is much to be I said for the soldier who is of little use to his country if he lacks what are known as animal spirits. But now, owing to the newspapers, like the canny Scot he jokes " wi' deeficulty:' The young subaltern in the Guards who has been recently , stripped, smeared with jam, and decorated with feathers, was no doubt exceedingly uncomfortable for a while. But , the custom of befeathering as a mode of 1 punishment dates back to medimval times. There was no jAm used in those days. Pitch—sometimes boiling pitch—was employed. The poor wretch was then thrown headlong into a cask of feathers. As soon as he was covered with them, dogs were set upon him and he had to run for his life, his 1 tormentors enjoying the sport and urging the hounds to worry their victim. People were rougher in those days. They had no antesthetics. Yet the savage creatures responsible for such punishment -proved themselves brave enough in the times of the Crusaders. The trade of a fight- ing man is a cruel one, and amongst a body of hardy soldiers, rightly or wrongly, an effeminate creature is despised, This is not so in a civilian community, where the feeling is rather to give extra regard to the helpless and incompetent. Therefore, if a young man joins the Army of his own free will he must be prepared to take the rough with the smooth.</p>
        <p>It is the fashion for those THE ART OF who would be reckoned ADVERTISING. among the artistic to decry advertisement as not only inartistic but uncivilised. An interesting corres- pondence in the " Times" has, however, brought 'out other aspects of the question which, if they do not supply a complete justification,at least may be regarded as going in mitigation of the practice of public advertising. The mistake which many people make in discussing the matter is the failure to distinguish what is objectionable and what is demanded by the public convenience. The hoarding which offends the eye as one passes along in the railway train may come within the former category; but it only emphasises, the exception. By no process of reasoning an it be denied that advertisements do perform a service to the public by drawing attention to goods or to events; and if that be admitted the objections which a4e raised to advertisements are limited to a very small pro- portion of those displayed. What is really desir- able is that advertisers—especially those who patronise the public hoardings—should recognise that an attractive and artistically-designed poster or leaflet is better in every way than an ugly one. There has been a great improvement in that direc- tion of late years; and than) is no indication that the results obtained by the advertisers have declined. It is to the extension of the improve- ment that the artistic should turn their attention.</p>
        <p>St. Stephen's has jast dis- STRIKING cussed Local Option, and LICENSING FIGURES. Sir Henry Campbell-Ban nerman bas told an elm:3p tionally large deputation of Liberal Members that the Government will bring in a drastic and efficient measure of Temperance reform early next year which shall settle, for good and all, the question of licensing for years to come. Some informing of political opinion is certain to proceed in the mean- time; and it is therefore well to know what line reformers are pursuing, and what means they may adopt to impress their view on the Cabinet. For one thing, they will open up the prospect of enhanced revenue, Messrs'. Rowntree and Sherwell, in their new volume on "The Taxation of the Liquor Traffic," offer an uncompromising load. They say, For instance, that "the Trade" in the United Kingdom is under-taxed, by comparison with the United States, by "from £9,000,000 to £15,000 000" annually ; London's licences (ore.. duciog £275,000), taxed like those of Greater New York pr 1,000 of the population would yield nearly £3 000,000; while the total for urban die- tricts would be £10,000,000 instead oi £1,750,000. These figures are presented with compliments to the Chancellor of the Exchequer. It would be interesting to know in good time what Mr. Asquith thinks of them. The Prime Minister is clearly impressed.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S "MAYFAIR" BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>A NEW BISCUIT FOR AFTERNOON TEA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>TRADES EXHIBITION AT THE PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>TRADES EXHIBITION AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>During the week the trades exhibition at the Parr Hall has been extensively patronized. Held with the object of bringing before the public labour- saving appliances and modern foods, it has aroused considerable interest. The cookery lectures, with practical demonstrations, by Miss Smallwood, have proved particularly attractive and on each occasion Miss Smallwood has addressed large audiences of ladies. Another pleasing feature has been a series of promenade concerts by capable artistes whose efforts have been well received, and in an anti-room flying lady exhibitions have occasioned no little mystification. As to the exhibits these are artis- tically arranged in the body of the Hall and have received considerable attention. The entrance Hall is occupied by Mr. Wadsworth, Sankey-street, and Mr. W. H. Ireland. The former shows a splendid selection of pianos and organs and many have admired the beautiful tones of the Broad- wood Pianoforte which he brings particularly before the notice of visitors. Mr. W. H. Ireland has an excellent selection of bicycles, ranging from the latest .pattern light path racer to the more substantial roadster and motor cycles. Messrs. W. Owen and Son, Sankey- street, make an effective display of jams &amp;c., and call special attention to their tea and coffee, cups of which, together with light refreshments are obtainable at the stall. Amongst the fair sex in particular Pendlebury's 1906 washer has aroused considerable interest and practi- cal demonstrations prove the validity of the claims of the invention as a labour-saving device. The cheapest artificial light yet invented is shown by Messrs. Haggett and Worthington, local electricians. Amongst the general exhibits is a miniature refreshment saloon, per- fectly and artistically fitted up by Van Houten's. Th I world-famed Van Houten's Cocoa is served in Royal Worcester cups,at the nominal charge of ld.,th is including biscuit, and a dainty serviette, in addition to which, ladies desirous of trying the cocoa at home, are readily supplied with a pretty tin, containing sufficient Van Houten's Cocoa for several cups. Messrs. Van Houten also exhibit their new Eating Chocolate, which ranks among chocolates as Van Houten's Cocoa does among cocoas. The exhibition will be open until the 18th inst.</p>
        <p>Nial..._</p>
        <p>J•I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>The Examiner, Thursday, April 12 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>A. new serial story by Morley Roberts, entitled " A Son of Empire" will commence in the " Examiner " on April 28th. * * * " Truth " gives the following as the recipe for a " Rag." Strip a subaltern ; wash clean with motor oil ; add jam ; sprinkle with pillow-feathers. This is a very pretty garnish for a Guardsman. *** '"The Rev. Dr. Aked, of Liverpool, said: "It is even said that one ot the brilliant men of our day, froni whom you are expecting so much, is to-day doing his work on champagne." —We have received letters from 057 M.P.'s denying the allegation.—" Punch." '</p>
        <p>'* * * • Let fanatical religionists go warily. Let them be- ware lest their amazing hostility to simple Bible teaching does not end in the exclusion of the Bible from the schools altogether. The forces of the new Parliament in favour of a purely secular system are strong and determined.—Dr. Afacnamara iii " Daily Chronicle." . .</p>
        <p>* * * The members of an Irish Orange Lodge have decided to signify their feeling with regard to Princess Ena's marriage to King Alnhonso by wearing bands of crape on their arms on the wedding (lay! The marriage, they believe, is only the outcome of one of a series of plots at present being made for the un- dermining of our Protestant Constitution. *** The complete absence of contentious subjects and the self-congratulatory nature of the proceedings at the Sankey Parish Council meeting on Monday called forth a remark to the effect that that august body is developing into a mutual admiration society. After the stormy scenes at meetings of local legislators not many miles from Sankey, it is quite a relief to attend , meetings where such harmony prevails. * * Captain Waring, the genial and popular Chief Superintendent of the Warrington Corps St. John Ambulance Brigade, was unable to be present at the annual prize distribution of the corps on Saturday : evening owing to the death of Mrs. Waring's mother. Our readers will recollect that it is only a few weeks since Captain Waring's only son died in India, and this second bereavement will, we are sure, evoke widespread sympathy with the Captain and his wife. * *. _</p>
        <p>At one of Blackburn's principal churches on Sunday a child, who had entered the family of a Mr. and Mrs. Edward, was christened "King Edward," and curious to add the son marked the seventh child born to this happy couple. There can be no question, about the appropriateness of the name under the circumstances, but we think the youngster who has to grow up and go to school under such a cognomen is to be pitied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCOgN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The opening run of Ow Wednesday Section of this club was held on Wednesday lea week to Chester. There was a goodly number present and, after partaking of an excellent tea, the majority of the members had a plea- sant row up the Dee, Runcorn being reached at 9.15. WIDNES FIRE BRIGADE. The annual report of the Widnes Fire Brigade has just been issued. It states that the total nnmber of fires that have occurred during the year 1905-5 (exclu- sive of chimney fires) is five, all of which were within the borough. One was serious and the remainder slight. The brigade had five calls, of which, on four occasions they extinguished the fires, the remaining one being extinguished by neighbours. The estimated total loss from fire is £450. RATES IN RUNCORN DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Urban District —Council have decided to levy a general district rate of 3s. 9d. in the £ for the current year, an increase of 3d. on last year. The increase is caused by the expenditure upon procuring Vyrnwy water. The magistrates ha viS signed the follow- ing poor rates :—Runcorn, Is. Gd. in the £ to produce £4,155 Is. sd. (a decrease of Id. ); Aston Grange, us.; Aston by Sutton, Is. 10d.; Sutton, 15.10 d.: Halton,2s.2d. (decrease 3d.); Stockharn, is. lid.; and Clifton, Is. lid RUNCORN CAPTAIN'S DEATH. On Tuesday, Robert Edwards, captain of the coasting vessel John and William, belonging to Messrs. Rowland of Liverpool, died at his home in Grove-street,Runcorn. Last Wednesday, when on a voyage from Penmaenmawr to Liverpool with a cargo of stone,he fell from a locker on which he was sitting on to the fire, and was some- what severely burned and injured his back. Every possible attention was paid him on board, and on arrival in port he was conveyed home and attended by Dr. M'Lennan ; but his case was regarded as hopeless, and he died as stated. He was fifty-nine years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WESLEYAN MISSION AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN MISSION AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The ten days' mission which has been conducted at the Victoria-road Wesleyan Church, Widnes, was con- tinued on Sunday. Mnl Armon Jones, the Welsh "gospel singer," related his experiences at the morning service and also sang to the great pleasure of the con- gregation. In the afternoon the Rev. Cecil Parker, of Penketh, addressed a meeting in the afternoon and Mr. Armon Jones sang. The service in the evening was conducted by the Rev. Cecil Parker, and solos were rendered by Mr. Amon Jones and his brother, Mr. Ellis Jones, of Liverpool. On Monday evening Mr. Armon Jones was the soloist at a special meeting. RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. _</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Sessions on Monday, before Sir Richard Brooke (in the chair), and Mr. John Littler, John Warder, Penketh-lane, was fined ss. for having used obscene language in Penketh-lane on March 31st. Arthur Fletcher, Halton-road, was charged on a warrant with having failed to appear on a summons on December 27th. He said he was in badcircumstances at the time. He was charged with having used obscene language in Halton-road on December 16th. There was a long list of previous convictions anld he was fined 10s., including costs. Richard Burt, Warrington, was fined is. 6c1., for having ridden a bicycle without a light in Chester-road, Sutton Weaver, on the night of March 25th.</p>
        <p>... EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON. Another example of the generosity of the Crosfield family to Warrington was furnished on Sunday, when : LOCOMOTIVE ORDERS FOR VULCAN FOUNDRY Mrs. Crosfield unveiled the beautiful stained-glass 1 The Buenos Ayres Great Southern Railway Company, window which she had presented to the Working- 1 who operate- some 2,500 miles of railway track in the men's Mission, Thewlis-street, in memory of her . Argentine, and who some few months ago placed with husband, the late Mr. John Crosfield. Warrington y, Ltd., of Newton-le-Willows, an and the Crosffeltls have long been inseparably associ- , locomotives, for fifteen heavy eight-wheeled bogie freight the Vulcan Foundry, ated, and though the members of the family are more : correspondent, orderswfoar73aB addittioenasiuluoacoflmraloiisZ 93 scattered than formerly they still continue to display S of which are to be of the compound goods type, whilst that interest in the welfare of the townspeople which the remaining 16 engines are to be built for working has always endeared them to the Warrington public,. either passenger or freight services. * * CONGREGATIONAL ANNIVERSARY. * The Unemployed Act, says Alderman Tinnion 1 The 65th Sunday school anniversary was observed (the chairman of the local Distress Committee) has when the y at the Newton Congregational Church, Rev. Mark Simon, of Liverpool, was the last Sunda not so far been of great value in Warrington. It was rre day was the musical service really not expected to be. The powers conferred ngheearf.rernA afternoon, for of r which an excellent programme of upon Distress Committees are so very restricted that sacred music had been prepared. There were large little can be done to relieve distress. Happily, how- 1 congregations. ever, employment seems to be pretty brisk in Warring- i EARLESTOWN PRIMITIVE METHODIST ton at present although as it is many unemployed 1 CHURCH. register their names at the bureau. The committee s . Encouraged by the s