        <p>tea prow._ . j * * Leyland, Morse, E. Mullins,Pendry% Ra'wlinson,Rollason * The pugnacity of the Stockton Heathens is pro- , and Woodcock. During the evening the conversation verbial but the residents of Whitefield-road appear to was interspersed by vocal items rendered by Mesdames be of a particularly combative turn. Not content with Beardshaw, Rawlings, and Smith, and Messrs. Jackson, being the prime movers in the tree planting dispute, Sprost?n and ,E. Mullins, all of which were thorolighLy 1 appreciated. The accompanists were Rev.J. H. RolLigon ,arochial and Mrs. E. Mullins. A recitation they have nw taken up arms against the by Mrs. Committee. That body thought it was conferring a Holleyock gave much pleasure • and altogether a most boon qn them by flagging the footpaths,but the White- enjoyable evening was concluded at ten o'clock. field-road dwellers imagine this would destroy the j ACTION FOR PERSONAL INJURY. i As reported a fortnight ago Thomas Evan Roberts, of rural aspect of the road, and a petition was duly signed and presented to the Parochial Committee which Wargrave-road, Earlestown, brought an action in the iat once capitulated and peace reigns once more. i Warrington County Court against Messrs. Bennie and * * * : Thompson, of Warrington,who have been making certain teurraetlohnisnitgoeltfhi? road at Vulcan Villatg; Judged by a somewhat ample standard it has been t e. d been left on' the proved beyond doubt that owing to the presence of !n2 IrrilMtsecrl2, and sCaivlE trees two persons cannot on certain footpaths in damages.—The case was to have been heard last Thurs- Stockton Heath, walk abreast. This is the result of day at Warrington, but Judge Bowen Rowlands had been a test by the Highways Committee of the District taken ill the day before and could not hold his Court. , Conncil—an unusually severe test —when it was found It was therefore adjourned to a special sitting, the date that two members of the Committee could not walk side for