her uncommon : with religious education irt elementary schools. :qr. '1 W. Thompson presided. Mr. Greaves Lord, a Liverpool achievement, i barrister, proposed the following resolution :—" That ' this meeting claims the continuance of the right of parents to have their children taught in their ovvn faith in their own schools during school hours by teachers on the staff who conscientiously believe what they teach. and is opposed to any proposal which would' divert to other purposes property held in trust for giving df religious instruction. '—Mr. R. M`Cartney, Manchester, seconded, and the Rev. Mr. Edmondson Parr, St. Helens, supported the motion, which was carried with acalaino- tion.</p>
        <p>* * * The fame of Widnes has spread even further than its pungent chemical perfumes. It has been likened to hell by Mr. Bart Kennedy, and is now compared with Naples, which, under present conditions, is not very unlike the other place. A correspondent at Naples, writing to a London paper on the eruption says: " The black deluge penetrates everywhere. It fills your eyes and ears. You feel it neat the skin and in your boots. In the streets the people are walking with their umbrellas open, although the sun is shining —" an English sun " they call it—here to-day. They might go further, and compare Naples under this visitation with Sheffield, Runcorn, or Widnes."</p>
        <p>--HEALTH OF EARLESTOWN AND NswroN Dr. Watkins, Medical Officer of Health, in his annual report for 1905 to the District Council, estimates the population of the township in the middle of last year at 18,859. The following are extracts from the report : Births.—Of the total number registered, 566 (16 illegitimate), 277 were males and 259 females. The birth-rate for the year was 30.33 per 1,000 of the estimated population. For 1904 the rate was 319 and the mean rate for the preceding ten years was 3112. The rate • for the whole of England and Wales for 1005 was 27•2 per 1.000. Town Hall Ward (the largest) hell 193 births, and Newton Ward, 65. Deatbs.—There were 270 deaths (3 illegitimate children) 134 males and 136 female. The death-rate for the year was 1447 per 1,000 of the estimated population for , deaths registered in the district. For 1904 it was 149 land the mean rate of the preceding ten years was 1556. Sixteen deaths occurred in public institutions beyond the district, viz., in Manchester Infirmary, 1, Haidock Cottage Hospital, 1, Warrington Infirmary 1, Warrington Fever Hospital, 3, Warrington Workhouse, 10; thirteen of those deaths were of males and 3of females. These 16 making a total of 280, the corrected death rate was , 1532 per 1,000. For 1901 it was 157, and the mean rate for the preceding ten years 1594. The rate for the whole of England and Wales for 1905 was 152 per 1,000. Infant Mortality.—Deaths under 1 year of age numbered 111 and were equal to a rate of 1307 per 1,000 births. `t!, 1901 the rate was 174 ; the mean rate for the preceding ten years was 152. The rate for the whole of England and Wales for 1905 was 128.1 There were 22 deaths from measles, and 52 from the 1 seven principal zymotio diseases.—The total number of I cases of infectious disease was 269.—The Birth-rate continues to go down, but with tho increase of work in the township, and the gradual return of young adults, it should improve.—The death-rate is lower, but not as low as it should be in such a district.—lnfectious diseases (excluding diarrhoea) were again very preva- lent throughout the year. Scarlet fever, as last year, existed in epidemic form all through the year ; there were 165 cases.—Measles existed in epidemic form in the third and early part of the fourth quarter—almost throughout the township, except the Wargrave Ward,' which had not many cases. The disease itself was of a rather • more severe type than usual and in many cases was followed by severe bronchitis and pneumonia, and accounted for many deaths. No case of small-pox occurred. The accommodation at the Warrington Fever Hospital has been ample during the year, every case, for which removal was desired, was accepted.—Unsound Food-5251b5. of unsound fish and meat have been surrendered during the year; 3501b5. of putrid fish were seized on one occasion. Legal proceedings followed, and the owner was fined £2 end costs.—Trade generally in the township has improved during the year, although it has not yet returned to its normal state. 'rho total number of cases in receipt of parish relief during the year was 1051. Of this number 908 received relief in money and kind,, and 143 received medical relief only. Of the total 141 male and 234 female adults and 531 children received relief in money and kind ; 23 male and 45 female adults and 73 children received medical relief only.</p>
        <p>* * Mr. E. Plinston, the Trades Council nominee on the Education Committee, maintains that the co-opted members of that authority " are having to do the work and are getting the kicks." This seems to us perfectly equitable. If any kicks require distribution they usually fall on those who have done the work—and done it wrongly in the opinion of the kickers. I In this case, bowc,,er, some impartial power is required to do a little kicking all round. The co-opted mem- bers are said to have extravagant tendencies--we don't actually endorse this view of them collectively —white there is no doubt that in the main the Corporation members of the committee have been somewhat lax in their attendance to duty. They have certainly no right to do all the kicking. * * The discussion on Burns's "To Mary in Heaven," recalls an episode in the career of Mark Twin. "Befo' de war " he was employed in the printing office of the " Weekly Hannibal Journal," and was once entrusted with the " answers to correspondents" column, during the temporary absence of the editor. A poetical effusiotr was sent in by an ardent lover to a Hannibal lady whose identity was thinly concealed under the heading "To Mary in H—i." Mark was unable to let slip such en opportunity, and framed a reply as follows :—" We will let this thing pass just this once, but we wish Mr. J. Gordan Runnels 0 understand distinctly that we have a character to sus- tain, and from this time forth when he wants to com- mune with his friends in H—l he must select some other medium than the columns of this journal." The paper sold amazingly, bat there was trouble in the editorial sanctum, and .nothing but Mark's youth saved him from being slaughtered in cold blood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>"Peter Pan."</head>
        <p>"Peter Pan."</p>
        <p>" Peter Pan ' will surely live in the memory of children young and old for a long time to come ; whilst the dog Nana,who bathes and dresses the little Darlings, the wonderful fight between the pirates and the Indians, the over-mastery of the pirates and the seizure of their ships by Peter Pan and the lost boys ; and lastly the home built in tile tree-tops by the fairies for Peter Pan, are spectacles of charming naivete—wonderfully effective in their appeal to the eye and to the memory of those who are still young and of those who have ever been young. Our readers will, we hope, make a note of the fact that "Peter Pan' opens at the Shakespeare Theatre, Liverpool on Mon- day, April 23rd, with a full London Company.</p>
        <p>. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE LATE IZEV. T. HOLLIS, OF ST. HELENS. A special service to the memory of the late Rev. T. Hollis, superintendent minister, was held at the Wesleyan Church, St. Helens, on Sunday morning. The Rev. F. R. Smith preached a sermon on "Character," and paid a tribute to the life and work of their respected superintendent minister: Special hymns and music were rendered, and the organist, Mr. H. Helsby, played the " Dead March." PROPOSED NEW CHURCH FOR PRESCOT.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool and the diocesan authorities are, it is reported, considering a proposal to erect a. church in the neighbourhood of Whiston Workhouse, with a parish formed out of the present parishes of Prescot, Whiston, and Rainhill. The township of Whiston is a very extensive one, and the great and in- creasing population in the neighbourhood of the Liv3r- pool main road, between Prescot and Rainhill, are almost two miles away from ‘Vhiston Church, so that the need of a church near the main road has been felt for a good many years past. ST. HELENS SHEPHERDS' JUBILEE. At Moss Bank, St. Helens, has existed for fifty years past a flourishing lcy'ge of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, under the title of "The Shepherds on the Hill." This lodge being first in numbers with 151 mem- bers, and being one of the wealthiest in the Order of Shepherds, the jubilee of the "Shepherds on the Hilt " was celebrated on Saturday by a dinner and congratula- tory addresses. Mr. F. C. Davies presided. The toast of "The Order " was proposed by Mr. C. D. Fothergill, and acknowleged by the Chief Shepherd of the order. Bro. J. H. Garteide, who complimented the Moss Bank lodge on its great prosperity. The order itself was founded in 1826, at Ashton-under-Lyne. Several friends from Liverpool and other parts of the county were present, and the proceedings were most interesting throughout. ST. HELENS SCHOOL'S CENTENARY.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Congregational Sunday schobls will celebrate their centenary in October next, the schools having been opened in September, 180 G. The usual anniversary services, by whioh funds are raised for the summer treat, &amp;c., were held on Sunday. The Rev. William Evans of Blackpool, preached morning and evening, and in the afternoon the church was filled by a great gathering of scholars and parents. Close on 700 teachers and scholars are connected with the school,and the present superinternients are Messrs. A. Thompson, W. Webster, W. Burrows, and W. Bartley. Special hymns, recitations, &amp;c., were given by,the children. It was announced that at the centenary services it was hoped they would have with them Principal Ritchie, who took the late Rev. J. A. Mitchell's pla.ce as prin- cipal of Nottingham Institute, along with the Rev. Silvester Horne and the Rev. Thomas Yates, and that in preparation for the centenary the Sunday schools were to be renovated and bcautifled. DANTE'S "INFERNO "OUTDONE.</p>
        <p>John Denton, a stalwart tnan,of I, Anne-street,created considerable amusement at the St. Helens Police Court on Nlonday, when he cross-examined the witnesses wko appeared against him on charges of assault. Denton had a black eye.—Police-constable Smith said he locked the man up out of a lodging-house, where he found him being held down on the floor by four of the lodgers. He alleged that he had paid 4d. for his lodgings, and as he had fallen out with the Keliys he asked for his 4d. back. Mr. Kelly said, " Yes, come inside and I will give you the 4d." They got him inside, and then set on him and gave it hint hot. The witnesses on the other side alleged that Denton was exceedingly violent, that he used pots and pokers, and was a perfect terror.—Denton told the magistrates that as soon as the Kellys got him into the house they set on him like a flock of bees. "Talk about Dante's 'lnferno,' it was not in it. I have been in many a rough scrap, but nothing like that."—A woman witness said that Walsh knocked Kelly down with a mallett. He had it for knocking them out N% hen they did not do as he wanted therm—The bench bound Denton over for six months, and tined him 10s. and costs for assaulting Patrick Walsh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>• WORKINGMEN'S MISSION. Memorial Window Unveiled by Mrs. Crosfield. SERMON BY REV. JOHN YONUE.</head>
        <p>• WORKINGMEN'S MISSION. Memorial Window Unveiled by Mrs. Crosfield. SERMON BY REV. JOHN YONUE.</p>
        <p>The intimate connection of the late Nlr. John Crosfield with the Workingmen's Mission, Thewlis- street, has been fittingly commemorated. By the generosity of Mrs. Crosfield a handsome stained glass window representative of Christ blessing the children has been erected in his memory at the Mission. This was unveiled at a special service held on Sunday after- noon at which there was a crowded congregation. Mrs. Crosfield performed the unveiling ceremony saying as she did so "This window is dedicated to the glory of God and in memory of John Crosfleld, founder of the Mission."</p>
        <p>During the course of a brief address the Rev. JOHN YONCE said the memory of an honest, true, and gener- ous life merited perpetuation—it deserved to be kept from premature or easy forgetfulness. Besides that it , was worth keeping, as a help, an example, an incentive, ' and an encouragement to those who were to occupy the , piece and the sphere lathe future. So far as they could' see to-day, the name, of " Crosfield "was not likely to fade into obscurity in this town, and least of all in that immediate locality. All the more was it desirable that the name of " John Crosfield " should receive this last- ing memorial, and receive it in that building, so that generations yet unborn, as they saw that window, might learn of the man who cared for that Christian cause from its earliest days and did so much for it as long as he lived, Mr. Crosfield held the very broadest opinions concerning Churches and creeds. By birth—he believed —and also by coneiction and preference, a member of the Society of Friends, he yet felt he could cultivate friendship with members of all the Churches and pro- fessors of all the creeds who feared God and thought upon His Name. In ell matters of .religion ho was tolerant of everything except] " intolerance." For that ite had to sympathy, and concerning those, whether lay or clerical, who unchurched each other because of ceremony or creed, conformity or nonconformity, he could, on occasion, speak with a good deal ' of charncteristic and emphatic repudiation. Every honest and earnest Christian, and all who ' tried to do right as in the sight of the Great Master, always had a friend in Mr. Crosfield. It was not every man of means who understood the art, and the virtue, of being generous—rather did it often appear as though a man's success in accumulating wealth rendered him increasingly incapable of distributing— bat the friend, whom they were met to memorialize the day had a generous heart, and a willing hand. Who could doubt that his many acts of kindness ascended to the7Hreat Father, and became a memorial in His sight? They were rejoicing that afternoon in the loving gift of Mrs. Crosfield, by which she sought to perpetuate the memory of her husband in that place, and among them as a Christian Society, for which and for whom he had such long and devoted attachment, but he was sure that although this beautiful gift was her provision they COM. pletely took their part in the loyalty of gratitude and love which his many years of help must have inspired. Personally he had many reasons to respect the memory of Mr. Crosfield. Although he did not think he could claim to have been one of his very intimate friends,still the occasione were numerous when he had the privilege of friendly intercourse with him, aud when he did hiss kindly services and he learned to very highly esteem him. He appreciated his breadth of view,— his freedom from all mere conventionalism, his habit of frank and fearless speech upon topics of the day, which might, indeed, not always have been relished quite as completely by others as by him (the preacher). Mr. Crosfleid admired true religious char- acter, wherever he discerned it. He hated all shams and mere pretences. He deprecated all religions parade and ostentation and he honoured all true, brave,fearless, noble men. Re believed him to have been a true and intelligent friend of the working classes ; and if he had been spared to see this day of progress, which now had so gloriously dawned upon the country he would have shared the gratitude and joy with which the nation at large had hailed it. That beautiful window reminded him of one of Mr. Crosfleld's peculiarly cherished delights, how genuinely he cared for children ,1 and young people. He supposed that the annual visit of their scholars paid for a long period to, Walton Loa was as true and great a delight to Mr. Crosfteld as ever it was to their scholars and people. Hence it seemed to him that the design chosen for this memorial window was very beautifully appropriate. It was appropriate also because this building is so closely identified with the training of children. To win the children to the Great .Saviour was to win the race to God.</p>
        <p>. During the service the solo" With verdure did" was excellently sung by Mrs.A.Dooth. Mr.Gough effectively sang" Behold I stand at the door and knock" while the anthem " Sing unto God " was ably given by the choir.</p>
        <p>Perseverance Awarded. At the Warriwrton Police Court on Saturday Mr, Harold Parson, of 32, Willis-street, applied to the magistrates for a certificate of exemption from vaccination in regard to one of his children. On Thursday Mr. Parson made a similar application which was refused.—Questioned on Saturday appli- cant said I conscientiously believe that vaccination would be injurious to the health of my child. I have had children vaccinated and they have broken out.— Mr. Fairclough : I have had ten childrrn vaccinated. I consider that an unvaccinated child is a danger to the public. I have conscientious objections to grant- ing the application. Mr. Fairclough then rose to leave, remarking to the other magistrates : Of course if the applic..nt satisfies you it will he all right. Mr. Fairclough then left the Bench, as did also Alder- man Tinnion. Messrs. J. W. Hayward and A. J. Jolley granted the order remarking that they had to carry out the law.</p>
        <p>, . -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Notice.</p>
        <p>The Warrington " Examiner " is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to &amp;liver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communiFa, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington Index to Inside Pages. PAGE.</p>
        <p>2. "A. Cruel Tempation." Great Sankey Parish Council. Padgate Sewage Scheme. 3. Women's Chat and Fashions.</p>
        <p>_ Workingmen's Mission : Memorial Window Unveiled. Science Notes. District News. 6. Local Church News. "A Dangerous Illusion," by Mr. 11. Whitham. Distress Committee. Warrington and the Ship Canal. Billiards, Liberals v. Catholics. Police News. Children's Corner. Draughts. 7. Football.</p>
        <p>Cycling Gossip. "Five thousand on the road," by W. 11. Ogilvie. ASK for CIINNLIGHAM'S HIGII-CLASS MINERAL WATERSI—Advt.. Personal. on inquiry this (Thursday) morning we were informed that there was no change in the condition of Dr. Kisby, whose serious illness we reported last week. LE!,-FRANCIS CYCLES—Britain's Best. Prices reduced, Demand increased. Inspection invited. Sole agent :—J. S. GA.RLAND, 51, Bridge St., War- rington.—Advt. Forthcoming Concert. • • - - - • - -</p>
        <p>A costume "a-nd coon concert in aid of the sick and poor and Sunday school funds of the Parish Church will be held in the Conservative Club Assembly Room on April 26th. An excellent programme has been arranged by Mrs. Ralph Johnson.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS, Garden Rollers, Garden Tools, Lawn Mower repairs a speciality.—J. S. GARLAND, 51,Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Penketh Friends' School. The annual sports in connection with the Penketh Friends' school were held on Saturday and were well attended. On account of the length of the programme most of the heats and some of the junior races took place earlier in the week. A new and attractive feature was a gymnasti.c display by the b ,ys, consisting of dumb-bell exercises and horse and parallel-bar practice..</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. IL W. Winstonley. The death took place at his residence, 16, Arthur- street, on Tuesday morning, at eight o'clock, of Mr. Ambrose Walter Winstanley, eldest son of Ur. George Winstanley, of St. Helens. The deceased, who was 39 years of age, had been a sufferer from asthma. He was a traveller for the Vacuum Oil Company, London. He was a Conservative in politics and worshipped at the Wesleyan Church in Bewsey-road. He leaves a widow and five children. J, R. WOOD, Piauo Dealer and Tuner, 47, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.-Advt. Mr. F. H. Crossley. This (Thursday) evening a presentation will be made to Mr Frank H. Crossley,the popular conductor of the Warrington Musical Society. It having become known that Mr. Crossley is shortly to be married, the members of the Musical Society took the opportunity of making some tangible expression of their appreci- ation of him, and as a result there should be a large gathering in Cairo-street schools this evening at eight o'clock. Mr. F. Monks: 3.P. will make the present- ation on behalf of the subscribers, arid it will take thl form of a handsome Georgian silver tea service, and roll top desk. interesting Local Wedding. - - -</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable local interest was solemnized at the Independent Methodist Chapel Friars'-green, on Wednesday, the contracting parties being Miss Mary E. Hudson, daughter of Mr. Hudson, 38, Parker-street, and Mr. William Henry Fell, Liver- pool. The bride, who is well known locally, was a teacher at Hamilton-street schools, and there was a large congregation to witness the ceremony. The bridesmaids were Miss Edith Jones, Manchester, and Miss Maggie Fell. The ceremony was performed by Mr. James Gandy, Enfield Hall. The reception was held in the schools, after which the happy couple left for Llandudno where the honeymoon is being spent. Everybody should see our SIN GUINEA CYCLES, unrivalled value, J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55 Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Death of Warrington's Oldest Tradesman. _ . _ .</p>
        <p>It is with regret we record the death of Mr. Joseph Shaw, butcher, which took place at his residence, Wilson Patten-street, on Sunday afternoon, at the age of 80 years. Deceased had been in falling health for a number of years, and had been confined to his bed for the past six weeks. He was the oldest and one of the most respected tradesmen in the town, nnd was president of the Warrington Tradesmen's Associa- tion. He had been actively connected with the butcher's business since he was twelve years of age when he attended the Liverpool markets on behalf of his sister. He commenced business himself in the Market Hall, opening the shop in Bridge-street in 1862. He was a Churchman and a staunch Conservative. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. The Easter Holidays. Given a continuance of the present delightful weather conditions, there will doubtless be a large exodus from Warrington this week-end to various favourite resorts. The railway companies bay° made special arrangements and the facilities offered are of the most complete-description. As usual Good Friday will be observed with solemnity in the Anglican and Roman Catholic Churches, where numerous services will be held. At some churches the Three Hours' Service, with 'addresscm on the Seven 'Words from the Cross, will be held from 12 to 3 o'clock. There wilfbe services in the morning at Wycliffe Con- gregational and Bold-street Wesleyan Churches and at the latter place of worship the annual foreign missionary meeting will be held in the evening. Easter Day will be celebrated with the customary rejoicings at the churches where the services will be of an appr3priately festal character. M ,nday of c mrse will be a general holiday and great numbers of the townspeople may be expected to betake themselves to the seaside•or to country places in the vicinity of the town. Cyclists in particular are looking forward to a highly eni )yable time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>– – – PUBLIC BENEFIT.-UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS fl 4 LANCASIIIRE.—Advt.</head>
        <p>- - - PUBLIC BENEFIT.-UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS fl 4 LANCASIIIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Friendly • Societies' Educational Representa- tive. A. meeting of the delegates from the local Friendly Societies was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday evening for the purpose of nominating a representa- tive to serve on the Education Committee. Mr. T. P. Bradshaw (Grand Master of the independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity) presided,and there was an attendance of thirty delegates representing the 'following societies:—Independent Order of Forresters Independent Order of Rechahites, Heart of Oak, Society, Order of Shepherds, Ancient Order of Shepherds, Irish National Forsters, Order of Druids, Independent Order of Oddfellows (Nfanchester Unity) and National Independent Order of Oddfellows. There were three nominees for the seat :—Mr. William Bibby, retiring representative (Order of Druids), Mr. Peter Peacock (Order of Foresters), and Mr. T. S, Ste (Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity). The voting was by ballot and resulted as follows :—Mr. T. S, Steel, 16 ; Hr. Peter Peacock 7; Mr. W. Bibby 7. Mr. Steel was therefore nominated as the representative and his name will be duly sub- mitted to the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
