      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>(Contimta front Page 6.) Preachers for Sunday. Bold-street Wesleyan Church : 10.30, Rev. J. Mann- ing ; 6.30, Rev. S. Pitt. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : 10.30, Rev. Matthews. ("The Symbolism of the Resurrection.") 6.0, Rev. .1. F. Matthews. (" New hope for the defeated.") Golborne-street Baptist Church : 10.30. Rev. H. Dunnico. (" The Kingdom of Christ.")--6.30, Rev. H. Dannico. (" The Church of the future.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>, Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>, Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday the preacher in the morn- ing was Mr. W. Hickman, and in the evening the Rev. A. C. Parker. There was a large attendance at an open meeting in connection with Mr. J. Upson's men's Bible class which was • held in the afternoon under the Presidency of Dr. Bennett. A powerful address on "The hand of Jesus " was delivered by Mr. Clarke. Several musical items were interpreted in a highly creditable manner by Mr. B. Wadiworth's Juvenile Choir and were much appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>- - Foreign missionary anniversary services were held at this Church on Sunday, the preacher being the Rev. H. Arnaud Scott. of Southport. There were good congre- gations,and Mr. Scott's disconrses,in which he eloquent- ly pleaded the cause of foreign missions, were listened to with deep interest. The offertories, which will be handed over to the Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Society, amounted to about 27—an advance on last year. The services will be continued on Good Friday. In the morning the Rev. George Lester (formeriy of the Bahamas) will preach, and in the evening the annual missionary meeting will be held, to be Addressed by the Rev. George LeSter, and the Rev. Cecil H. Wright (British Guiano). Mr. James Dodds will preside.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church. —" The Crucifixion." At Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening the choir rendered the late Sir John Stainer's work: "The Crucifixion: A meditation on the Sacred Passion of the Holy Redeemer." There was a large congregation. The music was rendered in a very devotional manner and reflected great credit on the choir. The principal soloists were lir. Arthur Griffiths (tenor) and Mr. Frank E. Browne (bass). Mr. Griffiths gave a fine interpretation of King ever glorious" while the voices ofjMessrs.Gri fff ths and Browne blended effectively in "So Thou fittest Thy in petition." The congre- gation joined belrtily n the hymns with which the work is interspersed. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac. ably presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Memorial Service.</head>
        <p>Memorial Service.</p>
        <p>Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel was filled on Sunday even- ing, when a special service was held in memory of the late Mr. Septimus Moss. The service was conducted by the Pastor, the Rev. J. S. Hughes, who took as his text the words " Our friend Lazarus slaepeth, but 11 are come that I may awake him out of sleep." Allusion was made to the life of the deceased, who had proved a loyal worker, a devoted member, and an ardent supporter of the church. He was faithful in attendance at all the services, and he would be missed, especially in the choir, where he took a leading and enthusiastic part. In conclusion the preacher showed the nobility of the service, the urgent need of such workers in their midst, and the splendid opportunities which, in spite of all difficulties, were opening up for the church at Hill Cliffe. Special hymns, favourites of the departed, were sung, such as" There is a land of pure delight," " Blest be the tie that binds," and ".Thou art gone to the grave, but we will not deplore thee." Mr. Hallas officiated at the organ, and concluded with a rendering of the Dead March in " Saul."</p>
        <p>The Rev. Paul Bulls' Visit.—" Social Purity." The Rev. Paul Bull, of the Community of the Resurrection, delivered the last of the series of Lenten addresses to men at the Parish Church on Friday. There was a crowded congregation and the discourse on " Social Purity" was followed with the deepest interest. IThe rev. gentleman has a plain and homely but nevertheless forceful style of delivery, and while he was particularly strong in his denunciation of the evils of impurity, there were in his address occasional flashes of quiet humour. The words selected for the text were; "When he came to himself he said how many hired servants of my father's have bread enougli and to spare, and I perish with hunger. I will arise and to go my father." The preacher said the text was one of the most beautiful in the Bible when looked at in its full, true meaning because in it our Lord had summed up all the sympathy of the heart of Jesus for humanity. Man was very often harsh in his judgment of his fellow men,but it was not so with the Saviour. In Jesus Christ man found One who desired to make the best of everyone and He would be very gentle with all who died their best. He (the preacher) would speak to them not as one standing on a pinnacle of virtue, but as a man with like passions to themselves, one who knew the tremendous force of temptation. Methods of vice now amongst us were practically unknown 100 years ago,and England at the present time was committing racial suicide. Dr. Taylor, one of the greatest medical authorities in Europe, had sounded the trumpet note of warning and reminded us that we were on the path of racial suioide. At the decay of other civilizations the same sins had occurredas were desolat- ing England at the present time. England stood lowest but one of European countries in its birth production. Russia and Japan were r roducing 50 per 1,000 births.ancl Germany 36 per 1,000, to our 27 or 28, while in some towns in Lancashire and Yorkshire it had fallen as low as 21 and 22. He believed the evil was most among the wealthier classes, but this dead rot was spreading down—or perhaps he should say spreading up—to the artizan classes and— destroying the most glorious life of England. He believed the cure for sin lay very largely in the clean teaching of the mystery of life. Any father who allowed his boy to go out into life uninstructed was allowing him to go blindfold on the edge of a precipice, in fact he was committing a crime against his son's future. The preacher urged his hearers to be pure in thoughts and words as that was the only way in which self-control could be won.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Baptist Church Colborne Street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church Colborne Street.</p>
        <p>. The above Church was crowded on Sunday even- ing when the Rev. H. Minnie° delivered a sermon on "The place of the Baptists in the life of to-day." Basing his remarks on Gala,tions 6c., xiv., the preacher said the common notion was that the Baptist denomina- tion existed for the sole purpose of teaching a particu- lar view of Baptism, and nractising the same rite in a particular way. That was not so, and the Baptist who believed that was conspicuously ignorant of the histori- cal principles which lay at the very foundation of his church. A true Baptist, placed righteousness before any ordinance, and conduct before any ceremony. They failed to appreciate the insight, heroism, and fidelity of their forefathers, if they imagined that those brave men suffered imprisonment and died on the scaffold for the sake of a silly squabble about ranch or little water. The principle out of which the Baptist Church originated was that which had throbbed in every moral uprising since the world began and a true Baptist was not a narrow-minded cantankerous, pettifogging specimen of humanity—although some who claimed to be Baptists , might be—but he was ever a passionate lighter for free- dom, a democrat, a deadly opponent of privilege, monopoly and tyranny. The place of the Baptists therefore in the making of England was no insignificant one and again and again in the evolution of England the flag of freedom had been borne aloft by Baptists hands. Time would only permit him to name a few out of coentless numbers who warked grandly, some like the sun in all its glory some like stars in midnight darkness. The immortal dreamer of Bedford was a Baptist as was that strenuous lover of liberty John Milton. It was a Roger Williams a IVelshman and a Baptist, who laid the foundation of religious liberty, in America from whence it is destined to spread throughout the world. John Foster the famous essayist and Robert Hall the prince of pulpit orators were both Baptists. William Cary the founder of modem missions was a Baptist, C. H. Spurgeon and Alexander Mclaven 'belonged to them. In the life of to-day they were not one whit, behind. The great and powerful fighter of all tyranny John Clifford was a Baptist and in the Church of their count -y the Baptists were represented by honoured names in -the persons of Lloyd George and Agustine Birrell. Baptists were ever foremost in the defence of these principles by which alone the great nation had been established and by 1 which alone it could be maintained. They loved reality, hated shams and were the pledged foes of all pretentious priestly or otherwise. Mr. Minnie() then proceeded to point out that whilst the Baptist Church had no creed in the narrow interputation of that word yet there were certain foundation principles. They did not require anyone seeking fellowship to give an account of there theologi- cal opinions, at any rate those churches which did so were false to Baptist principles. They asked one question. Do you believe in the Deathless Son of God and acknowledge Him to be your Master ? If you do then the door is open. This being so they looked upon the scriptures interpreted by each individnal believer as the final court of appeal and they repudiated vehem- ently every suggestion that the infinite grace of the infinite God was only dispensed through the mechanical agencies of the priest and sacraments. They stood for the inalienable right of every man to approach God for hmiself without the intervention of priest or prelate. The reason why Baptists had played so prominent a part in the education straggle was because an attempt had been made in the name of Education to teach the children of this country dogmas subversive of all they believ.W. They were told by some that such teaching was essential for the proper education of the rising generation. Such teaching was not worthy of the name of religious education it was a base attempt to plant the seeds of a blighting falsehood in the unsuspecting heart of a defenceless child. hi conclusion the preacher said that never was the Baptist witness more needed than it was to-day. It appealed to the truest instincts of manhood and gave scope for the most enduring fidelity. To them was entrusted the great task of securing absolute equality before the law for all Churches of Jesus Christ and to secure for every teacher in his profession and every scholar in the class absolute liberty \ from the tests of a sacredotal priesthood or a crude and narrow theology,</p>
      </div>
