      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC SERVANT'S THEFT.</p>
        <p>At Warring-to-n- on Monday, Elizabeth Donoghue (13), was chareed with stealing Li Is. the money of Louisa Perrin, Fairfield House, Manchester-road, and Mary Donoghue, mother of the other prisoner, Heaketh-sreett, was charged with receiving 19s. 41. knowing it to have been stolen.—The Chief Constable said the case was a very sad one. The little girl had been employed by Mrs. P?rrin as a domestic servant and considerable con- fidence was placed in her. On S kturday Mrs. Perrin left it satchel containing E 3 in gold hanging in the kitchen and subsequently she missed Li. On Sunday afternoon the little girl went to the shop of Mr. Robert Smith, 73, Manchester-road, and presenting the sovereign asked for change, saying that it was for Mrs. Perrin. Smith knowing the girl gave her the change. He naturally expected that the girl would go hack to Mrs. Perrin, and as she did not he went and informed Mrs. Perrin of what had happened. Informa- tion was given to the police, and Superintendent 111 ojkaghan visited Hesketh-street. At first both prisoners denied having seen the money. Then the girl said the sovereign had been given to her by a woman whom she did not know, nnd for whom she got the change. Later the girl admitted she stole the money, whi,th she said she gave to her mother. The mother was again qnestioned but until confronted with her daughter's statement denied having had the money. The balance of the change 15s. 8(1. was found under a brick in the yard of the prisoner's house.-Mrs. Donaghue said her daughter told her that the Li was ft gift from a gentle- man who lodged with Mrs. Perrin. When she (the mother) learnt that the money had been stolen she hid it, as she did not know what to do with it.—The mother was fined 205., and the daughter, having been warned by the magistrates, was allowed to go.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>FELL THROUGH THE ROOF.</head>
        <p>FELL THROUGH THE ROOF.</p>
        <p>Morgaret O'Mally, of no settled abode, who refused te eive any account of herself, was charged on remand at Warrington on Tuesdoy with being on the enclosed premises of the British Cocoa-rooms at 12.15 a.m. on April 3rd, with intent to commit a felony. Snperinten dent Monaghan stated that the manager (Mr. Fred Lamb) locked up the premises on the previous night, and shortly after midnight he heard some persons in conversation on the roof. Be got a light aril then he heard a scuffle. On going into the lavatory he found that the woman had fallen through the glnsa roof,cutting herself about the head. Prisoner now stated that she had been drinking heavily and not remember being on the premises. She was sent to gAol for one month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>P AStIVE RESISTERS SUM AIONED. —At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at the Hall Cafe; Stockton Heath, on Tuesday, the Revs. J. S. Hughes and H. Dormice, and Messrs. E. Lloyd Jones rani Henry Law, all of Stockton Heath, were summoned for non-payment of the education portion of the poor rate and the usual orders for payment were made, 10. ad. including costs in the case of Mr. J'ones, Us. 2d. in the case of Mr. Law is. 6d. in the case of Mr. Hughes, and 10s. in the case of Mr. Dunnico. There was no discussion, but the Rev J. S. Hughes remarked that the " Passive Resisters " 'oped that that was their last appearance before the magistrates under similar circumstances. All the amounts were included in one warrant.</p>
        <p>A correspondent of "The Law Clerk " mentions two clerks with a Liverpool firm of solicitors who have been in their employ for sixty-six and fifty-six years respectively. Both are still at work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
