      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DISTRESS COMMITTEE. ---- Opening of a Labour Exchangi.</head>
        <p>DISTRESS COMMITTEE. ---- Opening of a Labour Exchangi.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Distress Committee was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, Alderman Tinnion presiding. There were also, pre- sent :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Harrison, the Revs. W. Brice- camp and J. Yonge, Councillors Stott, Dr. Joseph, Wilson, Pemberton, Maginn, Wilkinson and Evans, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, S. Davies, J. Haley, J. Fargher (inquiry officer) and T. H. Spinks (assiatant to the Town Clerk.)</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL MATTERS. At a meeting of the Finance and General Purposes Sub-Committee, it was resolved that the Town Council be requested to include the sum of £2O) in their esti- mates for the ensuing year to meet the expenditure of rthe Distress Commit', that amount being made up as follows :—Salary of Inquiry Officer, £7B; "rent of office, £5 ; rent of telephone, £8 ; printing and stationery, &amp;c.. £2l • contingencies, £B5; total, £2OO.—A letter was read from the Nottingham Distress Committee stating that they proposed during the monthsl from March to October to cease providing employment unless the state of unemployment became abnormal, and also to dispense with their Investigation Officers until next winter, and asking for the views of this Committee on such proposal.— It was resolved that the clerk be requested to inform the Nottingham Distress Committee of the resolution come to for carrying on the Labour Bureau for at least another six months.</p>
        <p>The miuutes were approved. LABOUR.EXCIIANGE TO BE OPENED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Inquiry and Labour Sub-Com mittee on April 9th, the Inquiry Othcer's report was read,froin which it appeared that there were 292 persons registered since the opening of the Bureau, 265 being men and 27 women. Of this number 121 had obtained employment, 45 being through the agency of the Bureau. The following resolutions submitted by the Sub-Corn- mittee appointed to consider and report on the establish- ment of a Labour Exchange were approved and adopted : —That the existing office be continued as a Labour Bureau for at least another six months ' • That a Labour Exchange Department be instituted and that applicants who may desire it shall be registered on the Labor'. Bureau Register only, in respact of whotnja simple register of names, ages, addresses, and occupations shall be kept; that persons out of employment be advised to call at the office each morning in order that they may be ieformed where probable employment can be obtained ; That the morning office hours at the Bureau shall be from 8 a.m. to noon ; that the Town Clerk pre- pare and issue to employers a circular intimating that a Labour Exchange has been opened and that it is hoped it may be of some use in enabling employers to obtain such workpeople as they may need promptly and that the Office will be open at the above hours and is also on the telephone* A letter from the Secretary of the Association of Municipal Corporations was read suggesting that at the annual meeting of that Association the Chairman of the Distress Committee should attend with a view to a dis- cussion of the Unemployed Workmen Act, 1905. It was resolved that the Chairman of the Committee, Mr, Alderman Tinnion, be appointed to attend the anuual meeting to be held in London in May next. In moving the adoption of the minutes, Mr. JOLLY referred to the proposed establishment of a Labour ' Exchange. He said the departere would invohe no additional expenditure and it was hoped that although ' it was a very modest attempt, it might prove of some practical utility. The committee fully recognized that the trade unions did a great deal of the work for their own members which the suggested those which the might do and that many Labour Exchange performed functions analagous to friendly societies Committee was estnblisbed to perform. But they also recognized an additional fact which was sometimes overlooked and that was that there was a great I:Cfles of unorganized labour which lay outside the reach of trade unions and friendly societies. They hoped the Labour Exchange if carried on in an experimental way for a few months might be the germ of something of permanent utility to the section of the community he had named. Council tor WILKINSON seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT asked whether it was suggested that men who registered themselves at the exchange would be given preference over men who were registered at the bureau under the Act. Ha understood that the men registering at the exchange would not have to answer the questions contained in the inquiry piper which had to be filled at the bureau. The Rev. W. BRACECAMP thought it as of the utmost importance that the Labour Bureau should be carried on because it was much better that men who ; were out of work should have such a place to apply to. , The Rev. JOFIN YONGE said he believed the exchange' was merely a simplification of the registration as carried on under the Act. The CIitIRMAN said ther'e was nothing to prevent the officer taking the particulars obtained under the old system. Councillor PEMBERTON asked if the'labour exchange\ wail not an evasion of the Act ? I The CHAIRMAN : I understand the committee have been advised bit the Acting-Clerk that they can do this. Councillor- PEMBERTON : No one would think of registering under the Act if they can do it in the new way. Will not the committee run the risk of being surcharged with the cost ? The CHAIRMAN said it did not matter very rturth whether they ignored the Act or not. He thought the scheme suggested by the committee would be much better and simpler and do the same amount of work as the old system. The Actso far had not been of great value in Warrington. They had not used the emigration clauses land the only purpose for which they wished to continue the committee was to find employment for local men who were out of work. The committee had takee the advice of the Deputy Town Clerk who saw no difficulty in the way of the establishment of the exchange. Mr. Jou,av said the object or the committee was to try and evolve a simple organization to achieve the aims they had in view without all the elaborate book- keeping that the strict interpretation of the Order involved. A good deal of the officer's time had been taken up in book-keeping which was really not necessary. He thought the Local Government Board would be only too glad to help local authorities to •simplify their procedure. Many people seemed to imagine that, like other Government departments, the Local Government Board was simply fettered s.,ith red tape but that was a mistaken idea, as far as his experi- ence went. Dr. JOSF.PH said it seemed to him they were trying to make it as easy as possible to themselves and not to the poor fellows who were out of work. A case came under his notice which was pitiable and heart-breeikine,. It should be made as easy as possible for nervous, diffident people who were down on their luck to obtain work. Tbe worst cases, he was afraid, were kept back and it ought to be made as easy as pos3ible for applicants to come forward. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>FIRST D,IVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. CRUSFEET.D.S.—PIAyed it the War- rington rooms on Tuesday last. The meeting of these clubs had been looked forward to for some weeks by the draughts players of the town as likely to be the most interesting nritch of the season. So far this season the Warrington team had been undefeated, but as the Crosfleld's team has been considerably strengthened lately by the inclusion of F. Kelly, a well-known Liverpool player, it was thought by many that this would probably enable the soap-makers to score a victory. This proved to be the case, as, after a close and interesting contest, Crosfield's ran out winners by the score of five wins to three and six draws. Score : WINS. WARRINGTON DRAWS. CROSFIELD'S WINS. O F. Dunne ' 2' F. Kelly 0 O W. Rigby 0 T. Hardman O J. Hamblett 1 J. Conolly 0 F. Kenny 1 E. Norbury 1 J. P. Dixon 0 J. A. Fleming</p>
        <p>Or The Holborn Town Hall was put up for auction on Monday, and withdrawn when the bidding stopped at 425,000.</p>
      </div>
