      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Examiner, Thursday, April 12, 1906. '</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Thursday, April 12, 1906. '</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The preacher at the Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday was the Rev. J. F. fifatthews who both morn- ing and evening addressed large congregations. In the morning his subject was " The prophet of Paradox." basing his remarks on Isiah Iv. I. In the evening his subject was "The Gospel with power." He pointed out that when our most elaborate social schemes had been tested to their fullest extent it was still left for the Gospel of the grace of God to carry out the truest of reforms—sthe creation of a new man within. The Gbspel was a Gospel with power because it was intrinsi- cally one of power—the power of a unique and sublime personality inclose relationship with the Father.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting was held on Sunday when there was a fair attendance. Mr. William Miles presided and spoke words of sympathy and encouragement and ex- pressed the good he had received from those happy meetings week by week and the joy of the Gospel heard in their tuidst.—Mr. Blane, of the Manchester Lay Preachers was the speaker and spoke upon the " Crucifixion and the Garden." He alluded to the beauty, perfume and song in the garden. In the midst of these there was the Cross,and sorrow and death. The</p>
        <p>--- lessons were that in our crosses, temptations and sorrows we should remember the beauties, perfume and song around us that far exceed all the beauty of life— the perfume of character and the joy of service. Mr J. Barnes s•mg as a solo " Pray brethren pray." Mr INV. H. Payton, Mos. Bac. presided at the organ. Mr Garner and Mr. Smiley took pat in the meeting. The Secretary welcomed two new inembers,making a total of 444. A vote of sympathy was passed to James Tanner and his father and family in tho death of their dear mother. Dr. Agar Beet on " The Bible."—lnteresting</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Lecture at Bewsey-road.</head>
        <p>Lecture at Bewsey-road.</p>
        <p>' The Rev. Dr. Agar Beet, the well known theologian who formerly worked in the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit, leotured at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Monday evening on "The Bible: its place in the King- dom of God." There was a fair attendance, Alderman A. Bennett presiding. Amongst those present were the Revs. Sidney Pitt, J. Manning, W. Mellor and J. F. Matthews.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN observed that the lecture was being given in aid of the new Wesleyan Church at Stockton Heath—an object which merited their most generous sympathy and support. He was particularly proud to preside on account of the presence of his old friend the lecturer. He had pleasant memories of many of their old Warrington ministers, but if there was One he remembered with particular pleasure it was Dr. Beet. One was able to associate with Warrington in some way or another quite a number of men of the greatest ability and reputation,and he did not think he would be strain- ing matters if he were to place Dr. Beet in the gathering of Warrington worthies. One of the best known of Dr. Beet's works was written in the " good old town." Alderman Bennett proceeded to add his tribute to Dr. Beet for the flood of light he had thrown on those "last things" which he mi :.ht perhaps say were of the last importance. To him Dr. Beet's teaching had been as a ray of light in whet appeared to be the darkest places of the Scriptures. He had never been quite able to accept the old rigid, orthodox teaching with reference to the last things and when Dr. Beet was bold enough to enunciate the ideas which found expression in his hook and to sacrifice his position for the truth as he taw it, he established a greater claim on the gratitude of those who desired to seek the truth. It was almost impossible, continued Alderman Bennett, to conceive of civilization without the inspiration and the broad noble moral teaching we got from that fountain of all good— the Bible. He was inclined to think that if the Bible came on us suddenly and we saw it now for the first time, no one could read it calmly and dispassionately without coming to the csinclueiou that the Bible was divine. Applause.) The Rev. Dr. BesT said it was needless for him to spend any time in speaking about the great place the Bible had taken in human life, its vast circula- tion, its translation into so many languages, its influence upon the best people who had lived. He wanted to try , and explain the secret of all that—what the Bible was and what was its place in the Kingdom of God. The Bible contained two libraries of books, and they occu- pied very different positions in relation to the Ringdom of God. The New Testament was an account of one supreme personality, while tho old Testament was an account of many centuries of great personalities, one ' succeeding the other. However much they loved the Old Testament, the New Testement was infinitely dearer. There was nothing in the Old Testament quite like the old story of Jesus and His lore. Then again, the New Testa- ment was a great deal nearer to our own time. A great deal of the trouble that hid coma over the religious life of this country had beers about the 01(1 Testament. No one had a right to go to the Old Testament unless he began with the New. At first sight it seemed a long cry from the empty grave of Christ to the writing of the Pentateach, but the connecti on was very simple. Dr. Beet then referred in detail to the books of the New Testament, first dealing with the Epistles of Paul. As to the question of the genuine- ness of the letters' he pointed out that we knew ten times more about Paul than about any other of the supposed writers of any part of the New Testament or of the Old. Dealing with the authorship of the Epistle to the Romans he said that on reading it, one felt that that the writer was a man of immense intellectual power and the highest moral character. He (the lecturer) must say that in the Paul of the Acts of the Apostles he found a writer worthy of the letter, and the letter was one worthy of Paul. Oa going to the literature of the early Christian Church he found that from the days of the Apostles to this day all writers—Christians and Non- Christians—had accepted the Espistle to the Romans and some other letters attributed to Paul with complete confidence, without a shadow of doubters being actually written by the great Apostle. In the middle of the second century, within a huadred years of the death of Paul, everyone,friends and enonaies,accepted the thirteen epistles as the writings of Paul. Practically all scholars admitted that at any rate four of the Epistles—Romans, Ist and 2nd. Corinthians and Galatians—were written by Paul ; si Oa it in those letters they had a common platform on which to meet opponents. They bridged over the eighteen centuries that separated us from the great Apostle and as we read them we looked into the very heart of Paul and had reflected there the image of Jesus Christ. All the early Christian writers quoted the four Gospels as having been written by Matthew, Mark, Luke and John, and there- fore in some sense or other they came from those Apostles or else we had lost altogether the names of their authors. Every careful student loved the fourth Gospel and his own experience was that the more he read and studied it the more he loved it. First of all he was struck with the careful accuracy of its narra- tives, and this was particularly instanced in the matter of chronology. The fourth Gospel bore marks of an eye witness in a fashion that none of the others did ' and it also gave ia different type of teachine. The unanimous tradition of the ancient Church was that it was written by John the son of Zebedee," the beloved disciple." Dr. Beet alluded to the question of the I authorship of the Book of Revelation which he said he was afraid would have to remain an op m one. That, however, mattered little as the book could take care of itself. No sane man would, he thought, deny that it was written by a man called John but there was very strong reason for thinking that it was not the work of 1 the author of the fourth Gospel. Throughout the New 1 Testament they noticed the lowly homage paid to Jesus Christ as King of Kings and Lord of Lords. The New Testament wee complete documentary evidence that all the earlier followers of Christ bowed to Hine as the Son of God, as the corning Judge of the world, as being infinitely greater than the greatest of men. It was the documentary evidence on which rested our Faith in Christ, and our hope of eternal life, lie did not, how- ever, want them to put the New Testament too high. If they put it on a level with the personal Word of God they were patine it too high. The greet gift of God to man was not the New Testament but the gift of His only begotten Son to die for men. It was Christ and His death on the Cross that was the supreme proof of the infinite love of God, but the New Testament was the divine record of the revelation sf salvation and eternal life. With regard to the Old Testament, there was is great deal in it which they could not accept, and which was beneath them morally. There were, for instance, things in the Psalms which were used in some churches which were utterly unfit to be used in Christian worship. The Old Testament, with all its imperfections, was a correct picture of an imperfect spiritual life. it was the more correct because it contained the blemishes. The Old and New Testaments each had a record of historic revelations from God to men. The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman and the Lecturer, and said the chief charm of the lecture was that it was the result of Dr. Beret's personal research. The Rev. J. MANNING seconded, and said all the churches owed a debt to Dr. Beet for his long and brilliant services in defence of the Christian faith. The Rev, S. PITT supported, and expressed the oblige- gations of the congregation to Mr. .1. Dodds for the , . . part he had taken in arranging for Dr. Beets visit. The vote was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DIZZINESS AND PAIN</head>
        <p>DIZZINESS AND PAIN</p>
        <p>CURED BY VEND'S SEAWEED TONIC. CHARLES SQUIRES, Esq., 71, Park-street, Rother- ham writes :—" I urn very pleased to write you respect- ing my wife's health. Four months ago she was troubled very much with pain in the back and dizziness in the bead. I induced her to take VEso's bENNVEND TONIC which she did, and when she had taken three bottles, she was a different woman altogether ; in fact, after the third dose, the pain in the back had gone. She has told many neighbours about it." VENO'S SEAWEED Tpilc is guaranteed to permanently cure all ailments arising from a diseased condition of the stomach, liver, kidneys and blood, such as indiges- tion. wind, sluggish liver, weak and painful back, dizzi- ness, headache, kidney troubles, dropsy and female weakness, and is especially good for chronic constipa- tion and debility. Price la. Lid s. Bd., at Chemists L everywhere.</p>
