      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>"DANGEROUS ILLUSION."</head>
        <p>A "DANGEROUS ILLUSION."</p>
        <p>[By Mr. H. Whitham.]</p>
        <p>In my last letter I tried to draw attention to a state of things that is simply deplorable. While tlke poets sing of the "Brotherhood of Man," in reality we seem to be going further away from it. It is quite true that more money is now given away in charity than ever before in the world's history, that buildings are going up in every direction intended for the relief of the poor and ailing, and yet we the workers not only think but our thoughts are confirmed by notable writers on all hands, that all this charity is but a sop intended to keep us quiet. Yes, the cultured, the well-to-do, only care to keep the great seething erowd quiet. Labour has long been asking" Where do we come in ? " And now it has forced itself in it will stay in and bring more of its own kind in. A new party has been born, and it will try to solve 'the condition of the people problem." It is a party whose appear- ance speaks of more than a mere rejection of Protection. Labour men are beginning to see that Protection means murderous enmity among the wotld's workers, and the continued building of Dreadnoughts. They are learning that the continued private owner- ship of the land that we must tread upon, if we are to live at all, means the workers going back to the days of Garth the swineherd and to the wearing of the collar. In the United States the labour organizations have, through their leader, Samuel Gempeza, stated in very plain terms that their cry for more aud more will be continued until the earth and the fulness thereof is made to minister to the comfort and well-being of the whole of the people. In Russia the intellectuals have taught the people a lessen,and the Czar with his" bloody Sunday. "order has taught them another, and neither will be forgotten. In Germany the Socialists find their immense vote at the ballot box counts as nothing when placed beside the rich man's purse. Their eyes being opened, they are taking steps to alter this. In Belgium, the same state of things existing, a remedy Is to be found. In France the Socialists are a power making for good, and the , biggest practical Socialist body an the world, the London County Council, are doing good work in drawing France and the world's capitals into one complete fraternal organization. A dream this, is it not? "Nothing practical about it," I hear someone say. There never was anything put before the world but what was first the dream of an idealist. What is the better portion of the New Testament but the dream of an Idealist ?—A dream that the agnostic invites the Christian to make practical. There are signs of a movement being made, not only in the brawn of the world but in the brain. When a countess descends from her lofty pedestal to talk the landless into Radicalism our hope grows with her every action. Writers also are doing great work. The press simply teams with information. A power- ful and eloquent indictment of society at large which has just been issued by the Macmillan Company: " The bitter cry of the children " conveys information that will certainly stir every readers blood. The writer Mr. J. Spargs is a well known Anglo-American settle- ment worker. Dealing with child labour in America he says : It is not my purpose to deal with all the forms of child labour—the 25,000 or 30,000 employed in e.nd around the mines or quarries of this country, or, to extend the example, the 12,000 or 15,000 tobacco and cigar makers, and SO on down the glooruy vists. In New Jersey and Pennsylvania I have seen hundreds of children between ten and twelve years old work- ing in factories. Whether the children of to-day become physical, mental and moral cretins, or strong men and women fathers and mothers of virile sons and daughters, depends upon the decision of the nation. If the responsibility of this is fully recognized, and employment of children under fifteen years of age is forbidden throughout the land ; if the nation realizes that the demand for the protection of children is the highest form of patriotism, then and not till then will it be possible to look with confi- dence toward the future, unashamed and unafraid. Look ahead of this wretbhed statement, and we shall find that those who grow rich out of it all are , those who are filling your great West end mansions, I and are paving fabulous rents for your Scotch moors. • The following from the London correspondent to the " Chicago Tribune " gives us pretty fairly the attitude of London society ,—" We should all like to see the masses happy and prosperous, and all that sort of thing. We don't like to see the hungry men marching through the West End. We invite some of them there are too many to invite all —to our back doors and g've them hot coffee and sandwiches, and we con- tribute to the Mansion House fund. And we wish and wish hard, that somebody would do something for them that would provide them all with work and good wages." Men, we neither want their hot coffee nor yet their fund. We want conditions so altering that the environments of every child born into this world shall be conducive to its mental, moral and physical elevation. It has recently been proved by the foremost authorities that the proportion of children born healthy and strong is not greater among the rich than among the poor. The differences appear after birth. It is calculated that there are 95,000 unnecessary infantile deaths in the United States in a single year. Think of this and then join in the general howl that is being made about race suicide. During the past winter the unemployed of London thought it wise to wait upon Mr. Balfour, the late Premier. That highly philosophical gentle- man, in answer to the poor fellowa' argu- ments, advised that the people should get rid of any " dangerous illfleion " they might have touching the land. Well, at all events, the dispossessed must clearly see that those who hold the land and say it is theirs, as they would say the air we breathe was theirs if they could only gather it in,, will lie quite willing to wade through a blo)dy revelation before they will let go. As things are at present in England ne tax is paid on unoceupted land, and while the land-olvner does nothing to enhance the value of aai7ll7rsd'sanpocket by those who have " unearned increment " tphreeplaanidn,gtlioe s lnrrEithie no interest in the land. This Government is pledged to the . taxation of land values. As Henry George used to say, "Do you see the cat ? " One of the prin- ciples of taxing values is, it will be a remedy for the evils of over-crowding by bringing a lot of cheap land into the market. If owners were taxed for bare ground just the some as if it had buildings on it, that is taxed 1 at its selling value, they could not afford, to hold it I without improving it. Well that's one way of avoid- the bloody path, and when the people grasp its full meaning, no doubt a few more John Burns will get into the Cabinet and then things will hum. But now, while the grass is growing the horse may starve.A dead i sure thing s that the unemployed question will, year ' by year, increase in volume. Discovery and invention will surely increase the number of the out-of-work. Labour saving machines will increase in number and keep on pulling more men out of work and increase the physical demands made on those who remain at work, besides compelling the employers to discharge men over forty as not tieing able to stand the strain in the fight for the right to live. Let us leave all this misery for awhile and while we are skipping over to Canada, I wish to say that I am under no delusion about the denizens of the slums or the unemployed being fit ueople for sending to Canada. I know the Canadians do not want the refuse of the British cities. As President of the Board of Agricul- ture, Lord Carrington recently gave an experience ! that proves that there are thousands of Britishers who Icould and would make a splendid livelihood out of the land if they only had the chance. These thousands love the land. Then why not build up a great national movement and send these men where there is plenty of land and where they will be made welcome,instead of forcing them into the slums. I have a goodly list here of people who are spending a. deal of money in this work, but it is not enough. The Rev. W. J. Dawson an English minister who is over here trying to convert "the most Christian nation on I earth " to Christianity, travelling by the Canadian Pacific Railway referring to Canada says :—" Do English people really understand what kind of a possession they- have in this vast Dominion of Canada ?" I have asked myself this I question several times during recent years. A group forms in a party and gets hold of a " cry," This cry grows until it is adopted by the rank and file of the party, and so the red herring grows until by a strange feat in development it becomes the great serpent with about as much that is material about it. It may be the c 7 is " Protection." It may be " Big i England ' s against the Little Englander, or it may be the fatuous cry of the " Unionist " against Home; Rule. It all amounts to the same thing ! The British Empire, as a whole, is lost in the self glorification of ; the Big Englander.. Canada is, I suppose, a part of , the British Empire. I wonder how many of the I , readers of this paper know anything about the country? I have for some time been gathering a few itemsl about the land and the question the Rev. W. Dawson 1 11 puts to himself I will try to answer, first merely saying</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>that the informatioa I give is largely the result of Mr. Frank Carpenter's travels. If, daring recent years, we had given the question a thought at all we might have wondered how it was that Canada made such little progress, made such little noise in the world, while the United States, her big noisy sister, was claiming to be the whole thing. The two were born about the same time, but Canada must have been born tired, and has, uatil now, been sleeping. It is an undeveloped country, its resources almost unknown and in a great part unexplored. John Bull has, on the American continent, a much larger possession than Uncle Sam. He has been the land grabber of the universe, and his properties in Canada are bigger than anywhere else in the world. Canada is twice the size of India, it is bigger than Australia, and it is thirty times as big as Great Britain and Ireland. It contains one- i third of the land of the whole British Empire. Indeed it is a continent in itself, for it is almost as large aso Eu pe, and bigger than the United States, including Al ska, Porto Rico, Hawaii, Samoa, and the Phillip- ' pines all put together. Every thing in Canada is big. Scores of the names of places we have never heard tell : of before,and the names are big ones too If a map is ' at the reader's touch he may fancy he is almost at the : North Pole. Have you ever heard of Ungana ? I ask because to most of my readers it must seem, as to my- self, strange that the British Possessions should run My Dear Children, so far away North. Unglue lies between Labrador and : I expect you have all commenced your Easter Eludson Bay, north of. the province of Quebec, and it Holidays by now, and I propose giving a prize to is bigger than Texas, Kansas, and New Jersey . the boy and girl wlin send me the best account of combined. Keewatin, north of Ontario, has 470,000 -what they do during these holitla,s. I hope you square miles, enough to make ten States the size of will all try and win the prize, but good writing, and neatness will be taken into consideration. I New York, while Franklin above with its great ,whale' fishing grounds is larger still. The country is known hope that you will all spend a little time attend- as New Canada, or the New Northlands. We might ing to your flower gardens, you will tind that the make a number of comparisons, but let it be taken for young plants will want attention, and perhaps will require watering. I shall he pleased to hear granted that everything in Canada is big and rich, from any of you how the seeds are doing, as I , Saskatchewan and Alberta are empires in themselves. take great interest in this competition. I am Each of them has an area something like that of sending prizes this week to Faith Aie.vett for her France or Germany, and parts of them will raise more original piece of poetry, and also to Elsie Lewis , wheat than either of those countries. These provinces fcr the interesting ster:,7 she has sent. I have a are being rapidly colonized by American farmers, and great number ot contributions to be printed in undoubtedly an American Canada is growing up here. time, but as I said before, you must all wait your British Columbia is another empire through which rm. I hope we shall have nice bright weather new railroads are going. The mountains here ::ir the holidays, we certatilly had a very cold are rich in gold, copper and qther minerals. March,so we may hope for a little sunshine now. The story of Canada is just that of g British ColenY will say goud-bye tor this week, and wishing and to-day as a mere dependence it is just drift- you all very pleasant holidays. ing out of the hands of the Mother Country into the Front your affectionate hands of the American imigrants, of whom 100,000 UNCLE NED have within the past two years settled there. As Mr. Dawson says :—" Why spend a vast sum of money to settle 200 families on the land in England when for the same expenditure 1,900 families could be settled in Canada? " It exceeds the area of the United States by a quarter of a million square miles and its population is less than that of London. Here ia a chance to sea what Imperialism is worth. More than this, now is the time to take a long look into the future. The American continent is the future home of the English speaking races. I may continue these letters from Canada. San Francisco, March 1906. Dear Uncle Ned,</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,</p>
        <p>Can I please become one of your members? I should like to, very much. I have a lovely white. rabbit, and I call him "Snowflake," and I have two cats named Roby and Peter, and they will do anything I tell them. Dear Uncle Ned, I will try and get you a list of members. I think I must now close, with love, hoping to see my letter in print. I remain, Your loving niece, MAY SHAW.</p>
        <p>H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>- : -.- : --</p>
        <p>I hope you are quite well. This is the first letter I have 'written to the Children's Corner, and I hope it will do. It was my birthday not long ag,i, and I enjoyed it very much. I always like to read the tides in the Children's Corner every Saturday. I think I shall like very much to join and write letters to it every week as well. . With love from CHARLES BOTT. Dear Uncle Ned, . . . .</p>
        <p>I have only just joined the Corner, and am already taking an interest in it. My story lam sending you is called</p>
        <p>DARE TO DO RIGHT.</p>
        <p>A group of boys stood on the walk of a large drug shop, pelting one another with snowballs. In a unlucky moment the youngest sent a ball, spinning through the frosty air against the drug- gist's window. The crash terrified them all, but none of them so much as the little fellow who now stood pale and trembling with staring eyes, looking at the mischief he had done. , "Won't old Ross be wild! Run Ned, we won't tell. He wouldn't catch me !" "Little fool! He'll be caught, not pluck to run away. Well, I've done all I can for him," muttered the elder boy. The door opened, and an angry face appeared. "Who did this?" came in fierce tones from the owner's lips. " Who did it, I say?" No one answered. The trembling 'boy drew near, he faced the angry man, and in tones of truth replied. "I did it, sir." "And you dare to tell rue of it?" he said, as he caught Ned by the collar. "I dare not deny it, sir, I dare not tell'a lie I". The reply Was unexpected. The stern man paused ; he saw the pale cheek, the frightened eyes in which the soul of truth and courage shone,. and his heart was touched. "Come here, sir! What is your name?" Ed- ward Howe, sir. Oh What can Ido to pay you? I'll do anything "—and his eyes filled with tears—" only don't make my mother pay it, sir." "Will.you shovel my walk when the next snow falls?" Ned's face was bright, as he answered; "All winter, sir, I'll do it evervtirne, and more too, sir, I'll do anything." " Well, that's enough ; and do you know why I let you ofc so easy Well, it is because you are notafraid to tell the truth, I like a boy that tells the truth \always, When the snow foils, be sure you come to me." "I will sir." " all help him," shouted the others, and, as they turned away, three cheers were given for Mr. Rose. and three more for the boy who dare not run away. Your loving niece,</p>
        <p>• NELLiE HALSTEAD. P.S.-I am very glad to hear that your birth- ,1 day is on the same day as my own.</p>
        <p>-1-0- : -</p>
        <p>THE COLONEL'S LAST COMMAND. ".Shoulder to shoulder, my boys I" he cried, And fight like the men you are. We'll give them a lesson, we will, my lads, Those cads on the hills afar." Then gallantly dashed the colonel's men— For they were the ninety-second— The brave old Gordon's, side by side, As they fought for Britain's name. And hark ! the guns ot the enemy Are already starting to play ; And the war-horse glowing impatiently„ Is accompanying with its neigh. But the fiery blood in the Gordon - Is rising with his steed's, And he draws his sword and carbine To serve his country's need.</p>
        <p>tillery have opened fire, ,nd thicker grows the fight; hey see their colours kvaving higher, Amid the waning light. Again the colonels voice is heard My. lads, your Hies dearly sell For we are 'only one to tour And with these words he fell. T. -,till kept fighting br •:!1,•</p>
        <p>hey still kept fighting bravely on, Though their hearts were with him they loved. They never saw him smile again— He died just as the field was-won. (Original.)</p>
        <p>F. MEW.</p>
        <p>HOW JOAN BECAME A PRINCESS. Once upon a time there lived in France a little girl who lived with her father, who was a wood- cutter, in a little hut in the middle of a dense forest. Her name was Joan, and her mother had died when she was a baby. Joan's father treated her very cruelly because she was so beautiful. Joan had no companions to play with, so she was quite content to play with the trees and flowers. If Joan ever asked her father to take her to see the little white town over the hill she would only receive a blow for an answer.</p>
        <p>One day, when her father had gone to the town to sell some Wood, Joan went into the forest to talk to the flowers, and as she sat down upon a mossy bank, she noticed that some daflbdils had been stepped on, so she tenderly raised the heads of the flowers to life again, when suddenly a little fairy arose from one of the flowers and, turning tawards Joan, she said in a sweet, silvery voice, "Thank you, Joan, now what can I do for you?" For a moment Joan was too frightened to speak, then she replied, "Oh, kind fairy, will you please take inc away from my cruel father, and let me. see more of thgreat world." "Yes, Joan," replied the fairy, "You shall be taken away from your cruel father, and you shall see more of the great world," and alter saying these words the fairy vanished, leaving Joan looking at the daffo- dils in sheer amazetnent. Just then Joan heard her father calling her, so she returned to the but. Her father greeted her with a shower ot blows, and ordered her to get his tea.. All through the night Joan was thinking of the fairy, and wondered it her words would ever come true. Joan's father went to the town with sonic wood again the next morning. Her father had not been gone very long when she heard the sound of a bugle, and on rushing out saw a prince and his servants coming through the forest. The prince came up to Joan and asked for a drink of water. This Joan gave linn at once. The prince . • • asked Joan many questions, and then he told her that she was the most . beautiful girl he had ever seen. He then asked Joan if she would like to • tome with him to his palace. Of course, Joan readily consented to this, and bidding one (it his servants to lift her up behind him, the prince took Joan home to his palace Thus the fairy's words had come true, and Joan was taken away from her cruel father, and he saw more of the great world. The next day Joan was married to the prince, and she lived happily ever after.</p>
        <p>ELSIE LEWIS.</p>
