      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>Judgment of Court of Appeal.</p>
        <p>The Centt of Appeal on Saturday delivered judgment in the case of Crosfietd and others v. the Manchester Ship Canal Company on the application to settle the minutes of the judgment of the Court of Appeal affirm- ing the decision of Mr. Justice Byrne. The plaintiffs wished to have the judgment of Mr. Justice Byrne as it stood. The defendants on the other band, wanted to get certain dicta of their Lordships embodied in the judgment of the Court. This dicta, the plaintiffs alleged, would cause difficulty to the official referee in the inquiry as to damages. Lori Justice Cozens-Hardy said the first part of the order would stand,strikimr" out the words of the declara- tion made by Mr. Justice Byrne. Then the order would proceed as follows :—Between the date of the agree- ment of April 9,189 i, and January 1, 1.897, the original obligations of the defendant Company to dredge the channel of the Mersey at Warrington to a certain depth were duly fulfilled by the defendants. ['his Court declares that according to the true construction of the Act and the agreement the obligation of the Canal Company so long as there shall be any permanent alteration in the level of low water of spring tide at Warrington Bridge is to maintain the same channel dredged which was originally dredged. The Court declares further that the obligation of the Company to maintain the channel dredged is not an obligation that the channel should at all times he free of obstruction, but an obligation to maintain the channel dredged by removing obstructions as and when they arise within a reasonable time, and the Court,. being of opinion that the level of low water of spring tides at Warrington Bridge was not permanently altered between the time when the channel was originally dredged and the date of the judg- ment, says that the defendants have not at all times, since August 16th, 1899, maintaned the channel dredged in accordance with the declaration therein contained, and that navigation has been obstructed, and that the defendants have not in , all cases immediately on the receipt of written notice of the surveyor of the Corporation certifying the fact of obstruction commenced to dredge and continued to dredge the channel. The Judge added that there would be an order that the foregoing variations of the judg- ment of Mr. Justice Byrne should be atBrmed. Mr. Cripps, K.C., stated that it had been agreed that the costs were to follow the order of the House of Lords.</p>
      </div>
