      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>THRIFT.</head>
        <p>THRIFT.</p>
        <p>Two boys once went to the same school. One was the son of a rich man, and the other was the son of p poor man. The rich boy's name was Arthur, and the name of the poor boy was Alfred. Arthur xvas dull. while Alfred was industrious. It soon became time for the two boys to leave school. ' Alfre,l soon obtained a situation, for he had been highly recommended by the head- master. He became richer and richer, for be saved all the money that was not needed for the necessaries.</p>
        <p>With Arthur it was different, he spent all his money. Ile then tried to find work but did not succeed. lle began to despair, for he had. not a penny in the whole:World. One day, as Alfred was riding down one of the streets of London, he happened to see and recognise Arthur. He was beginning t., talk to him when he saw how wane and ill Arthur limited. When he got borne I, learnt that Arthur had not been thrifty. Alfred told him that it he had not been such a spend- thrift, he would not have been in such a state of poYerte. • Aithur had itow fully repented, and, from that day, to the day of his death, he was never known to be stricken'with poverty again. DAISY A. STANLEY.</p>
