      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Cycle Campers.</head>
        <p>The Cycle Campers.</p>
        <p>During the coming Easter holidays the Cycle Campers will again pitch their camp at Cherry Tree Farm. Ley Hill,•a rustle 'spot near Cheshani. ritanding-high and dry; with hedges affording good shelter from the wind. This society of enthusiasts ;lex, numbers over 200 cyclists of -both sexes, and tlas lately founded a journal of its own. Periodically, nleet logs called "Camp Fires" are held, when the kactical postibilities of the campers' tent equip- 'Dent and cuisine are demonstrated to the sceptical or curious. On a somewhat wider scale is the (iisplay of camp kits and requisites to be given at the Crystal Palace on Saturday, April 21, and certain subsequent dates. To Mr. T. H. Holding, the Cycle Campers' President, belongs the credit "e evolving the light and compact equipment, capable of being carried on a bicycle.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Lane Riding.</head>
        <p>Lane Riding.</p>
        <p>Those riders who have grown over-familiar with the main reads in their particular districts will Ind it a refreshing change to give a little study to the exploration .of the lanes. As soon as dust takes the place of mud upon the highways, the lanes afford an attractive alternative' to the monotony of the more direct main routes. They frequently pass through the more secluded beauties of a district, and the hedgerows are abloom with flowers seldom seep beside the more frequented ways. Even within six miles of the heart of the great metropolis, there are spots where the over- arching foliage.Lnd the music of the birds transport the cyclist into fair-distant country. Now that in tearlv every district the electric tramway is throwing out its tentacles, it is culy in the lanes that the cyclist can enjoy nature undisturbed, vbile yet within easy reach of home.</p>
