        <p>,o` t*.k% 14330"4104. 5,"1,07K, , tr 604. - .</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S GAMES. \ 13. T. P. Or? *Wigan 2--5 19 Runcorn -3 13 *Harrow -6 24 Salford -0 0 Oldham ' 0-3 9 Bradford -1 3 Leigh O-3 9 *Hull K R. o—i 3 *Halifax -4 18 Rochdale H O-0 0 *Warrington -2 8 Hull -2 8 *Widnes -6 22 Brighouse R. ... 0-0 0 *Dewsbury ~ 3-4 18 Broughtdu R. ... 1-1 5 *Normenton -3 9 Morecambe -1 5 *Leeds 2-8 28 Brantley -0 2 *Hunslet -5 31 Huddersfield ... 0-1 3 "Swinton O-2 6 York l-1 5 'Pontefract -9 10 Wakefield T O-1 5 *Batley -7 33 Castleford O-0 0 Played on ground of club marked (*)</p>
        <p>Warringtqn v. Hull—A Brilliant Gains The match with Hull is generally looked 4on as one of the chief attractions of the football season at War- rington, for of recent years both teams have given a fine display whenever they have met. Saturdays match was no exception to the rule and the exhibition was generally voted first class. Beth teams were strongly represented, and summer-like weather prevailed. The crowd numbered about 5,000. Teams and officials :-- Warrington—Knight, back ; Fish, Isherwood, Dickin- son, and Taylor. three-quarter backs ; Hockenhall and Lees half backs ; Preston, Boardman, Thomas, Jolley, Swift, Harmer, Heath, and Shugars forwards. Hull—Taylor, back ; Freear, Pullen, Cook, and Parry, three-quarter Lacks ; Goddard and Harebell. half backs; Hargreaves, Harrison, Carve), Land, Kilburn, Rhodes, Lewis, and Carroll, forwards. Referee, Mr. A. Smith, Halifax. Touch judges,Messrs. j. Edwards, St. Helens, and J. Davies, Flixton• Hull were early compelled to defend. From the first pack Ilockenhall passed to Taylor, and the wing man crossing to the open, the home foxwards rushed over and Thomas scored. Hull next threatened danger through the medium of Bluebell and Cook, and only the, superb defence of Fish and Isherwood saved the situa- tion. Twice in quick succession Parry accounted for Fish in brilliant style, and then superior passing again gave Bull the advantage, a series of transfers between Cook, Goddard, end Freear being checked by Dickinson and Fish. Hockenhall next effected a lice run, but found the Hull cnstodian safe. At half-time Warring- ton led by I try (3 points) to nil. Turning round, Warrington forced the pace, but the Hull custodian played a brilliant game. Eventually Hull were penalised for fouling Knight,and Fish placed goal. Another Another success followed soon after, Taylor putting on the finishing touch to a round of passing initiated by Lees. Preston failed at goal. Hull rallied splendidly in the last stages' and really magnificent work by the hall backs, Burcbell sud Clocidard, gave the rear line plenty of opportunities. Cook scored an exceptionally fine try, after running from half-way, whilst smart following up led to Carvel scoring. Both attempts ut goal were failures. Warrington returned to the attack with spirit, and Heath deserved a try when he grounded under the posts, but the pass from Harmer was judged forward. Isherwood and Lees also made fine efforts but were pulled down a yard from the line. Final : Warrington, one goal, two tries (8 points) ; Hull, two tries (6 points).</p>
