      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>FIVE THOUSAND ON 'IJiF ROAD,</head>
        <p>FIVE THOUSAND ON 'IJiF ROAD,</p>
        <p>W .11. OGILVIE. (Author of" Rua and Bones," 4c.).</p>
        <p>A faint tinge of grey mantles over the gum- trees to the east of the camp, and the drover in charge flings aside his blanket • and leaps to his feet. The cook is already busy with the fire, and four heaps of blankets testify to the sleep- ing presence of the drover's men. " Hulloh ! T 4" roars the drover in a voice that sweeps through the river timber, mid dies in the ranges —" After those horses—quick and lively now !" A boy of fifteen rolls Out of his blankets. rubbing his eyes, and mechanically takes the bridles off the cart and stumbles sleepily to- wards the bend of the river where the jingle of the horse bells conies up, on the breeze of the dawn. The three shepherds get up and roll . . . . .</p>
        <p>their blankets into bundles ready to. be packed , in the cart ; then they take their towels and go '' down to the river for a wash. The drover walks over to the low brush-fence that stir- rounds the sheep, and with a hasty glance round assures himself that the correct number-- , approsimately--is there. and that none have been crushed or smothered during the night! Then he performs his ablutions in a bucket, 'offers up a mental prayer for a good day's grass. and goes over to see what the cook hes got for breakfast. The -cook soon has his chops frying in the tamp-oven. and a laiast of water boil- ing for the tem and the day's first meal is taken. ;1s they drink fbi scalding hot tea, and eat the chops upon tin plates held on their knees. the boy comes up with the horses, ridinq one and driving the others n front of him. He then has breakfast %%Aide ILc drover and hiS nueiu catch and saddie their leases. Then the sheep are let out of the " break." These breaks are</p>
        <p>large yards or enclosures, built with boughs and logs, U ,silitable distances along the tnivel- "hug stock rods ; and a drover nrr i-,os itnhi day's journey so as to bare his clung :ate'tme or them, A passible. Then the sheep, being rectire- ly enclosed, are safe till -morning. and require no watching ; this watching at stook at night, which mast be done when there are " no breaks," being 71 weariness of the to the drover and his tired shepherchs, LEAVING, TIIIO - BRYAN.</p>
        <p>There ale tire thonsand sheep in the break. and they ripnie away Irian the fence like blown wavelets as the men step near. The drover lets out rather iii re than half of • them, standing himself at the gateway to prevent . crowding they rush through, and sends them away ut charge of his two most reliable men. Then lie closes the gateway ; there is no gate, so I soy the gateway advisedly. This is done by drag- ging the top of it pine tree or a few law: sap. hugs across the opening. The flo,k set free spreads rapidly over the plain, the leaders run- ning, the last ones stepping slowly on their ludicrous stilt-like legs. Soon the leaders are steadied by the dogs, and the mob draws rapidly away. There is fence on the right hand skle and the windi»g river on the left; so that droving at this parti- cular place is easy, mid-, one of the men rides slowly ahead, the other follows as slowly be- hind. When this first mob has got away some distance the remainder of the sheep are let out, and banded over to the other shepherd end tie boy. Tlien the drover gives directions to‘the cook as to where lie shall stop for a dinner- tamp, takes :mother drink from the tea-bucket, and rides away at the sheep. lly thiX tune the son is rising, a red ball of fire, over the</p>
        <p>nip:ll ridge ; anal, PVerythmg points to nothet scorching ' day. l'he cook i 1 i 1 breaklaSt, washes np his tin plates and pints (tin mos \ which hold a pint). rolls tip the drover's 'blankets and his own. 0 id, with ,the p 0 is them in the cart : packs tip .( he camp ware ; harnesses the horse into the springeart. sod starts oft utter the moving. •-cionds of dtot which betoken the flocks in front. THR 1,21:5T BALT.</p>
        <p>About three or four mites will form the stage for the morning, and nt that distance, the cart will be drawn up at the first nitable place pro- mising botli water nod lirewood. The horse is taken out, bobbled. and let go to feed and have a roll. The tire is lit, the bucket ,boiled and a cold lunch prepared for the men. The men round up their sheep, still in" two distinct mobs, in the shadow of the nearest trees; and. in each ease, one man watches them while the other gees over to the cairn' and bas his dinner. The. drover .who has probably during the morn- ing ridden on ahead to. see what the grass is like, and to pick it camp for The coining night, now returns and has his dinner. Sometime-s the shepherd's berses are bobbled enc. to teed et dinner-time, and the saddles taken off them ; other times they are merely allowed to pick about trailing their bridles or are lied up in the shade of the trees. The length of the dinner- camp varies With the season of the year, the amount of feed found in the morning or ex- pected in the afternoon, the condition of the sheep, and so oil. When the weather, is very hot a mid-day camp will begin at o'clock and last till 4 o'clock, but ill the cool winter weather a couple of hours is sullicient rest for the sheep. These variations are only in accord with the habits of sheep when left to themselves in a station paddock. In the efternoon, if the sheep have had a barren morning and grass is scarce until close to the even i»g caii up. they are al- lowed to travel over the bare country at a good pace, and then they liare plenty of time to get a feed on the good pasture before being shut up for the night.. Thus will be seen the advantage ot the drover going ahead to ascertain exitaly whew the grass, if any, is. TILE HUNGRY ANIMALS' ASUSTI. Slowly they move off the dinner-camp ; they have had very little_ grass during the morning, and the leaders are unreasonable and the strag- glers sulky, and the sore footed dogs are kept busy continually running round the spreading wings " or verges of the mobs, to keep them within bounds ; for here the country is open, without a fence, and sheep, if not constantly checked, and turned in upon each other, will soon spread ever three or four miles of country. After travelling over a mile or so of Innen dusty plain, the leading mob crosses a ridge. and comes out into a clearing about hill a mile across, on which is a fresh shoot of short green grass. The hungry animal's rush forward at sight of it, and soon they have crossed the whole space like a fleecy white cloud. They have no desire to spread now, and all fall to work steadi- ly feeding, and the chop-chop 'of their busy teeth is music to the drover and his men. At first the sheep rum from bunch to bunch, on the' principle, no doubt, that the furthest fruit is the sweetest, but soon they settle down and scarcely leave the one spot till they have filled themselves. ‘l4radually the hollow flanks are ;died up. and one would scarcely credit the dif- ference that half an limit's feeding makes to the look of a sheep which has fasted for nearly went' hours. The men sit idly on their horses watching them, or stretch themselves under it convenient_ tree, keeping an eye upon their charge. however. The tired dogs lie gasping ill the shade, but ready at a word from their mas- ters to spring up and turn in the stragglers if they wander beyond prescribed limits. After loaf an hour or so, if there is grass further on, the leading mob will pass on, and the second flock will be steadied up on the same ground. Hut of all feeding grounds the drover loves best the one which is near his night's camp, for then lie ean let his sheep int themselves at the last moment. so that thl.yr go on to camp comfort- able mid satisfied, and better equipped to with- -11 cold or wet night, and possibly another hungry 11:Cfruitig. • . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TYPICAL CAMPING onorNii.</head>
        <p>A TYPICAL CAMPING onorNii.</p>
        <p>To-night their camp is in a corner of a large paddock, upon the boundary fence of a new station. The grass in its vicinity is good short feed for sheep, but of little use for horses, so the drover gives orders for them to be put throligh the gate on to the next station, where the tall bluegrass waves among the bushes. This of course is, morally speaking, thieving grass, but the drover who will not steal grass for his horses is looked upon with contempt, nsul indeed will have difit•itity in proceeding far on his way, for the legli imate stock-roaa is of course eaten bare by the many thousands con- tinually pat:sing along it. 'the road allowed is—in open coon try--.--half mile in extent ; but this in many places is fenced as narrow as a couple of chains, forming the dreaded ' lanes " of the drover, in which there is never grass or water, and 80:11C of which extend for unincy miles at a stretch. The men respond gladly 16 the cook's 811111- 111011 s to slipper, and the drover, who has left the saddle on his horse, joinsnthem at the meal. Then he mounts his hungry steed mite more, and rides off to the station ahead, live miles away, to " report," or give notice of his coming on the run to the station manager. After a long canter be reaches the white route of the home- stead, glittering in the moonlight. He rides up to the garden fence, and calls, " Anyone in " Dulloh I" Comes back from the vine-Azalea verandah. "Is the boss there ?" A tall inoi walks across the garden. " I have come," says the drover, "to let you know I will be on your rim at daylight to- morrow morning with live thol"i'd W"Ulera from Alerryhorra travelling to Goal born."</p>
        <p>" Who is in charge c• " T nin—Dtmenn McLetql." " All right ! f wiU ,end up it mail in the morning to clear the station flieep out of your way. I;y the way, you can put your horses in that horse paddocic of mine to-night, close to your camp.' Thtutk you," says the drover, chuckling softly to himself, for he knows that his nags are already up to their knees in the long hish station grass. " Good-night " " (;tsit!.-night !'' end the 'drover turns his bor,e, 21 lld Call tits Biding briskly back to camp, he doer, not tail to take notice of the main featiires of the nauj which he yjjl have to traven;e in the rooming ; marking in his mind where the hest grass is, which side of is ridge to travel, where to Mid water for his dinner-camp, and otber &amp;lane. Imidentally .he observes that the feed is good all the way tilimg, and he knows 60,, if the boundary rider sent out in the morn- ing is not too onki,„,„, he will be able to move his sheep slowly, and give them the opportunity Of it good graze. duty of tin stntion man. however, is to keep the drover within the, im- aginary that marks the half mile road, MP.: to lire. bun travelling rorwurd at. the minimum rate of six miles a day— which is required of him by law. Between these 'classes of men there is much friction, and many a heavy-handed battle waged upon the plains between the harassed drover and the conscientious servant of the squatter. Arrived at the five mile gate the drover pulls the saddle mid bridle off his horse, takes the hobbles from the saddle and puts them on him. and lets him go with the rest. Then he carries his riding gear to the . camp, unrolls his blankets lays his head • 'upon the saddle, and lights his pipe under the stars for half mi hour's smoke before turning in. And that i the Western .drover's day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>GARDE REPUBLICAINE BAND.</head>
        <p>GARDE REPUBLICAINE BAND.</p>
        <p>In a paragrarili under this heading in our last issue we referred to the talking machine records which this celebrated band made for the Columbia Phonograph Company during their recent visit to this countrSi. By a typographical error, however, we described the Columbia machine as the" gramophone" instead of the " graphophone." Our readers will do well to note that the latter is the only correct designation of both the cylinder and disc machines of the Columbia Company. Columbia Graphophone ilecords will, however, fit any make of machine so that the Carde Republicaine elec- tions should have a very wide sale.</p>
