      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITORS!</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITORS!</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. [ONE OF TILE 4099.—Notwithstanding your note we still think with the 5599 that the winning aide was the better side at the election in 'Warrington. Does it not occur to you that complaints of bribery come with peculiar unfitness from the party you represent as a voter ?—ED. " Es."]</p>
        <p>District Nurses and the Election Sir,—The committee of the Warrington District /Cursing Association desire that you will publish the following in your paper next Saturday : "That the Superintendent having called attention to a charge made that the nurses canvassed for the Liberal Candi- date in the recent election, and having given on her own behalf and that of .he nurses an unqualified denial of the statement, the committee is satisfied with her explanation."—Yours truly, J. D. VAIRCLOUCH, Hon. Sec., W.D.N.A. Thornfield, Latchford, Warrington, April 7th 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Association Football.</head>
        <p>Association Football.</p>
        <p>- Sir,—With your permission I will reply at length to a letter which appeared in your issue of Saturday last, under the above heading and signed " Full Back:" In the first place it is to be regretted that " Full Back " had not the courage to disclose his identity and come out in the open. Let me come to the points raised in the letter, First he asks " Why is there not a Warring- ton Association Football Club ?" and states that "that question had been asked of many people on many occasions, but the answer had not been forthcoming." Here it is. Under the agreement entered into between the Northern Rugby Union and the Football Associa- tion it is laid down that in a town where the Northern Rugby Union game is in existence, no Association Club shall be allowed to use the name of the town,if used by the Rugby Club, the word "Association" after the name I of the town not being a sufficient distinction within 1 the meaning of the agreement, so that a club formed ' now could not take the name of" Warrington A.F.C." "Full back "could have satisfied himself on that point had he referred to the rules of the football association. At the time the now defunct Warrington Club played on the cricket ground at Arpley the egreenent referred to was not in existence. "Full back" then refers to the annual dinner of the District Football Association I and charges the Executive Committee with patting each other on the back, and playing the game common to such functions. To that charge I plead guilty, but ' is not" Full Back" guilty of a similar offence. The writer of the letter last week, is a player of the 1 Onghtrington Park Club, and in his letter he writes of the "excellent exposition." Is he not patting himself on the back and is he not playing the game.common to writers of anonymous letter? "Full back's" chief grievance, however seems to be that he was not invited to dinner with the Executive Committee in, preference to ont.iders. Is " Full Back " not aware ' that a levy has been made on the Executive Comtuittoel to pay for those whom he calls outsiders. Does it follow that becallse the annual dinner was held on the day of the Final that the competing teams should be invited. If the dinner had been held in June or July; as had been suggested, would his contention still apply. " Full Back" is evidently under the impression that the Executive Committee should run a Football Club, but the duties of the District Association are clearly defined by the Football Association, a few of which are to see that all Clubs are properly constituted, to deal with cases of misconduct and to govern any Cup or Medal Competition sanctioned by the parent body. I agree with Mr. Lewis, who stated that "Association Fbotball in Warrington had not made the pro- gress it ought to have done." Full Back "" asks " Who is responsible for that ?" I reply, the pnblio of Warrington. They had the opportunity of supporting the Association code in the town when a team composed of professionals and some of the finest amateurs pro- duceri in this district was in existence, but the support accorded would not even run a local team of amateurs. You may tempt the rugby supporters to witness the final of the cup on a fine day, but it is a different matter to get them to attend Association matches throughout the season. If " Full Back "wishes to form a new club in War- rington (call it say Warrington United or Warrington Central) he has the remedy in his own hands. Let him call a meeting of those interested. Get the meeting to sanction its formation. Become affiliated according to rules, apply for admission to any league or combination they wish to enter,and if he succeeds where others have failed he will have earned the thanks of all admirers of the dribbling code. I say that the Executive Committee of the District Football Association, of which I am a paid servant, have done much (I am again open to the serious charge of patting on the back) to encourage the game in the district, as but for their efforts we should not to-day have a League composed of two divisions nor should we have a Referees' Association and quite possibly no Cup Competition. After years of agitation the Execu- tive Committee received permission to deal with cases of misconduct which would otherwise have had to be dealt with at Liverpool and elsewhere. In one season alone 97 cases of misconduct were dealt with locally. What would have been the result had these cases been dealt with at Liverpool, Manchester, Chester, &amp;c. Most of the clubs could not have met the expense of send- ing their players to those towns and would have disbanded Again the formation of the Referees' Association (permission for which was at first refused) was a great saving to all clubs under the jurisdiction of the District Association, otherwise referees from outside towns would have been called in and in addition to paying them a higher fee than at present paid the travelling expenses in some cases alone would have been more than could be paid by the majority of the Clubs in the League. In conclusion, I say that the District Association does not exist for the purpose of forming Clubs but to see that clubs when formed are properly governed.— Yours truly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>T. P. BRADSHAW, Secretary. Warrington and District Foothill Association. STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>T. P. BRADSHAW, Secretary. Warrington and District Foothill Association. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Parochial Committee. The monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held et the Hall Cafe on Tuesday when there were present :—Messrs. G. Pilling in the chair), H. J. Westbrook, F. Pearson, .1. Whitley, F. Warburton, .1. Hallows, T. R. Westbrook, T. Williams, W. Mclntyre G. F. Ashton (clerk), and J. Swinton (surveyor).</p>
        <p>THE TOWN CLERK AND TREE PLANTING. The clerk produced correspondence from the Board of Trade with reference to the lighting of the dangerous tram standards in the village, a report having been pre- sented to the Board to the effect that the Corporation were failing to carry out a verbal agreement entered into with the Parochial Committee in 1902. The Board stated that they werenot able to deal with any question of undertaking an agreement between the parties. They enclosed a letter which they had received from the Town Clerk of Warrington dated the 24th March, 1900, which stated that for the Parish Council to say i that they relied upon under- takings given n 1902 was not consistent with facts. If the Corporation were to comply with the requiT ments of the Council it would mean that the main street of the village would practically be lighted at the expense of the Corporation. If the village was properly lighted the question would not have arisen. Trees had been planted in Stockton Heath, including those shown on the plan. and he thought that if the contention of the Rural District Council that the tram- way standards were dangerous was a good one,then the tree guards, which were a little less in diameter than the standards, should come under that head. These trees and guards had been planted with the consent of the District Council. and in some cases on much narrower footpaths. The Corporation contended that they should not be called upon to light more than the two standards which were now lighted. The matter was referred to the special committee which has the subject in hand. ENCROACHING HF.DC ES. On the motion of sir. F. Warburton. seconded by Mr. T. R. Westbrook, it was decided that the attention .of the surveyors to the County Council and the District Council he drawn to several hedges which encroached .on the footpath. THE FLAGGING OF NVIIITEFI CID-ROAD. Mr. Warburton presented a petition from ratepayers and residents of' Whitefleld-road, urging that the pro- posal to tlaz the footpaths et present unflagged was unnecessary, as flags made the tragic noisy, and were a source of danger to pedestrians in frosty weather, and would rob the road of that rural aspect which they believed there was a general desire should be retained. They thought the proposal involved a useless expendi- ture of money. Mr. T. R. Westbrook : I propose that we grant self government to Whitefleld-road. (Laughter.) Mr. Pearson moved that the District COURCii be asked to accept the recommendation. He thought it was a reasonable one. (Hear, hear.) Mr. T. R. Westbrook seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION</p>
        <p>-- The second round of the Wairington Works Competi- tion will be completed this week and the concluding stages entered upon on Monday. Interest continues to he maintained in the progress of the contest and the intermediate rounds have been well attended. Last Friday Robertson's defeated the Wire Rope, but were disqualified for playing ineligible men. The results of the other matches in the second round are :—Greenings I; points, Post Office 3 points: P. and K. 3, Whitecross 3 ; Fletchers 13, Richmond's 4. To-morrow morning matches will be decided between C.L.C. and Locos, and P. and K. v. \Vhitecross. The draw for the third round has resulted as follows :—Fletchers v. Wire Rope, Monday 10.15. Greenings v. Naylors or Vnitecross. Monday 11.15., C.L.C. v. Locos or Corpor- ation Gas, Tuesday P. and K., v. Whitecross or Cros- •.fields, Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
