      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>MR. BIRRELL'S DRASTIC REFORMS. Public Control of all Rate-Aided Schools. •</p>
        <p>TESTS ABOLISHED.</p>
        <p>The Religious instruction Difficulty.</p>
        <p>IMr. Birrell, on Monday, introduced the Education Bill in the House of Commons. It provides that on ! and after January 1, 1908, no school shall be recognised Ins a public elementary school unless it is controlled by the local educational authority, thus involving abolition of the existing dual system of provided and non- provided schools. Non-provided schools are to be taken over, under an arrangement with the owners, by the local education authority. The complete proper control thus established will carry with it the appointment by the local education authority of I the teachers, to whom no creed test can be applied. In all schools there is to be the same kind of religious instructiorn as is now given in provided schools. There will be no facilities for Idenominational instruction in provided schools, but in schools now classed as non-provided facilities for de- nominational teaching, of the kind stipulated for by the owners of the schools,can be given on two mornings a week, out of school hours,at the cost of the denomina- tion, and by teachers selected by it. In towns and populous places the local education authority may, on application, give extended facilities for religious instruction of some special character in an; trans- ferred Voluntary school, provided the authority is satisfied, after holding a local inquiry, that the parents of at least four-fifths of the children attending the school desire such facilities, and further provided that there is sufficient accommodation elsewhere for children whose parents do not desire special facilities. A farther annual grant of £1,000,000 is to be made from the exchequer in aid of the local education rate, representirpg a capital of between £23,000,000 and £30,000,0007 Wales is to have a National Council for educational purposes. In the debate which followed Mr. Birrell's speech, Sir W. Anson described the numerical proportion system associated with extended facilities for denominational teaching, as ridiculous. He claimed that facilities should also be given in the present Council schools. Many churchmen, he said, would feel it an affront to their consciences if children whose parents desired it were not allowed to have church teaching given in school hours by teachers whose convictions enabled them to give it whole-heartedly. Mr. Ramsay Macdonald, speaking for the Labour party, opposed religious instruc- tion, and said they would divide the House against it at a later stage. Dr. Macnamara appealed to the high denominalists not to coalesce with the ultea-secularists against Bible teaching. Mr. Wyndham described the measure as a new Act of Uniformity, and said that if the Opposition failed to secure what they considered justice they must seek to redress the injustice in the next Parliament. The Bill was read a first time amid cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LOCAL OPINIONS. Rev. John Wmge.</head>
        <p>LOCAL OPINIONS. Rev. John Wmge.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John Tonga said : I hail the Education Bill, so far as I can form an opinion of it from Mr. Birrell's speech, as an honest and earnest attempt to deal with the most complicated and perplexing questiont of the day. A measure that should please all partie4 and satisfy all their contradictory demands, is beyond the keenest wit of man to devise. This Bill secures the cardinal points for which the Country has given an unequivocal mandate. It is to be hoped that when the extremists of every opinion have duly blown their blasts of criticism and censure,they will feel able to meet at a Round Table Conference, 'wad come to a working agreement. The sooner, the better. The country is tired of this dispute, the main combatants ; in which are the clerics. The conflict of religious ! opinion is rapidiy becoming itself irreligious, both in spirit and in style.. One is disposed to adopt the utterance of our honest and sensible Premier, "Enough of this foolery ! Let us get to business." I cordially agree with the candid word of a vicar to me the other day : 'lf the clerics of all creeds would keep out of the way, the Education Question would soon be settled:"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Rev. J. F. Matthews.</head>
        <p>Rev. J. F. Matthews.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews writes : Mr. Birrell's speech in the House of Commons on Monday night when introducing the first reading of the new Education Bill revealed his great and unsus- pected powers as a constructive statesman. That he realized the gravity of his task is as evident to Us, as to those who heard him. The Bill is a bold measure, and has met with general and widespread approval. It is but the logical sequence of the Act of 1902. Long ago, Archbishop Temple told us that if the Church appealed for rate aid, she must be prepared to surrender her schools to popular control. This the Bill secures, with a restoration of some measure of autonomy to certain treas. The mention of over- burdened, and in any judgment, often incompetent existing local authorities, led me to hope that the ' gcl hoc' system with amplifications would be res- tored. I hope that during the Committee stage of the Bill this principle may be adopted and vastly extended. Until I see a copy of the Bill it will be impossible to pronounce upon its final value as a measure of real educational reform. Is it too much t hope, that the free Evening Continuation Schools w;11 be included, and the age limit of elementary school children raised to 13 years ? This would be an incal-' culable gain to the working classes. Everybody will credit Mr. Birrell with. the purest intentions in his ! capable handling of the religious difficulty. I join however with the Labour men in hoping that Bible teaching will be deleted from the Bill. It seems that the preponderating opinion of more moderate men is for its retention, while the fullest facilities are to be offered to the denominationalist in non-provided schools where parents wish it out of school hours. I don't like the four-fifths principle at all. Perhaps the yells of execration will come from those denominationalists who think the Romanist has been too highly favoured. The unification of our school system, the abolition of the dual basis, the freedom of teachers from religious tests need now only to be supplemented by the resuscitation of popularly elected bodies for the sole control and administration of education, for us to look upon our elementary schools as" public " in the best sense. At any rate, we have at last arrived within a fair view of a reasonable, just, equitable, if not a final settlement of a matter of first importance to our intellectual and commercial well-being.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Mr. §. W. Jeffery.</head>
        <p>Mr. §. W. Jeffery.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. W. Jeffery, ex-president of the local branch of the N.U.T., and secretary of the Warrington Certifi- cated Class Teachers' Association, writes : This expression of opinion it is desired will not be taken as representative of that of the body of local teachers, but as individual only. From that point of view I cannot but regard the Bill with satisfaction, and recognise it as an honest attempt to solve a national difficulty. The abolition of the dual system of provided and non-provided schools, c mpled with the propose'd abolition of religious tests for teachers, are two features of the Bill that will appeal to the main body of teachers. This question of religious teats has long been a sore one, and it is to be hoped that the religious partisans will recognise the honesty el! the Minister of Education in his attempt to deal fairly with art , admittedly difficult point. In the casa of extended facilitie3; I have no doubt, there will be consderable conflict of opinion even amongst, teachers themselves. But the satisfactory working of this will depend very largely upon the local education anthority. One had almost looked for an entire reversion to the Pd /cc principle. This however has not eventuated, although theremust naturally he 'modifications made in the Bill during its passage through the mse. Taken as a whole, the Bill will 1 think receive the commendations of the National Union of Teachers which meets in conference at Scarborough next week. Alderman Bennett, J.P.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett Writes : I have always been anxious, as Chairman of the Education Committee, to steer clear of contmversial topics, and I therefore rather hesitate to express an opinion on the Bill. I may say, however, that, like Mr. Bernard Shaw, I regtrd the Education Act of I 902 as "one of the greatest legislative advances made in our time," and have never been able to understand the violent hostility which it provoked.. I am sure that it was fair in intention, and my experience leads me to believe that it has been reasonably equitable in effect. But undoubtedly it failed to satisfy all parties. I believe that Mr. Birrell's Bill is an equally honest attempt to achieve the impossible. I can only hope that men of every faith will honestly mite to help him in his arduous task, hut f most confess that I am tot over-sanguine. The Bill doc,i not strike me as ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Examiner, Thursday, April 12, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Thursday, April 12, 1906.</p>
        <p>particularly logical, and it is essentially a com- promise. It seems to me that there are only three intelligible courses open. The first is purely secular teaching. I object to that because I believe, with Mr. Birrell, that, where there is no vision, the people perish. The second is plain Bible teaching. That is enough for me, and would, I think, do much to allay :the acrimony of religions bitterness as it prevails to da, . That the Jew and the Agnostic would have just as much right to object to pay rates for simple Bible teaching as the Nonconformist has to paying them to teach the creeds. And why should they be satisfied with a mere Conscience Clause any more than he? and nobody wants to sow the seed for a second crop of Passive Resisters. Moreover,the Roman Catholic and Anglicans are just as much dissatisfied with unseo- tarian teaching as the Nonconformist with the creeds. And have they not exactly the same rights to their opinion'? At any rate, they have expended many millions to promote them. It follows then, in my opinion, that the only permanent solution is to rent or buy the Voluntary schools and give to every class in the community the right to teach its own specific doctrines at convenient times in all the Elementary schools alike. The principle is absolutely fair to everybody, and the method of its apnlication is, after all, a matter of detail. I fail to see that, ' educationally, the new Bill is any great improvement on the last; but I welcome the concession of a further million from the National Exchequer and the exten- sion of the terms allowed for the repayment of our educational loans, as probably this mly encourage us to pat up decent and attractive buildings instead of the mere barns or "warehouses "with which we have been lately threatened.</p>
        <p>abolish what he calls religious tests for teachers is really impracticable and absurd. All recognized teachers are tested now. They are tested in grammar, in geography, in English,in mathematics, and without the qualifications thus obtained a teacher will stand a poor chance of a post. But Mr. Birrell evidently thinks that anybody, whether he knows his subject or not, will do to teach religion. Whether intentional or not, there is a real contempt for religion implied in this attitude. A striking feature of the speech was the xnconscions sectarianism by which it was pervaded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Canon Willis.</head>
        <p>Canon Willis.</p>
        <p>Tho Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) writes :—I have been asked by several papers to give my opinion of the new Education Bill. After necessarily brief but ' earnest thought I beg to say that I consider it uncalled for, unjust, injurious to true religion and the highest interests of the nation : —(1) Uncalled for. The great question at the General Election was Free Trade, not religious education. It was not on this question that the Liberal party obtained their great majority, but on Free Trade. The country has shown no dis- satisfaction with the Act of 1902.. Here it has worked admirably. Churchmen, Roman Catholics and Non- conformists have worked together in committees in perfect accord, and the teachers, scholars, parents and the public generally have been perfectly satisfied. Before an Act which has given such general satisfac- tion is abolished surely an appeal on this head ought to be made to the country. (2). Unjust. The Volun- tary schools have been erected by private con- tributions, in many cases under trusts expressly drawn for the purpose of giving to scholars religious 'instruction in the faith of that hotly to which they belong. Surely these trusts might not to be set aside simply because aid which has been rightly earned is given from public funds to which the sup- porters of these schools have conbributed their fair share. (3). Injurious to true religion and the highest interests of the State. This is the most important point of all. To those who think the Ten Commandments and the Sermon on the Mount were spoken to people generally without reference to ether divine requirements the neW Education Bill with its miserable provision for religions instruction may suffice, but, and to those who know that the words were uttered in view of God's intention to bring all mankind into His Church and there through life in infancy, childhood, manhood, and womanhood give them help and strength through appointed means of grace to keep these Commandments, such a Bill can only bs viewed with pain and shame as opposed to the will of God, hostile to the Church of Jesus ,Christ, unworthy of a Christian nation, and likely in I the end to banish and destroy those very virtues by which Mr. Birrell rightly sets such store.</p>
        <p>Canon Morley Stevenson. . Interviewed by our representative on Tuesday morn- mg, Canon Morley Stevenson, who spoke with some reservation, not having seen a copy of the actual bill, and having to judge from the summaries published in the daily papers, said I confess that to me the Bill is a very great disappointment. I think Mr. Birrell had a great chance of effecting a permanent settlement of a very difficult question and that he has thrown the chance away. We have been encouraged on all hands to look for a Bill which was going to satisfy the demands of all reasonable people and we are greatly disappointed to recive a Bill which certainly will not satisfy a very great number of people whoml hope are not altogether unreasonable. The Bill endows out of the rates a particular kind of religious instruction which is congenial to a certain number of people but which to a very large number of people is altogether , inadequate and 'unsatisfactory. The conse-mence is ' that a large number of us will have to be rated to support religious teaching which we do not believe in and denied the opportunity of having religious teach ing which we do believe in. That is to say that one ratepayer is to have what he wants and his next-door neighbour who pays the same rates is to have nothing. With regard to facilities for religious teach- ing I am not quite clear as to the meaning of the Bill, but as far as I can see it would practically work out that the Jews and the Roman Catholics would have some kind of facilities which would be denied to other people because Jews and Roman Catholics will be able to have schools to which their children alone would go. From the nature of the case—thousands of Church of England schools being the only ones in certain districts —our schools can- not- be filled entirely by Church of England chil- dren, and because the Church of England has built schools for her own people and has gladly welcomed , children from other people, site is to be penalised awl refutted a privilege which is to be granted to other denominations. That !teems to me to be very unjust. With regard to our being allowed to give religions instruction twice a week it is not clear whether that is to. be within or without school hours. In any case I don't think it is worth much. If it is without school hours we can do that without any Bill to give us leave, as the buildings are ours. If within it will mean a very difficult matter to get spacial teachers; make special arrangements, and upset the work of the school by various denominations sending representatives to give teaching and I think this arrangement would work very badly. One serious blot on the Bill it seems to me, is that the children who are exempted under the conscience clause.are in Future not required to attend school. That will mean that parents who can employ their children to earn a few pence in any manner, will withdraw those chil- dren under the conscience clause and instead of them receiving the teaching which they otherwise would receive they will be running about the streets. That seems to me a very unnecessary thing. There can he no difficulty really in a child being withdrawn from religious teaching and having other teaching instead given, to it within tile sehaol walls. It is obvious, if we are going to make possible a choice between attendance at Retool at 9 o'clock and 9.15, that a large number of children will prefer 9.45 for ; reasons other than religions. With reference to the abolition of tests for teachers if the new kind of religious teaching is to be enforced everywhere it means nothing to us. l7nder the present system of course religious tests are of great importance. On the whole instead of making for peace and settling the question definitely once and for all, the Bill will stir up such feeling throughout the whole country that we shall probably have a very stormy time indeed and w hat the end will be I cannot say. But it may be another instance of the fatal Govern- ment policy which we have had in other instances, the same,bluster and climb down as over South Africa and Chinese Labour. Rev. E. C. E. Carleton.</p>
        <p>i The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton said : The speech i in accordance with Mr. Birrell's past utterances. The measure is a distinctly sectarian one as outlined in the speech. The main features of the Bill appear to be an approlpriation of Church and Roman Catholic property at a nominal compensation, cotnbined with an endowment of Undenorninationalism (that is Free Chttrchism, I suppose) out of the rates. One serious blot or rather blunder of Mr. Eirrell arises from the endeavour to differentiate in favour of a syllabus of nligions instruction as against religious creeds, Mr. Birrell being evidently oblivious of the fact that the Creed itself is merely a syllabus or an outline of religious teaching. By this method of differentia- tion, however, he .is able to faNtur any and every variety of creed or syllabus that ,may emanate front the public representative bodies. that will control edncation, while the ancient Creed of Christendom_ the most unsectarian formulary in the world—is placed under a ban, thus endowing modern Nonconformity in all its varied phases. Another great blot is that the measure is not consistent with the great principle of religious toleration which began in 11180 and which, at the earnest desire of Nonconformists, has been carried to its utmost extent. The principle that this legislation expressed ,was the free exercise of religion to every individual and the restriction that is now being sought to be placed upon it as regards education is distinctly retrogressive from the point of view of religious liberty. The proposal to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Rev. Neville Wilson.</head>
        <p>Rev. Neville Wilson.</p>
        <p>The Rev Neville Wilson, speaking from the Roman Catholic point of view, said : I consider the Bill, as I Understand it from Mr. Birrell's speech, to be an outrage which disregards logic and violates justice. In establishing School-Board religion —E; iise the term merely for convenience—in all schools 1 he admits he is illogical and I have no hesitation in saying that be is unjust. This religious teaching is as abhorrent to Roman I Catholics as any tenet of our faith can possibly be to anyone else. To have to pay for it in rates or taxes was unjust even when we were allowed to teach our I own religion in our own schools and at our own , expense, but to have to do so when this liberty is I denied us is intolerable. The " facilities " Ur. Birrell makes such play with are a mockery. The Catholic , character of our schools is to be destroyed by statute ' and it is an insult to our intelligence to imagine that we shall be soothed by his recommending these schools to the tender mercies of the local authorities wh) are often hostile now and who will be ten-fold more so then. The Catholic character of our schools being destroyed, there would be no inducement for the children to attend their old schools which would be quickly filled by non-Catholics, and the Catholic children would be scattered through- out the schools of the district, a few in each of all ages and all standards, making Mr. Birrell's promised facilities absolutely useless. Ur. Birrell mentions the splendid It maan Catholic training college at Liverpool which attracts visitors from all parts of Europe. What is he going to do with these and other Roman Catholic training colleges ? If this measure is forced upon us, not the peace which Mr. Birrell says he desires but strife more bitter than any which has hitherto arisen on this question will be the result. I cannot think ' said the Rev. N. Wilson in conclusion 'that Catholics will submit to pay for School-Board religion or to send their children to schools whose Catholic character has been destroyed.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Mr. A. J. Jolley.</head>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley, asked to give an opinion on the general educational side of the Bill, writes :—" The new Bit is in most respects a *natural development from the Act. of 1902 ; it gives fuller powers to local authorities and lays neon them the responsibility for the upkeep of the buildings of such of the Volun- tm-y schools as they, by agreement with the trustees, utilise. it still further reduces Abe distinction between provided and non-provided or Voluntary schools. It extends to all teachers the immunity from religious tests partially granted by the 190.2 Act. In view of the requirements of the Board of Education, whose determination to secure for all school children a thoroughly healthy and .satisfactory environment every sane citizen approves, involving heavy capital outlay in the erection of new buildings, the proposal of the Bill to extend the period of repayment of loans for such purposes is welcome and justifiable. Whether the additional grant of a million will raore than cover the increased outlay in respect of the upkeep of transferred school premises, particularly in places like Warrington, where, until recently, almost the whole of the school accommodation was volun- tarily provided, is very doubtful. The provisions of the Bill in respect of religious instruction appear on the whole to be less equitable than those of the. Act of 1902, less considerate of the rights of minorities, likely to arouse bitter feelings and to bring nearer the complete secularisation of elementary education an eventuality which no earnest Christian or social reformer can regard as other than a national calamity, and of the danger of which Mr. Birrell himself seems uneasily conscious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS AND MR. GROSFIELD M.P. An Explanatioa Wanted from the</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS AND MR. GROSFIELD M.P. An Explanatioa Wanted from the</p>
        <p>Member.</p>
        <p>At the close'of the ocdinary business of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening reference was made to the absence from Parliament of the Borough member when the Trades Disputes Bill was introduced. Mr. Howmsrox said ho thought that Council as a trade unionist body ought to have Some explanation from the Borough Member as to why he was not present to carry out the pledges be made to the electors of the borough in the recent division on the Trades Disputes Bill. Mr. EDGE said it was rather curious that Mr. Gros- field should be absent, as the Tradas Disputes question was one of his great points. Mr. IiOWLISTON moved that the Secretary should write and ask Mr. Crosflell for an explanation of his absence. Mr. POD MORE seconded. He thought it was a very important matter for the Council to take in hand, see- in g that they were ailhody of undoubtedly Mr. Crosfield played on the Trades Disputes Bill in every speech he made which he (Mr. Podrnore) ' read. No doubt he would get a great deal of support because of that measure and he (Mr.Podmore) presumed at the time it was a trade union Trades Disputes Bill that Mr. Crosfield was speaking about. Of course, he might have been wrong—A,.. Crosfield might have mental reservations. That Council as a representative body of organized labour ought to demand an explana- tion from Mr. Crosfield. Of course, they must not pre- judge him. lie might have a very good explanation or he might have none. Councillor &amp;refer said one of the members of the Council qsked him to send a letter or telegram to Mr.( Crosfield asking him to carry out his promise. tfo felt, however, that after the definite promises Mr. Crosfield had given to the trade unionists of the town there was no reason to spend money to ask him to fulfil them. It was fortunate he did not telegraph as Mr. Crostield would evidently not have been in the House of Commons to receive the message. This question appealed to trade unionists more than any other—it was a vital question. It meant either the life or death of trade unionism. Whether Mr. Crosfield knew that the Liberals were not going to oppose Mr. Hudson's Bill he could not say. If he did know th it, he perhaps felt safe in leaving the House. judging from the position as laid before the House on Wednes- day by the Attorney-General in regard to the Govern- ment's Bill it seemed the members would not know. If there had not been a strong Labour group in the House Labour men would have been placed in a worse position by the Government Bill than they were by the Judge's decision in the Taff Vale case. rt was only through the presence of the Labour group that the Government did not raise opposition to Mr. Hudson's Bill, Now 'was the time for Labour to agitate. They had a Government which might be more easily squeezed than any Government he could remember. Mr. HroGixeorrost asked the secretary how many trade unionists were represented by that Council. Councillor Srcn'r said 1,600 members were paid for, but one or two societies did not pay for the full com- plement of members. The Cif AIRMAN said that on several occasions letters were written to the late member and a great deal of sarcasm was used at times about the way in which he treated them. Perhaps it might be some consolation to members who belonged to the party if they got to know the reason why the present member was absent from the House. Mr. EDWARD PLINSTON said he was a little taken aback on hearing that Mr. Crosfield did not vote on the question. He was, however' assured that the member ' had asked permission for a fortnight's or three weeks, holiday and it was granted to him. Councilor Srorr raid the ?organization from which Mr. Crosfield asked permission to take a holiday was an organization that did not pay any subscriptions—or did not up to quite recently. He referred to the Progressive Labour League. Mr. Crosfield asked permission for a holiday for three weeks and said he honed they would not prevent hint from taking it. The Progressive Labour Latvia was not going to speak for theta (the Trades Council). The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
