      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Lomas, the Salford captain and ex-Bramley half- back, daring the five seasons he has played for the Lancashire club, has scored 714 points in club matches alone, and in addition some 50 points in county, benefit, and international matches. In purely club matches his record up-to-date is as follows :—Season 1901-2 53 goals 22 tries, or 172 points; 1902-3, 20 goals 18 tries, or 91 points ; 1903-4, 56 goals, 25 tries, or 187 points ; 1904-5, 30 goals 20 tries, or 132 points ; 1905-6, 33 goals 21 tries, or 129 points, making a grand total of 714. This heats all records in Lancashire football. Lomas is only 26, and as he is playing as well as ever, there is every probability of his reaching four figures.</p>
        <p>- The match at Warrington last Saturday fulfilled all expectations. A finer display of the Rugby game has rarely been seen on the Wilderspool ground, and as Wariington finally won by S points to 6 points, the crowd was very good•humoured at the close. It will be remembered that Hull last year gave Warrington their best game at home, and the scoring was of .a very free character, while the margin at the close in favour of Warrington was just the same. On Satur- day there was a try less obtained by each team, but as each defence was penetrated twice there was plenty of excitement. Warrington just won by the only goal of the match, and they deserved success' not only from the general run of the play, but because the goal was the outcome' of a piece of foul play by which the home custodian was" knocked out." At one period War- rington were 8 points to the good, but Hull never relaxed their efforts, and a little luck favouring them in the closing stages, they put on a couple of tries which gave Warrington a fright.</p>
        <p>Play right through was exceedingly fast and some of the individual and combined runs by both sets of bAcks were a treat to witness. The Warrington men showed better combination than in any match this year and ;both tries were the outcome of clever Pao. Dlitc:h of the credit is due to Hockenhall •ssm and Loees, whose methoda in getting the ball away were a big advancd on 'anything they have done together before. Fish was a bit over-played by Parry whose superior ezmdition and speed told a tale on several occasions. In fact the Warrington crack would have had a couple of tries if he had been able to move at his best pace. Avoirdupois proved a serious hindrance, however. The best man on the field was Taylor, the Hull custodian, who displayed positively brilliant form and more than once turned defence into attack. He was far ahead of Knight, who played below form. The two Hull halves, Burchell and Goddard, and Cook at-centre, also showed much cleverness, but they were well covered by the home centres. Forward, there was only one team in it, and the Warrington front rankers excelled themselves in getting possession and heeling out smartly. The beague table up to date is as follows : For. Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. P. D. T. P. G. T. P. centage Hunslet 27 22 5 044G1 GI 305 14 27 109 81'48 Leigh Oldham 35 25 8 25251 99 399 24 21 111 7428 ' Leeds Keighley 25 17 7 13537 57 245 21 30 132 7000 Wigan 30 20 9 14147 84 346 20 36 148 6833 Halifax 33 19 7 74539 53 237 32 41 187 68•18' IC.R 33 21 9 34535 54 232 37 33 173 6818 Hud'flold 26 15 9 23228 50 206 26 33 151 6153 Bradford 3l IS 11 23846 79 329 no 38 174 6129 Runcorn Bro'ton R 2917 11 13540 83 329 32 39 181 6034' St. Helens 26 15 10 13131 53 227 26 32 148 59.61 Swinton 29 15 11 33325 42 176 22 32 140 5689 Warrington 35 17 16 23640 50 245 27 42 180 51'42 Salford 29 13 13 32934 57 239 36 33 186 50.00 • Wak'ficild T 3012 14 42837 36 182 32 49 211 16.60 Hull 32 14 17 12941 GO 202 34 46 203 4531 Pontefract 23 913 11927 33 153 27 38 108 4P30 York 30 10 18 22233 30 156 34 46 226 36•66 Dewsbury 32 10 20 22221 29 129 38 53 235 34.37! Batley 3l 818 521 36 27 153 40 43 209 33.37 Barrow 27 827 21815 31 123 4'3 72 302 3333 Widnes 24 715 21611 26 100 23 51 205 33'33 Bromley , Norrnanton 21 415 210 711 47 29 50 208 23110 CaStleford lB 313 2 812 7 45 45 68 294 22'22 • RochdaleH 29. 320 61212 26 102 33 70 276 2068. Brighouseß 21 319 2 915 18 84 39 77 309 1666 !Morecambe 22 217 3 712 23 93 32 64 256 1590 Millom l9 215 2 611 13 67 40 77 323 15.78 The points are two for a win and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>The " A" team unfortunately failed to get the better of Oldalim at Watersheddings on Saturday, even though they had such a strong team. They found the Oldham team in fine form, first teamers like Fletcher, White, Civil, .an d Spottistrood proving rather too good for Warrington, who lost by 9 points to 5 points. This .ties them to win or draw their remaining Competition match with St. Helens at War- rington on Monday. They should have no olifficuly in getting two points from the match seeing that they won at St. Helens, but nothing will be left to chance as this is the deciding encounter.</p>
        <p>On Good Friday morning at 10.30, the " A " team is at home to Runcorn " A " in a " friendly." Tiro</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovallst."</p>
        <p>To-morrow (Friday) Warrington play their annual Good Friday match with Halifax and on present form they should secure another two points. On Monday they go to St. Helens and ought to be successful there, though the Sainth are by no means an easy team to beat on their own ground.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team against Halifax will be the same as against Hull, except that Berry takes the place of Knight.</p>
        <p>Saturday, of course, sees the first Northern Union Cup semi-final tie at Warrington Considerable local interest in the event is being manifested notwithstand- ing the fact that the contesting teams—Salford and Keighley—are foreign to the district. Probabilities point to a , Salford success, though the team which could beat Hull at Hull must not be dispised. Their season's rec )rd show Keighley to be a much improved side, and they ought to give the Reds a hard game. With Lomas back, the Salford men should be rather the more classy team, however, and I fully expect them to have a comfortable margin at the close of hostilities. This is Salford's sixth appearance in the , semi-finals, so that they should be free from all stage fright. Strange to say, however, with all their chances they have never won the Cup, and I think I the odds in the final are upon Bradford this year.</p>
        <p>The appointed officials are :—Referee, Mr. W. Robinson (Manningham) ; touch judges, Messrs. W. Jones (Runcorn) and C. Platt (Swinton). Warrington members will have the privilege of pay,. ing for the semi-final, but the latest information is that they can go either on the Is. or 6d. side at those prices and have sitting accommodation found for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>List of Fixtures.</p>
        <p>An attractive list of fixtures has been arranged by the Warrington Cricket Club, who open their season on Saturday. They have again entered the South-West Lancashire League Competition and are hoping to have a much more successful season than last year. The old and prominent members of the club are still associated with the team and the introduction of now blood, which it is hoped will result from the reduction in the entrance fee, ought to strengthen the combination. The list of fixtures is as follows, the teams marked with an asterisk being League engagements.</p>
