      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>CHARGES AGAINST COUNCILLORS. One To Be Prosecuted.</head>
        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST COUNCILLORS. One To Be Prosecuted.</p>
        <p>NO CASE AGAINST COUNCILLOR PErABERTON.</p>
        <p>-- It wilLie within the recollection of our readers thaticharges under the Corrupt Practices Act have been preferred against two members of the Warring- ton Town Council, and that as a result of those charges and the investigations of a special Committee of the Council, the Town Clerk drew up cases which were submitted to the Attorney-General. It will also be remembered that at a public Council meeting Councillor Pemberton openly identified himself as one of the members against whom a criminal charge , had been made. The name of the other Councillor was never pub'icly divulged, though we believe that it is an open secret. Possibly owing to the change of Government, there has been a con- siderable delay in the receipt of a report from the I Attorney-General, and that document only reached' Warrington this week.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the General Purposes Corn mittee was called and held at 5 o'clock on Wednes- day, when the report was presented to the members of the Council. We understand that the Attorney- General says there is no case against Councillor Pemberton, but that a prosecution against the other Councillor must be instituted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>WINDOW BOXES. A window-box can be easily made by any handy man out of a planed deal board, from seven inches by one • inch, the width to be in accordance with the window- sill, a suitable width being six inches. Make the back a couple of inches deeper than the front, this will pre- vent the window being splashed from the soil in rainy weather. The bottom should have several holes bored in it, so-as to allow the surplus water to escape. A . layer of about one inch of broken bricks or cinders- I should then be placed in the bottom, and them some cocoa-nut fibre, rotten manure or leaf mould over this, and fill up to within an inch of the top with good garden soil, to which has been added a little manure,and if the soil be heavy a small quantity of sand., To make the box secure, nail, or screw wedge-shaped pieces of wood on the bottom, so that the box may stand level, and fasten at the sides to the wall. A piece of moulding about one inch to one and a half inches deep' placed along the front, will improve the appearance. The box should then be painted a dull green or brown. Now as to the seeds t 6 sow and plants to grow. For cheapness sow seeds such as canary creeper, candytuft, rmignonette, nasturtium (dwarf or tall), silene, virginia stock, all of which can be obtained in penny packets. A very small quantity will suffice and might be varied, sowing fresh supplies as one or other of them has got past its best. A good effect can be obtained by planting sweet peas at the ends of the box, training them nettle sides of the window, and tilling the middle of the box with dwarf nasturtium, probably the best for this purpose being the scarlet king. Quite a 1 novelty would be to train a scarlet runner up the side of the window. By putting in a fresh bean or two at intervals of two or three weeks, a ~cood crop of scarlet flowers can be obtained, and probaldy a small crop of beans. The effect is best finished off by plenty of 1 scarlet geranium and marguerite daisies in the body of the box, if cost is not an essential element. A very pretty " all-yellow " box is obtained by a background of golden calceolaria.s, baying in front of them dwarf French or African marigolds of orange colour, and allowing canary creeper to hang down over i the front. Another good effect s obtained by filling the middle of the box with marguerite daisies, and ivy-- leaved geraniums overhanging the front. These need comparatively little attention. PLANTS FOR SUNLESS SITUATIONS. I have been asked, during the last week, to give 5 list of plants which can be grown where there is little or no sun. Pansies, both bedding, show, and fancY kinds, violas crocusses, tulips, daffodils, hyacinthS. , snowdrops, &amp;C., would do admirably in such places, also lilies, such as the orange and tiger. Ferns, periwinklea. gardener's garter grass, mixed with the bulbs, would make a nice variety. It is now too late to put in bulbs, but the lilies and other plants might be obtained at once, and the bulbs planted in the autumn. Prim- roses and polyanthuses would be at home there, Ills(' Solomon's seal, saxifrage, forget-me-not and naimulus. Very few bedding plants would be successful in this situation. The best climbers for such an aspect are the fine-leaved Virginian creeper (ampelopsis veitchi) and the strong-growing ivies. J. F. A public meeting to organise work in floe-soy Ward will be held in Hoyle-street Mission Room on Friday, April 20.</p>
