        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT. Political Clubs for Women.—The first politic al club for women is shortly to be opened in London under the title of the "Ladies Imperial." Women's clubs are well on the way to becoming as numerous, and as diversified 'in their interests and ambitions as the older institutions for men, but this will be the first political women's club, and will be devoted to the interests of the Con- servative party. The intention of the founders is to obtain for theclnb a recognised place in the political world, corresponding to that of the Carlton Club. and, if they are successful, in this aim, there is no doubt that it will shortly be followed on the other side by a "„„eulan's Reform Club, or a Ladies National Liberal. tulc,e Prettiest Girls.—An American paper, d:scussing he beauty and grace of English girlhood, is not far cant •In • ltsconclusion that the prettiest eirls are to be found ~.11 the London shops. Neither the stage, or the dr twieg 'eon's of May fair, oiild eclipse the show rooms of the Peat drapery houses, whose attendants are hardly with- °et excention. perfectly featured and developed, 1105sessing beautiful complexions, and an easy carriage acd charm of expression. In this respect" shopland " would certainly compare favourably with the crowd seen at the private view of the Academy, on the lawn at Ascot or at the Oxford and Cambridge cricket-match, which rank as the leading Society beauty shows of the season. Girl Golf Caddies.—No one familiar with the game doubt that the moral atmosphere of the golf-links not all that could be desired, but the argument that "ecause boy caddies are likely to grow up into wastrels, therefore girls should take their place,seems particularly Malapropos—The grown-up Caddy one usually finds on the courses in Scotland involve an expense of about 4e• or ss. a day, and to most people eolf is a sufficiently cxnensive amusement with half, or less than half that charge, with which the boy caddie is content. This is shy they are patronized by most of the twelve hundred zolf clubs in the Kingdom, and there is no doubt that the boy, loafing about, waiting for employment, which "'nes to him on some days and not on others is apt to !all into idleness and mischief. It is just the time of 'l.re for him when discipline is most needed, Mit it is difficult to see what would be gained by substituting guns; girls for boys. There would be the same objec- ii,'°", and some would have greater force, for even if had the strength to tramp miles, carrying a heavy 1;4 of clubs, they would be liable to fall into similar habits and certainly they would not be elevated by ona,W, ords of wrath and profanity, which many men use `es links, although they wonld not dream of resort- -0 such laneuage at any other time. Chennins Reforms.—" Shopping, like everything ge, is undergoing changes, and amongst retail estab- . ishments there is a tendency to eliminate, as far as bessible, the old eustoms and associations of the shop. jtie splendid saloons of the great London drapery , houses fitted with living models dressed in the moat I ivtne creations" for thelawn at Ascot, or for Lords, or the private view of the academy. suggest rather the drawing-room of a great Society hostess, than the atmosphere of the shop, and similar changes are taking placetn other trades. One instance is afforded by the reform of the butcher's shop, which, instead of its suggestions of sawdust, grease, damaged frocks, and unpleasant" emelliness," is now appearing in London With all the meat stored show-cases, hygienically venti- lated, the shop-fittings and premises being otherwise kept spotlessly clean from contact with the meat. There is nothing more objectionable, in going to such a shop, than to one's milliner, or dressmaker, and, the depriving of the butcher's shop of its unpleasantness, is only one Of msnv reforms now adonted by shopkeepers. " Slave" Bangles.—Quite every other girl or woman tune meets is wearing the heavy gold or " roiled " gold slave " 'bangles, which if not very ornamental, and lather savouripg of the barbaric, are at least useful in keeping up the very fashionable long gloves. Newer and prettier however, are the " slave " bangles carried out in mother-of-pearl, which are appearing in several colours. There is the modish green known as absinthe trettv pearl grey, dove, and rose tints, besides number- -1 ss shades in blue. The woman with an elastic dress allowance should see to it that her bangles match her ecstume, when the effect will be particularly good. ,peacock-Feather Mounts.—There was a time when no 'dinner would have been found ready to exploit the Peacock's feathers, but superstition is out of fashion w-a-days. and peacock feather mounts are in great yeluand. These are generally of a somewhat exagger- ated height, and after a tussle with the wind they take lint() themselves a wild appearance that is not altogether ?ecornine. Moreover, they are being dyed the most ,thlpossible shades, a procedure which adds considerably 10 the weirdness of the mode. , New Notions,—in London, the short skirt is correct before luncheon, but not afterwards, and tea-gowns are larely donned for tea. The smart" woman instead • changes her" visiting " toilette for some pretty light frock which does duty until the dinner hour approaches. Another innovation is the hat worn by a hostess at an afternoon party. ' Hitherto it has not been considered correct for a woman to wear a hat or teems in her own house, Mit we have changed these notions, and at after- noon crushes it is often very difficult unless of course l‘he happens to be ft familiar friend to distinguish one's hostess. A bee-line for the woman without a hat, was the only thing visitors had to worry about when enter-</p>
        <p>0 ,- • ____.,.... , - DRESS—F.h.SI ZION—HO/IC. • _. ... AN THE Gm. WITII A VOICE. Mme. Emma Eames. the famous singer. hits • been giving some scrind advice to ".the girl with a voice." Mine. E tiers wisely counsels girls not to rush 07. abroad before finding out whether or not they rrisQess a voice worthy of high development. " Never mind, what your friends say,' advises the. great singer. "It, is not that they mean lo Patter. They simply do not know. Only good instructo7s are capable of making the decision when finding and ' placing ' your vocal organ. If after faithful trial you find yourself to be eligible, by all means go abroad, in order to learn the languages on their native ground, so to speak. Also to profit by the advantages of a rriesieal atmosphere singu- larly rich and ripe from many years of estab- lished and universal musicl culture—a culture which in Germany has become really popular, the privilege of the humblest peasant as well as of the highest prince. And, above all these things. become your own .possessor. Remember that yourself must be to you your truest guide, for it is the will-power and the temperament-power of your stock-in-trade. Make your mind master of the situation before you give rein to your imagination. In other words, learn your psychic and musical technique, keep your heart single, and AVORK ! "</p>
        <p>HOW LIQUID AIR IS MADE. Air is compressed to 1,200 to 1,500 pounds pet square inch, passed into receptacles, where it is feeed from moistus and other impurities, then into expansion chaMbers, and through long coils of pipe. It becomes intensely cold, reaching finally 3124eg. below zero, at which point it be- comes liquid. The liquid is drawn off in insu- lated vessels, and keeps for days gradually lessen. lug until it is all evaporated..</p>
