        <p>selected' team being : Knight, full back ; Jordan, Cartwright, Brooks and Kenyon, threequarters ; Lloyd and Davies, half-backs ' • Forster, Morrison , Ashton, Hewitt, and Bradshaw forwards. Lambert is resting but will probably be on the team on Monday. On Saturday, the 28th inst., the postponed match with Hunslet will take place at Warrington and con- clude the League fixtures for both teams. In all probability this will be the deciding match for the League Championship as Leigh and Hunslet are still flunking a neck-and-neck race. cycling. Ideal weather favoured the Cycling Club's run to Bollington on Saturday. Twenty-two members turned out and on arrival at the Stamford Arms a bowling main was arranged. After saVeral good rounds the winners were Messrs. G. Bowman and W. Taylor. An excellent tea was served and was thoroughly enjoyed. Warrington was reached about ten after a delightful I moonlight ride and the fixture was successful in every , respect. . I The prospects for Easter are most encouraging and , given a continuance of the present genial conditions there should be many enjoyable tours this week-end. , A contingent of the 'Warrington Club intend visiting the Ellesmere district, returning vitt Llangollen. This is a favourite outing and a big muster is expected. Ala TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL Report on Local Educational Work. The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held at Bold-street Wesleyan School on Tuesday evening under the presidency of Mr. Plumb. There was a fair attendance including the secretary (Councillor Stott). " RIGHT TO WORK COMMITTEE. The SECRETARY reported that a conference of trade unionists had been held and was unanimously in favour of the formation of a local "right to work "committee. It with felt that such a committee would be able to do a great amount of good, especially if it could work in A Keighley correspondent sends the following : connection with the National Committee. The object " Keighley 