presentative members were responsible for extravagant expenditure—if there had been any during the 'year—became they had a majority on the Sites sad Buildings Committee, and also on the General Com- mittee. They were told that schools could be erectad at a cost of £lO 10s. per head. If they could be built for that sum in accordance with the requirements of the, Board of Education, let the Committee have them done at that prioe,but he had not yet had a satisfactory explanation as to why the sub-committee should have accepted plans at £l3 17s: per head if the work could be done for RIO. The Mayor had said that the co-opted members were banded together to support extravagant expenditure. His Worship had made statements before which were not absolute facts, and the present statement was without foundation. During his (Mr. Plinston's) term of office he had differed with the co-opted members on many occasions, and had moved amendments against extravagance in matters of salary. The co-opted mem- ,bers had to do the work while Town Council representa- tives put in an appearance for a few minutes and then cleared off to other work in connection with the town. He was of opinion that value for money was being obtained in Warrington in education. The increased education rate was mostly due to the provision of further accommodation and the increase of salaries in accordance with the scale and it was entirely due to the representative members from the Town Council whe had approved it. He did not think the Committee had acted wisely in employing only fully certificated teachers. Mr. Plinston also said that often there were too many subjects on the school curriculum and the result was that the "cramming " process was brought to bear. He deprecated a suggestion that attendance at evening schools should be made compulsory. With regard to the new Education Bill he regretted that it would not provide for the direct election of the education authority by the ratepayers. He believed, however, that an endeavour had been made to face the religious difficulty in a satis- factory manner. He was not in favour of abolishing religion entirely from the schools but lie said that dis- tinctive dogmas should not be taught during school hours. He believed that in the turmoil that might take place a brighter era would be evolved and they would find that the majority of the people of the country looked upon elementary education rather than religious dogmas taught in the schools as the basis of uplifting the children. (Applause.) Councillor S rorr, lin moving a vote of thanks to Mr. Plinstou, said he considered that during the last three or four months the co-opted members of the Education Committee had been grossly insulted by members of the Town Council who had grossly neglected their duty. It was said that the co-opted members did not represent anyone and it surprised him that those who had the most criticism to offer were much less representative than those whom they criticised. He hoped the time would come when the question of degling with educa- tion would be taken entirely out of the hands of the Town Council. It was impossible for Councillors to do the work in connection with a municipal body einci- ' ently and to carry on the educational work at the same time. They should have a specially elected body for the purpose. Mr. Gas:ALM( seconded and the vote was accorded. Mr. PLINSTON in responding said he should , support the sending back of the names of Mr. Jolley and Mr. May to therown Council as members of the committee for the simple reason that the scheme stated that the Council should appoint at least one lady. Therefore the Council should do its work with less grumbling.</p>
